      <main id="content"><article class="companion-reader"><nav class="reader-breadcrumb"><a href="../../samplewiki.html">SampleWiki</a> / <a href="index.html">Companion frontiers</a> / <a href="proof-technology.html">Reusable proof technology</a> / <a href="proof-delta.html">What changed in the proof?</a></nav><header><p class="companion-status">Planned local formalization</p><h1>Smoothed Picard HMC and the proxy warm start</h1><p>Producer: low-accuracy transport control, then an implementable proxy warm start.</p></header><a class="ulg-context" href="../../../lean-foundations.html?focus=case:ASTIS-SW-SPHMC-2026"><span>Formal topology</span><strong>Open this result&#x27;s proof branch</strong><b>↗</b></a><section><h2>Source setting and conventions</h2><div class="formula sw-casebook-formula">\[d\ge1,\quad V\in C^2(\mathbb R^d),\quad 0&lt;\alpha I\preceq\nabla^2V(x)\preceq\beta I,\quad \kappa=\beta/\alpha,\quad \pi(dx)=Z^{-1}e^{-V(x)}dx\]</div><ul><li>The Hessian inequalities hold at every point; alpha and beta are positive real numbers. Separate Hessian-to-chord and Gibbs-integrability proofs support the compiled normalized PBPS augmentation certificate: positive Gibbs integral, probability and exact product-volume joint density. Consult linked cells for independent-review and admission states. Conditional kernels, the sampling process and its guarantees remain separate.</li><li>TV(P,Q) means sup over measurable events of |P(A)-Q(A)|. For probability laws with densities it is half their L1 distance.</li><li>R_2(P||Q)=log integral (dP/dQ)^2 dQ when P is absolutely continuous with respect to Q, and infinity otherwise. The PBPS paper writes D_2 and calls the target mu; here the common target is pi.</li><li>Expected oracle queries are not worst-case runtime. Reference-point preparation, proximal access and gradient-only reductions must be charged under the particular theorem&#x27;s model.</li></ul></section><section class="companion-theorem" id="sphmc-w2" data-proof-status="planned">
<p class="companion-status">RED · source theorem known · local proof open</p>
<h2>Low-accuracy W2 guarantee</h2>
<p class="companion-source">Source: <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1">2609.06906v1</a>. SPHMC Theorem 1.1; Algorithm 3.2; Section 4.5; Appendix D</p>
<p><a class="button primary" href="../../../live/index.html?workspace=ASTIS-SW-SPHMC-2026&theorem=sphmc-w2">Open this theorem in the interactive research workspace</a></p>
<h3>Statement</h3><p>Given a reference point with small gradient, the source algorithm outputs a probability law satisfying the displayed normalized Wasserstein error. This theorem counts gradient and proximal queries; gradient-only implementation is a separate reduction.</p>
<ul><li>The common C2 strong-convexity/smoothness setting.</li><li>0 &lt; epsilon &lt;= 1; a supplied x_ref satisfies ||grad V(x_ref)|| &lt;= sqrt(alpha d).</li><li>Use the source algorithm and its parameter choices, not an arbitrary Picard or HMC discretization.</li></ul><div class="formula sw-casebook-formula">\[\sqrt\alpha W_2(\widehat\pi,\pi)\le\varepsilon,\qquad \mathbb E Q_{\nabla,\mathrm{prox}}=O\!\left[\left(\kappa^2+\kappa^{7/6}d^{1/6}\varepsilon^{-1/3}\right)\log^4\frac{e\kappa d}{\varepsilon}\right]\]</div>
<details class="companion-lean"><summary>Lean statement — not formalized yet</summary><p>A future declaration must bind the probability laws, normalization, regularity, source algorithm and oracle model explicitly. The formula is a source theorem contract, not Lean code.</p><p>No corresponding ASTIS declaration is asserted. Search locations below are candidates, not established dependencies or copied library proofs.</p></details>
<h3>Proof architecture and calculations</h3><p>This is a source-linked proof route, not a complete reconstruction of all cited lemmas. The dependencies below remain separate formalization tasks.</p>
<ol class="companion-steps"><li id="sphmc-w2-step-01"><h4>Smooth the density</h4><div class="formula sw-casebook-formula">\[e^{-V_\eta}=e^{-V}*\mathcal N(0,\eta I),\qquad \nabla V_\eta(y)=\mathbb E[\nabla V(X)\mid X+\sqrt\eta G=y]\]</div><p>This is not the Gaussian average of V. Differentiation under the integral and a specified conditional-law version justify the score identity. The implementable score estimator has bias as well as variance; it is not assumed exactly unbiased.</p><p class="companion-source">Source: <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1">2609.06906v1</a>. Section 3.1, (3.1)–(3.2); Section 4.1</p><p><a class="button" href="../../../live/index.html?workspace=ASTIS-SW-SPHMC-2026&theorem=sphmc-w2&step=sphmc-w2-step-01">Track or question this step in Live Formalization</a></p><details class="companion-lean"><summary>Lean step — not formalized yet</summary><p>This mathematical step is still open in Lean. The future declaration must expose exactly the objects and side conditions named above; no source-cited wrapper is counted as a proof.</p><p>No corresponding ASTIS declaration is asserted. Search locations below are candidates, not established dependencies or copied library proofs.</p></details></li><li id="sphmc-w2-step-02"><h4>Integrate the smoothed Hamiltonian equation</h4><div class="formula sw-casebook-formula">\[X_t=X_0+tP_0-\int_0^t(t-s)\nabla V_\eta(X_s)\,ds\]</div><p>Picard iteration approximates the trajectory, while Chebyshev–Lobatto quadrature approximates its integrals. Smoothing regularity, quadrature remainder and stochastic score error are separate inputs to the local-error estimate.</p><p class="companion-source">Source: <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1">2609.06906v1</a>. Sections 3.2, 4.2–4.4; Appendix B</p><p><a class="button" href="../../../live/index.html?workspace=ASTIS-SW-SPHMC-2026&theorem=sphmc-w2&step=sphmc-w2-step-02">Track or question this step in Live Formalization</a></p><details class="companion-lean"><summary>Lean step — not formalized yet</summary><p>This mathematical step is still open in Lean. The future declaration must expose exactly the objects and side conditions named above; no source-cited wrapper is counted as a proof.</p><p>No corresponding ASTIS declaration is asserted. Search locations below are candidates, not established dependencies or copied library proofs.</p></details></li><li id="sphmc-w2-step-03"><h4>Budget all three errors</h4><div class="formula sw-casebook-formula">\[W_2(\widehat\pi,\pi)\le W_2(\widehat\pi,\pi_\eta)+W_2(\pi_\eta,\pi)\]</div><p>The first term includes iteration and numerical errors; the second is the smoothing bias. Finite second moments and the exact-kernel contraction must be supplied before using this triangle inequality.</p><p class="companion-source">Source: <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1">2609.06906v1</a>. Section 4.5; Appendix C.3</p><p><a class="button" href="../../../live/index.html?workspace=ASTIS-SW-SPHMC-2026&theorem=sphmc-w2&step=sphmc-w2-step-03">Track or question this step in Live Formalization</a></p><details class="companion-lean"><summary>Lean step — not formalized yet</summary><p>This mathematical step is still open in Lean. The future declaration must expose exactly the objects and side conditions named above; no source-cited wrapper is counted as a proof.</p><p>No corresponding ASTIS declaration is asserted. Search locations below are candidates, not established dependencies or copied library proofs.</p></details></li></ol>
<details class="companion-lean"><summary>Lean proof — not formalized yet</summary><p>Formalization will first match the named technology interfaces, then assemble this source theorem. No placeholder proof or source-cited wrapper has been added.</p><p>No corresponding ASTIS declaration is asserted. Search locations below are candidates, not established dependencies or copied library proofs.</p></details>
<h3>Strict boundary</h3><p>No Wq, Renyi warmness, exact stationarity of a discretization, or cost-free proximal oracle follows from this W2 statement.</p></section><section class="companion-theorem" id="sphmc-proxy" data-proof-status="planned">
<p class="companion-status">RED · source theorem known · local proof open</p>
<h2>An implementable law near a Renyi-warm proxy</h2>
<p class="companion-source">Source: <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1">2609.06906v1</a>. SPHMC Theorem 1.2; Theorem 7.1(ii); Sections 5–6</p>
<p><a class="button primary" href="../../../live/index.html?workspace=ASTIS-SW-SPHMC-2026&theorem=sphmc-proxy">Open this theorem in the interactive research workspace</a></p>
<h3>Statement</h3><p>For every allowed delta the algorithm produces the actual law below. A second, existential comparison law is close in TV and has bounded order-2 Renyi divergence. The comparison law need not be directly sampled.</p>
<ul><li>The common setting and the same reference-point bound as Theorem 1.1.</li><li>0 &lt; delta &lt; 1/2.</li><li>The recursive generator uses higher-moment smoothed sampling and terminal RGO implementation; this is not a general W2-to-Renyi implication.</li></ul><div class="formula sw-casebook-formula">\[\exists\widehat\pi_\delta^\dagger:\quad \operatorname{TV}(\widehat\pi_\delta,\widehat\pi_\delta^\dagger)\le\delta,\quad R_2(\widehat\pi_\delta^\dagger\Vert\pi)\le1,\qquad \mathbb E Q_\nabla=O\!\left(\kappa^{7/6}d^{1/6}\log^9\frac{e\kappa d}{\delta}\right)\]</div>
<details class="companion-lean"><summary>Lean statement — not formalized yet</summary><p>A future declaration must bind the probability laws, normalization, regularity, source algorithm and oracle model explicitly. The formula is a source theorem contract, not Lean code.</p><p>No corresponding ASTIS declaration is asserted. Search locations below are candidates, not established dependencies or copied library proofs.</p></details>
<h3>Proof architecture and calculations</h3><p>This is a source-linked proof route, not a complete reconstruction of all cited lemmas. The dependencies below remain separate formalization tasks.</p>
<ol class="companion-steps"><li id="sphmc-proxy-step-01"><h4>Turn a moment bound into a nearby bounded-displacement coupling</h4><div class="formula sw-casebook-formula">\[W_p(P,Q)\le r\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\exists P^\dagger:\ \operatorname{TV}(P,P^\dagger)\le\delta,\quad W_\infty(P^\dagger,Q)\le r\delta^{-1/p}\]</div><p>For p &gt;= 2 and finite p-moments, replace a coupled sample by its partner on the event where displacement exceeds r delta^(-1/p). Markov&#x27;s inequality bounds that event; measurability and coupling attainment must be justified.</p><p class="companion-source">Source: <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1">2609.06906v1</a>. Lemma 6.2</p><p><a class="button" href="../../../live/index.html?workspace=ASTIS-SW-SPHMC-2026&theorem=sphmc-proxy&step=sphmc-proxy-step-01">Track or question this step in Live Formalization</a></p><details class="companion-lean"><summary>Lean step — not formalized yet</summary><p>This mathematical step is still open in Lean. The future declaration must expose exactly the objects and side conditions named above; no source-cited wrapper is counted as a proof.</p><p>No corresponding ASTIS declaration is asserted. Search locations below are candidates, not established dependencies or copied library proofs.</p></details></li><li id="sphmc-proxy-step-02"><h4>Regularize both laws before measuring divergence</h4><div class="formula sw-casebook-formula">\[R_q(P^\dagger*\gamma_\tau\Vert Q*\gamma_\tau)\le\frac{qW_p(P,Q)^2}{2\tau\delta^{2/p}},\qquad q&gt;1,\quad\tau&gt;0\]</div><p>A Gaussian reverse-transport bound applies after convolution. Recursive conditional/RGO updates then recover the desired unsmoothed target; deleting that recursion would change the theorem.</p><p class="companion-source">Source: <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1">2609.06906v1</a>. Lemma 6.3(ii); Theorems 6.1 and 6.5</p><p><a class="button" href="../../../live/index.html?workspace=ASTIS-SW-SPHMC-2026&theorem=sphmc-proxy&step=sphmc-proxy-step-02">Track or question this step in Live Formalization</a></p><details class="companion-lean"><summary>Lean step — not formalized yet</summary><p>This mathematical step is still open in Lean. The future declaration must expose exactly the objects and side conditions named above; no source-cited wrapper is counted as a proof.</p><p>No corresponding ASTIS declaration is asserted. Search locations below are candidates, not established dependencies or copied library proofs.</p></details></li><li id="sphmc-proxy-step-03"><h4>Track the moving conditional target and oracle bill</h4><div class="formula sw-casebook-formula">\[(A^+)^{-1}=A^{-1}+a^{-1},\qquad u^+=A^+(u/A+y/a)\]</div><p>Completing the square closes the RGO family under another RGO. The recursive stages and outer proximal sampler improve conditioning; the kappa-squared setup term from Theorem 1.1 is not silently discarded.</p><p class="companion-source">Source: <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1">2609.06906v1</a>. Lemma 6.4; Sections 6.3–6.4 and 7.1</p><p><a class="button" href="../../../live/index.html?workspace=ASTIS-SW-SPHMC-2026&theorem=sphmc-proxy&step=sphmc-proxy-step-03">Track or question this step in Live Formalization</a></p><details class="companion-lean"><summary>Lean step — not formalized yet</summary><p>This mathematical step is still open in Lean. The future declaration must expose exactly the objects and side conditions named above; no source-cited wrapper is counted as a proof.</p><p>No corresponding ASTIS declaration is asserted. Search locations below are candidates, not established dependencies or copied library proofs.</p></details></li></ol>
<details class="companion-lean"><summary>Lean proof — not formalized yet</summary><p>Formalization will first match the named technology interfaces, then assemble this source theorem. No placeholder proof or source-cited wrapper has been added.</p><p>No corresponding ASTIS declaration is asserted. Search locations below are candidates, not established dependencies or copied library proofs.</p></details>
<h3>Strict boundary</h3><p>Only the proxy has the displayed Renyi certificate. Genuine Renyi warmness of the actual output requires the stronger W_psi2 input in the separate source theorem.</p></section><p><a href="proof-technology.html">Expand reusable prerequisites and next packets →</a></p></article><section id="affine-gibbs" data-publication-item="affine-gibbs"><h2>Actual affine normalized Gibbs transport</h2><p>Fan Chen, Sinho Chewi, Jianfeng Lu, Matthew S. Zhang; canonical affine prerequisite.</p><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.Ex8">arXiv:2609.06906v1 · SPHMC arXiv:2609.06906v1 standingS1.E1/S2.p1.1, true RGO densityS2.Ex1, actualprox S2.E1/S4.Ex3/S4.SS1.p4.1, literalrho S4.Ex8, trueaffine law/rhocurvature S4.SS1.p4.2, scoped mode/Lip/IBP S4.SS1.p4.3 and only lastTWO4.6 inequalities S4.E6; FIRSTW2/TalagrandLSI and numericalcap/work excluded.</a> · faithful paraphrase</p><h3>Complete source statement (Samplinglib restatement)</h3><p>The actual pushforward of the normalized Lebesgue Gibbs tilt under x-&gt;p+s*x is the normalized Lebesgue tilt with inverse-affine potential x-&gt;F(s^-1*(x-p)). The nonzero actual Lebesgue Jacobian cancels between partition and set numerator. This canonical identity alone makes no probability assertion.</p><ul><li>Finite real Hilbert/Borel full space E, including dimension zero; any function F:E-&gt;R, any translation p:E and a real scale s!=0.</li><li>No continuity, measurability of F, exponential integrability, probability or moment premise. Equality is of Mathlib totalized normalized tilted measures; probability is a separate obligation for every consumer.</li></ul><h3>Actual affine normalized Gibbs transport</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[T(x)=p+sx,\quad H(x)=s^{-1}(x-p),\quad T_\#(Z_F^{-1}e^{-F}dx)=Z_{F\circ H}^{-1}e^{-F\circ H}dx.\]</div><h2>Read the formalized proofs</h2><p>Each statement and proof below has its own closed Lean disclosure. Samplinglib parents, Mathlib calls and external mathematical sources are distinguished in each proof.</p><article class="proof-reader" data-authored-declaration="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.AffineGibbs.map_affine_gibbs"><div class="eyebrow">Samplinglib mathematical lesson</div><h2>Actual affine normalized Gibbs transport</h2><p><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.AffineGibbs.map_affine_gibbs</code> · theorem · <a href="../../../lessons/index.html">Teaching coverage</a></p><section class="reader-first-pass" aria-label="First reading"><div class="reader-first-pass-label">Read this first</div><h2>Mathematical statement</h2><p class="reader-claim">The actual pushforward of the normalized Lebesgue Gibbs tilt under x-&gt;p+s*x is the normalized Lebesgue tilt with inverse-affine potential x-&gt;F(s^-1*(x-p)). The nonzero actual Lebesgue Jacobian cancels between partition and set numerator. This canonical identity alone makes no probability assertion.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation reader-main-equation">\[T(x)=p+sx,\quad H(x)=s^{-1}(x-p),\quad T_\#(Z_F^{-1}e^{-F}dx)=Z_{F\circ H}^{-1}e^{-F\circ H}dx.\]</div><h3>Proof roadmap</h3><ol class="proof-roadmap"><li><span>1</span><strong>Fix the true affine inverse</strong><p>Use T(x)=p+s*x and H(x)=s^-1*(x-p). Nonzero s gives H(T(x))=x and a measurable forward map, even for negative scales or zero dimension.</p></li><li><span>2</span><strong>Transform the actual Lebesgue integrals</strong><p>The canonical Haar scalar and translation change of variables applies to actual Bochner integrals. Apply it to exp(-F o H) and to its measurable-set indicator. Both the original partition and set numerator acquire the same real factor J; no moment or integrability estimate is assumed.</p></li><li><span>3</span><strong>Cancel the Jacobian in the normalized law</strong><p>Use map_apply on the measurable affine map and tilted_apply_eq_ofReal_integral on each measurable set. J!=0 cancels in the numerator/partition ratio. Mathlib totalizes improper integrals; this identity does not certify probability. The source consumer below derives positive integrability internally.</p></li></ol></section><details class="reader-ledger assumption-ledger"><summary>Full assumptions and notation</summary><h3>All objects and hypotheses</h3><ul><li>Finite real Hilbert/Borel full space E, including dimension zero; any function F:E-&gt;R, any translation p:E and a real scale s!=0.</li><li>No continuity, measurability of F, exponential integrability, probability or moment premise. Equality is of Mathlib totalized normalized tilted measures; probability is a separate obligation for every consumer.</li></ul><p class="muted"></p></details><h2>Mathematical proof</h2><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>1. Fix the true affine inverse</h4><p>Use T(x)=p+s*x and H(x)=s^-1*(x-p). Nonzero s gives H(T(x))=x and a measurable forward map, even for negative scales or zero dimension.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[H(T(x))=x,\quad J=|s^{-d}|\ne0.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>map_affine_gibbs; Measurable.smul; smul_smul</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>2. Transform the actual Lebesgue integrals</h4><p>The canonical Haar scalar and translation change of variables applies to actual Bochner integrals. Apply it to exp(-F o H) and to its measurable-set indicator. Both the original partition and set numerator acquire the same real factor J; no moment or integrability estimate is assumed.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[Z_F=JZ_{F\circ H},\quad\int_{T^{-1}B}e^{-F}dx=J\int_Be^{-F\circ H}dx.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>Measure.integral_comp_smul; integral_add_left_eq_self; integral_indicator</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>3. Cancel the Jacobian in the normalized law</h4><p>Use map_apply on the measurable affine map and tilted_apply_eq_ofReal_integral on each measurable set. J!=0 cancels in the numerator/partition ratio. Mathlib totalizes improper integrals; this identity does not certify probability. The source consumer below derives positive integrability internally.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\frac{J\int_Be^{-F\circ H}}{JZ_{F\circ H}}=\frac{\int_Be^{-F\circ H}}{Z_{F\circ H}}.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>Measure.map_apply; tilted_apply_eq_ofReal_integral&#x27;; integral_div; mul_div_mul_left</p></details></div><section class="lean-verification"><h2>Lean verification</h2><p>The exact proposition and proof begin folded so the mathematical argument remains the primary reading path.</p><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-statement" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.AffineGibbs.map_affine_gibbs"><summary>Lean statement · map_affine_gibbs</summary><p>The actual pushforward of the normalized Lebesgue Gibbs tilt under x-&gt;p+s*x is the normalized Lebesgue tilt with inverse-affine potential x-&gt;F(s^-1*(x-p)). The nonzero actual Lebesgue Jacobian cancels between partition and set numerator. This canonical identity alone makes no probability assertion.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem map_affine_gibbs (F : E → ℝ) (p : E) {s : ℝ} (hs : s ≠ 0) :
    Measure.map (fun x : E =&gt; p+s • x)
      ((volume : Measure E).tilted (fun x =&gt; -F x)) =
      volume.tilted (fun x =&gt; -F (s⁻¹ • (x-p)))</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-measure-affinegibbs.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-proof" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.AffineGibbs.map_affine_gibbs"><summary>Lean proof · map_affine_gibbs</summary><p>Fix the true affine inverse; Transform the actual Lebesgue integrals; Cancel the Jacobian in the normalized law</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem map_affine_gibbs (F : E → ℝ) (p : E) {s : ℝ} (hs : s ≠ 0) :
    Measure.map (fun x : E =&gt; p+s • x)
      ((volume : Measure E).tilted (fun x =&gt; -F x)) =
      volume.tilted (fun x =&gt; -F (s⁻¹ • (x-p))) := by
  let T := fun x : E =&gt; p+s • x
  let H := fun x : E =&gt; s⁻¹ • (x-p)
  let J : ℝ := |(s^Module.finrank ℝ E)⁻¹|
  have hJ : J ≠ 0 := abs_ne_zero.mpr (inv_ne_zero (pow_ne_zero _ hs))
  have hm : Measurable T := by dsimp [T]; fun_prop
  have hHT (x : E) : H (T x) = x := by simp [H,T,smul_smul,hs]
  have change_variables (f : E → ℝ) : (∫ x, f (T x)) = J*(∫ x, f x) := by
    have h := Measure.integral_comp_smul (volume : Measure E) (fun x =&gt; f (p+x)) s
    simpa only [integral_add_left_eq_self,smul_eq_mul,T,J] using h
  have hZ : (∫ x, Real.exp (-F x)) = J*(∫ x, Real.exp (-F (H x))) := by
    simpa only [hHT] using change_variables (fun x =&gt; Real.exp (-F (H x)))
  ext B hB
  rw [Measure.map_apply hm hB,tilted_apply_eq_ofReal_integral&#x27; _ (hB.preimage hm),
    tilted_apply_eq_ofReal_integral&#x27; _ hB]
  congr 1
  rw [integral_div,integral_div]
  have hI : (∫ x in T ⁻¹&#x27; B, Real.exp (-F x)) =
      J*(∫ x in B, Real.exp (-F (H x))) := by
    have h := change_variables (B.indicator (fun x =&gt; Real.exp (-F (H x))))
    have he : (fun x =&gt; B.indicator (fun y =&gt; Real.exp (-F (H y))) (T x)) =
        (T ⁻¹&#x27; B).indicator (fun x =&gt; Real.exp (-F x)) := by
      funext x
      by_cases hx : T x ∈ B &lt;;&gt; simp [Set.indicator,hx,hHT]
    rw [he,integral_indicator (hB.preimage hm),integral_indicator hB] at h
    exact h
  rw [hI,hZ]
  exact mul_div_mul_left _ _ hJ

end
end AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.AffineGibbs</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-measure-affinegibbs.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details></section><section data-source-comparison="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.AffineGibbs.map_affine_gibbs"><h3>Source assumptions versus formal assumptions</h3><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Actual Lean</th><th>Difference kind</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Euclidean source true RGO standardization and normalized density; standing beta1/alphakappainv.</td><td>Finite real Hilbert/Borel full space E, including dimension zero; any function F:E-&gt;R, any translation p:E and a real scale s!=0.
No continuity, measurability of F, exponential integrability, probability or moment premise. Equality is of Mathlib totalized normalized tilted measures; probability is a separate obligation for every consumer.</td><td>generalization</td><td>Disclose finite Hilbert/Borel incl0 and measurable parameter-space extensions. Canonical affine identity is more general and does not assert probability; actual source consumer produces probability and moments.</td></tr><tr><td>Printed density proportionality and position IBP omit full normalization/regularity details.</td><td>The actual pushforward of the normalized Lebesgue Gibbs tilt under x-&gt;p+s*x is the normalized Lebesgue tilt with inverse-affine potential x-&gt;F(s^-1*(x-p)). The nonzero actual Lebesgue Jacobian cancels between partition and set numerator. This canonical identity alone makes no probability assertion.</td><td>source-implicit</td><td>Actual Haar Jacobian and constant cancellation are internally proved; exact noncompact IBP producer is reused. No normalizer/law/moment certificate is added.</td></tr><tr><td>First W2 comparison4.6, bias/MGF/fullLemma4.2, higher/Picard/Wp/warmness, main results and expected query costs.</td><td>Canonical signed affine identity for arbitrary F and nonzero scale, no eta/curvature/probability/integrability premise. The current actual SPHMC consumer separately produces allpositiveeta true posterior normalization and lastTWO4.6 moments. FIRSTW2/bias/MGF/fullLemma/main/work/composition remainOPEN; generic map identity itself is no probability assertion.</td><td>unresolved</td><td>The last TWO squared comparisons alone do not establish W2 or full Lemma4.2. No numerical-prox cap, algorithm/work or TV-to-unbounded-cost transfer is claimed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Canonical signed affine identity for arbitrary F and nonzero scale, no eta/curvature/probability/integrability premise. The current actual SPHMC consumer separately produces allpositiveeta true posterior normalization and lastTWO4.6 moments. FIRSTW2/bias/MGF/fullLemma/main/work/composition remainOPEN; generic map identity itself is no probability assertion.</p><p><strong>Encoder–denoiser:</strong> accepted · equivalent-after-elaboration</p><p>A generalization is not a source correction. Proposed missing conditions require separate independent repair review. No proposed repair silently changes the original theorem.</p></section><details class="reader-ledger boundary-ledger"><summary>Scope and omitted-condition boundaries</summary><ul><li>Canonical signed affine identity for arbitrary F and nonzero scale, no eta/curvature/probability/integrability premise. The current actual SPHMC consumer separately produces allpositiveeta true posterior normalization and lastTWO4.6 moments. FIRSTW2/bias/MGF/fullLemma/main/work/composition remainOPEN; generic map identity itself is no probability assertion.</li></ul></details><details class="proof-reader-provenance reader-ledger"><summary>Sources, dependencies, and reuse</summary><h2>Source and reuse</h2><h3>Samplinglib parents called</h3><ul></ul><h3>Mathlib API called (external library)</h3><ul><li>Measure.integral_comp_smul</li><li>integral_add_left_eq_self</li><li>integral_indicator</li><li>Measure.map_apply</li><li>tilted_apply_eq_ofReal_integral&#x27;</li><li>mul_div_mul_left</li></ul><h3>Mathematical sources</h3><ul><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.Ex8">SPHMC Ex8/p4.2/p4.3 and scoped4.6; affine prerequisite</a> — Canonical signed affine identity for arbitrary F and nonzero scale, no eta/curvature/probability/integrability premise. The current actual SPHMC consumer separately produces allpositiveeta true posterior normalization and lastTWO4.6 moments. FIRSTW2/bias/MGF/fullLemma/main/work/composition remainOPEN; generic map identity itself is no probability assertion.</li></ul><p>Samplinglib prose is not a quotation or a source-equivalence certificate. Definitions and aliases are explained as constructions, not counted as new mathematical proofs.</p></details></article><h3>Which proof obligations and prerequisites are covered?</h3><ul><li><span class="status status-green">Independently verified prerequisite</span> True standardized RGO and last TWO4.6 comparisons</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> First W2 comparison, bias/MGF and full Lemma4.2 remaining</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Both main results and actual-input expected-query-cost composition remaining</li></ul></section><section id="balanced-rademacher-count-clt" data-publication-item="balanced-rademacher-count-clt"><h2>Actual finite-count Rademacher sums converge weakly to the unit Gaussian</h2><p>Mathlib Thomas Zhu and Etienne Marion scalar CLT; ASTIS-authored actual count-law/zero-index adapter. Independent source-first exact StatementSeal; Gaussian function entropy and full flip-energy limits remain separate.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/db584cd6d46c92f209a44c0f1c829460d327499d/Mathlib/Probability/CentralLimitTheorem.lean#L59">Pinned Mathlib db584cd6; Lean4.33.0. SPHMC arXiv2609.06906v1 background, not its printed theorem. · tendsto_charFun_inv_sqrt_mul_pow L59-L76; LevyConvergence L215; authored actual Bool-count specialization; SPHMC FIRST S4.E6 GaussianLSI omitted background</a> · faithful paraphrase</p><h3>Complete source statement (Samplinglib restatement)</h3><p>There exists a family of real probability laws lambda_N, for every natural N, equal to the actual pushforward of normalized counting measure on all Fin N -&gt; Bool configurations by the signed sum divided by sqrt(N). The true sign is +1 and the false sign is -1. Its zero law is dirac0, and the successor sequence lambda_(N+1) converges weakly to gaussianReal0,1 (mean0, variance1). No law, normalization, moment, independence or CLT certificate is supplied.</p><ul><li>Closed authored background specialization; no caller mathematical premises.</li><li>Actual finite carrier and normalized count law, literal sign true1/false-1, totalized inverse square root and empty zero sum. All probability, measurability and finite moment domains are produced internally.</li><li>ProbabilityMeasure Real has the canonical weak law topology. gaussianReal0,1 uses varianceNNReal1. No entropy, energy, W2, LSI, T2 or cost upgrade is implied.</li></ul><h3>Actual finite-count Rademacher sums converge weakly to the unit Gaussian</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\mu_N=2^{-N}\sum_{\varepsilon\in\{0,1\}^N}\delta_\varepsilon,\quad S_N(\varepsilon)=N^{-1/2}\sum_{j=1}^N\sigma(\varepsilon_j),\quad\lambda_N=(S_N)_\#\mu_N,\quad\lambda_0=\delta_0,\quad\lambda_{N+1}\Rightarrow\mathcal N(0,1).\]</div><h2>Read the formalized proofs</h2><p>Each statement and proof below has its own closed Lean disclosure. Samplinglib parents, Mathlib calls and external mathematical sources are distinguished in each proof.</p><article class="proof-reader" data-authored-declaration="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.BalancedRademacherCLT.balanced_count_sum_tendsto_gaussian"><div class="eyebrow">Samplinglib mathematical lesson</div><h2>Actual finite-count Rademacher sums converge weakly to the unit Gaussian</h2><p><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.BalancedRademacherCLT.balanced_count_sum_tendsto_gaussian</code> · theorem · <a href="../../../lessons/index.html">Teaching coverage</a></p><section class="reader-first-pass" aria-label="First reading"><div class="reader-first-pass-label">Read this first</div><h2>Mathematical statement</h2><p class="reader-claim">There exists a family of real probability laws lambda_N, for every natural N, equal to the actual pushforward of normalized counting measure on all Fin N -&gt; Bool configurations by the signed sum divided by sqrt(N). The true sign is +1 and the false sign is -1. Its zero law is dirac0, and the successor sequence lambda_(N+1) converges weakly to gaussianReal0,1 (mean0, variance1). No law, normalization, moment, independence or CLT certificate is supplied.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation reader-main-equation">\[\mu_N=2^{-N}\sum_{\varepsilon\in\{0,1\}^N}\delta_\varepsilon,\quad S_N(\varepsilon)=N^{-1/2}\sum_{j=1}^N\sigma(\varepsilon_j),\quad\lambda_N=(S_N)_\#\mu_N,\quad\lambda_0=\delta_0,\quad\lambda_{N+1}\Rightarrow\mathcal N(0,1).\]</div><h3>Proof roadmap</h3><ol class="proof-roadmap"><li><span>1</span><strong>Build the actual laws, including zero</strong><p>The finite carrier has positive cardinal2^N. Scaling counting measure by its reciprocal gives mass1. All finite functions are measurable and Bochner integrable; each normalized sum is pushed forward to a genuine probability law. AtN0 the empty sum is0, so its map law is the unit atom at0, without any fallback.</p></li><li><span>2</span><strong>Produce the single-coordinate moments</strong><p>Use the actual balanced Bool count law and sigma(true)=1, sigma(false)=-1. The finite integral is the two-value average; hence the coordinate has mean0 and secondmoment1. These moments and measurable sign map are proof ingredients, never target premises.</p></li><li><span>3</span><strong>Prove the finite characteristic-function product</strong><p>For every configuration, exp(i t sum sigma_j) is the product of exp(i t sigma_j). Sum over all configurations using Fintype.sum_pow; multiplying by2^-N gives the Nth power of the actual single-coordinate characteristic function. This is an authored count-law adapter, not an assumed independence or copied infinite-iid construction.</p></li><li><span>4</span><strong>Apply the pinned scalar CLT to the derived moments</strong><p>Mathlib characteristic-function CLT applies to this actual measurable balanced coordinate with its internally derived mean0 and secondmoment1. It yields convergence of the same Nth-power expression to exp(-t^2/2), the exact characteristic function of mean0 variance1 Gaussian.</p></li><li><span>5</span><strong>Return weak convergence and the actual successor family</strong><p>Levy convergence upgrades the pointwise characteristic-function limit of genuine probability measures to weak convergence. Composition with the cofinal n-&gt;n+1 sequence gives the sealed positive-index limit. The same family supplies every literal map equality and its zero atom.</p></li></ol></section><details class="reader-ledger assumption-ledger"><summary>Full assumptions and notation</summary><h3>All objects and hypotheses</h3><ul><li>Closed authored background specialization; no caller mathematical premises.</li><li>Actual finite carrier and normalized count law, literal sign true1/false-1, totalized inverse square root and empty zero sum. All probability, measurability and finite moment domains are produced internally.</li><li>ProbabilityMeasure Real has the canonical weak law topology. gaussianReal0,1 uses varianceNNReal1. No entropy, energy, W2, LSI, T2 or cost upgrade is implied.</li></ul><p class="muted"></p></details><h2>Mathematical proof</h2><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>1. Build the actual laws, including zero</h4><p>The finite carrier has positive cardinal2^N. Scaling counting measure by its reciprocal gives mass1. All finite functions are measurable and Bochner integrable; each normalized sum is pushed forward to a genuine probability law. AtN0 the empty sum is0, so its map law is the unit atom at0, without any fallback.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\mu_N(\{0,1\}^N)=2^{-N}2^N=1,\qquad S_0=0,\quad\lambda_0=\delta_0.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>private countLaw_probability; countLaw_integral; actualLaw; actualLaw_zero</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>2. Produce the single-coordinate moments</h4><p>Use the actual balanced Bool count law and sigma(true)=1, sigma(false)=-1. The finite integral is the two-value average; hence the coordinate has mean0 and secondmoment1. These moments and measurable sign map are proof ingredients, never target premises.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\mathbb E\sigma=\tfrac12(1-1)=0,\qquad\mathbb E\sigma^2=\tfrac12(1+1)=1.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>private sign_mean; sign_second_moment; measurable_of_countable</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>3. Prove the finite characteristic-function product</h4><p>For every configuration, exp(i t sum sigma_j) is the product of exp(i t sigma_j). Sum over all configurations using Fintype.sum_pow; multiplying by2^-N gives the Nth power of the actual single-coordinate characteristic function. This is an authored count-law adapter, not an assumed independence or copied infinite-iid construction.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\widehat\lambda_N(t)=\left[\tfrac12\sum_{b\in\{0,1\}}\exp(i t\sigma(b)/\sqrt N)\right]^N.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>private coordinate_charFun; unscaled_charFun; normalized_charFun; Complex.exp_sum; Fintype.sum_pow</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>4. Apply the pinned scalar CLT to the derived moments</h4><p>Mathlib characteristic-function CLT applies to this actual measurable balanced coordinate with its internally derived mean0 and secondmoment1. It yields convergence of the same Nth-power expression to exp(-t^2/2), the exact characteristic function of mean0 variance1 Gaussian.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\widehat\lambda_N(t)\longrightarrow\exp(-t^2/2)=\widehat{\mathcal N(0,1)}(t).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>ProbabilityTheory.tendsto_charFun_inv_sqrt_mul_pow; ProbabilityTheory.charFun_gaussianReal</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>5. Return weak convergence and the actual successor family</h4><p>Levy convergence upgrades the pointwise characteristic-function limit of genuine probability measures to weak convergence. Composition with the cofinal n-&gt;n+1 sequence gives the sealed positive-index limit. The same family supplies every literal map equality and its zero atom.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\lambda_N\Rightarrow\mathcal N(0,1)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\lambda_{N+1}\Rightarrow\mathcal N(0,1).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>MeasureTheory.ProbabilityMeasure.tendsto_iff_tendsto_charFun; private actualLaw_tendsto; Filter.tendsto_add_atTop_nat</p></details></div><section class="lean-verification"><h2>Lean verification</h2><p>The exact proposition and proof begin folded so the mathematical argument remains the primary reading path.</p><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-statement" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.BalancedRademacherCLT.balanced_count_sum_tendsto_gaussian"><summary>Lean statement · balanced_count_sum_tendsto_gaussian</summary><p>There exists a family of real probability laws lambda_N, for every natural N, equal to the actual pushforward of normalized counting measure on all Fin N -&gt; Bool configurations by the signed sum divided by sqrt(N). The true sign is +1 and the false sign is -1. Its zero law is dirac0, and the successor sequence lambda_(N+1) converges weakly to gaussianReal0,1 (mean0, variance1). No law, normalization, moment, independence or CLT certificate is supplied.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem balanced_count_sum_tendsto_gaussian :
    ∃ laws : ℕ → ProbabilityMeasure ℝ,
      (∀ n : ℕ, (laws n : Measure ℝ) =
        (((Fintype.card (Fin n → Bool) : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ •
          (Measure.count : Measure (Fin n → Bool))).map
          (fun ε : Fin n → Bool =&gt; (Real.sqrt (n : ℝ))⁻¹ *
            ∑ j : Fin n, if ε j then (1 : ℝ) else -1))) ∧
      (laws 0 : Measure ℝ) = Measure.dirac 0 ∧
      Tendsto (fun n : ℕ =&gt; laws (n + 1)) atTop
        (𝓝 (⟨gaussianReal 0 1, inferInstance⟩ : ProbabilityMeasure ℝ))</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-probability-balancedrademacherclt.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-proof" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.BalancedRademacherCLT.balanced_count_sum_tendsto_gaussian"><summary>Lean proof · balanced_count_sum_tendsto_gaussian</summary><p>Build the actual laws, including zero; Produce the single-coordinate moments; Prove the finite characteristic-function product; Apply the pinned scalar CLT to the derived moments; Return weak convergence and the actual successor family</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem balanced_count_sum_tendsto_gaussian :
    ∃ laws : ℕ → ProbabilityMeasure ℝ,
      (∀ n : ℕ, (laws n : Measure ℝ) =
        (((Fintype.card (Fin n → Bool) : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ •
          (Measure.count : Measure (Fin n → Bool))).map
          (fun ε : Fin n → Bool =&gt; (Real.sqrt (n : ℝ))⁻¹ *
            ∑ j : Fin n, if ε j then (1 : ℝ) else -1))) ∧
      (laws 0 : Measure ℝ) = Measure.dirac 0 ∧
      Tendsto (fun n : ℕ =&gt; laws (n + 1)) atTop
        (𝓝 (⟨gaussianReal 0 1, inferInstance⟩ : ProbabilityMeasure ℝ)) := by
  refine ⟨actualLaw, ?_, actualLaw_zero, ?_⟩
  · intro n
    rfl
  · exact actualLaw_tendsto.comp (tendsto_add_atTop_nat 1)

end AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.BalancedRademacherCLT</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-probability-balancedrademacherclt.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details></section><section data-source-comparison="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.BalancedRademacherCLT.balanced_count_sum_tendsto_gaussian"><h3>Source assumptions versus formal assumptions</h3><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Actual Lean</th><th>Difference kind</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pinned scalar characteristic-function CLT requires measurable coordinate, mean0 and secondmoment1.</td><td>Closed actual balanced finite-count specialization derives those ingredients internally.</td><td>same</td><td>No general CLT proof is duplicated and no certificate becomes a public binder; actual finite adapter is disclosed as authored.</td></tr><tr><td>Finite Boolean normalized sum and variance-one real Gaussian background.</td><td>All N actual map laws; N0 actual dirac0; cofinal successor weak convergence.</td><td>source-implicit</td><td>The empty finite sum and inverse0 yield the exact degenerate law. No variance-one claim is made atN0.</td></tr><tr><td>Gaussian entropy/full-flip-energy limit, LSI, dimension/cutoff extension and Talagrand transport.</td><td>Actual finite Bool count/card map for all N, signs true1 false-1, inverse sqrtN; N0 dirac0 and positive successor weak-law Gaussian0,1. No input law/moment/limit certificate. No Gaussian entropy/energy/LSI/T2/paper completion. Focused3160PASS: actual N1 mean0/secondmoment1 and actual N0secondmoment0, three standard3 prints. Independent wholeproof/blind/source/topology/commit admission and shared integration remain separate.</td><td>unresolved</td><td>Weak convergence is insufficient for unbounded observables, entropy/energy limits or unbounded expected costs; every downstream consumer remains open.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Actual finite Bool count/card map for all N, signs true1 false-1, inverse sqrtN; N0 dirac0 and positive successor weak-law Gaussian0,1. No input law/moment/limit certificate. No Gaussian entropy/energy/LSI/T2/paper completion. Focused3160PASS: actual N1 mean0/secondmoment1 and actual N0secondmoment0, three standard3 prints. Independent wholeproof/blind/source/topology/commit admission and shared integration remain separate.</p><p><strong>Encoder–denoiser:</strong> accepted · equivalent-after-elaboration</p><p>A generalization is not a source correction. Proposed missing conditions require separate independent repair review. No proposed repair silently changes the original theorem.</p></section><details class="reader-ledger boundary-ledger"><summary>Scope and omitted-condition boundaries</summary><ul><li>Actual finite Bool count/card map for all N, signs true1 false-1, inverse sqrtN; N0 dirac0 and positive successor weak-law Gaussian0,1. No input law/moment/limit certificate. No Gaussian entropy/energy/LSI/T2/paper completion. Focused3160PASS: actual N1 mean0/secondmoment1 and actual N0secondmoment0, three standard3 prints. Independent wholeproof/blind/source/topology/commit admission and shared integration remain separate.</li></ul></details><details class="proof-reader-provenance reader-ledger"><summary>Sources, dependencies, and reuse</summary><h2>Source and reuse</h2><h3>Samplinglib parents called</h3><ul></ul><h3>Mathlib API called (external library)</h3><ul><li>ProbabilityTheory.tendsto_charFun_inv_sqrt_mul_pow</li><li>MeasureTheory.ProbabilityMeasure.tendsto_iff_tendsto_charFun</li><li>MeasureTheory.charFun_map_mul_comp</li><li>Fintype.sum_pow</li><li>Complex.exp_sum</li><li>MeasureTheory.integral_fintype</li></ul><h3>Mathematical sources</h3><ul><li><a href="https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/db584cd6d46c92f209a44c0f1c829460d327499d/Mathlib/Probability/CentralLimitTheorem.lean#L59">tendsto_charFun_inv_sqrt_mul_pow L59-L76; LevyConvergence L215; authored actual Bool-count specialization; SPHMC FIRST S4.E6 GaussianLSI omitted background</a> — Actual finite Bool count/card map for all N, signs true1 false-1, inverse sqrtN; N0 dirac0 and positive successor weak-law Gaussian0,1. No input law/moment/limit certificate. No Gaussian entropy/energy/LSI/T2/paper completion. Focused3160PASS: actual N1 mean0/secondmoment1 and actual N0secondmoment0, three standard3 prints. Independent wholeproof/blind/source/topology/commit admission and shared integration remain separate.</li><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.E6">Actual SPHMC omitted GaussianLSI background consumer</a> — Not printedFIRST4.6 or GaussianLSI proof.</li></ul><p>Samplinglib prose is not a quotation or a source-equivalence certificate. Definitions and aliases are explained as constructions, not counted as new mathematical proofs.</p></details></article><h3>Which proof obligations and prerequisites are covered?</h3><ul><li><span class="status status-orange">Formalized proof edge; source adapter/review separate</span> Actual finite count normalized-sum law and Gaussian weak limit</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Entropy/full-flip-energy/GaussianLSI/T2/paper main remain open</li></ul></section><section id="bernoulli-function-log-sobolev" data-publication-item="bernoulli-function-log-sobolev"><h2>True finite Boolean function log-Sobolev inequality</h2><p>Yuanhe Zhang, Jason D. Lee, Fanghui Liu. Necessary function-entropy background; SPHMC Gaussian/paper consumers remain open.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/YuanheZ/lean-stat-learning-theory/blob/d0f506f0a695018265dccb33bcb05e2f5ca1c876/SLT/GaussianLSI/BernoulliLSI.lean">Pinned SLT d0f506f0a695018265dccb33bcb05e2f5ca1c876, Apache2; local Lean4.33 Mathlib db584cd6 · bernoulli_logSobolev L1236 onward; actual law L113-L225; authored entropy/RMS induction replaces Han proof route; SPHMC FIRST S4.E6 omitted GaussianLSI background</a> · faithful paraphrase</p><h3>Complete source statement (Samplinglib restatement)</h3><p>For every finite n including zero and arbitrary signed real h on all Boolean coordinates, normalized counting measure is probability and all three integrals are genuine L1; homogeneous entropy of h squared is at most half the expectation of the unnormalized sum of full coordinate-flip squares.</p><ul><li>Arbitrary natural n, including0; arbitrary signed real h on all Fin n→Bool.</li><li>Literal normalized counting law and full Function.update flip; probability/three L1 domains are outputs. No public inequality, smoothness or domain certificates.</li></ul><h3>True finite Boolean function log-Sobolev inequality</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\mu_n=2^{-n}\sum_{\varepsilon\in\{0,1\}^n}\delta_\varepsilon,\qquad\operatorname{Ent}_{\mu_n}(h^2)\le\frac12\mathbb E_{\mu_n}\sum_{j=1}^n(h(\varepsilon)-h(\varepsilon^{(j)}))^2.\]</div><h2>Read the formalized proofs</h2><p>Each statement and proof below has its own closed Lean disclosure. Samplinglib parents, Mathlib calls and external mathematical sources are distinguished in each proof.</p><article class="proof-reader" data-authored-declaration="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.FunctionalInequalities.BernoulliLogSobolev.bernoulli_function_logSobolev"><div class="eyebrow">Samplinglib mathematical lesson</div><h2>True finite Boolean function log-Sobolev inequality</h2><p><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.FunctionalInequalities.BernoulliLogSobolev.bernoulli_function_logSobolev</code> · theorem · <a href="../../../lessons/index.html">Teaching coverage</a></p><section class="reader-first-pass" aria-label="First reading"><div class="reader-first-pass-label">Read this first</div><h2>Mathematical statement</h2><p class="reader-claim">For every finite n including zero and arbitrary signed real h on all Boolean coordinates, normalized counting measure is probability and all three integrals are genuine L1; homogeneous entropy of h squared is at most half the expectation of the unnormalized sum of full coordinate-flip squares.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation reader-main-equation">\[\mu_n=2^{-n}\sum_{\varepsilon\in\{0,1\}^n}\delta_\varepsilon,\qquad\operatorname{Ent}_{\mu_n}(h^2)\le\frac12\mathbb E_{\mu_n}\sum_{j=1}^n(h(\varepsilon)-h(\varepsilon^{(j)}))^2.\]</div><h3>Proof roadmap</h3><ol class="proof-roadmap"><li><span>1</span><strong>Use the actual finite law</strong><p>Construct probability from normalized counting measure and actual cardinal2^n. Finite Bochner integrability is derived, and Fin.snoc gives the true balanced last-coordinate integral decomposition.</p></li><li><span>2</span><strong>Exact entropy chain</strong><p>Let g(x)=sqrt((h(x,+)²+h(x,-)²)/2). Expanding both sides under the same finite law gives an equality: the last-coordinate two-point entropy plus the entropy of g². No conditional entropy bound is assumed.</p></li><li><span>3</span><strong>Genuine RMS contraction</strong><p>The reverse two-dimensional Euclidean norm inequality yields the squared difference bound for g across each retained coordinate. Its proof derives the Cauchy-Schwarz cross term from (ad-bc)²≥0 and actual sqrt square identities.</p></li><li><span>4</span><strong>Integrate the two bounds with the exact energy split</strong><p>Use the signed two-point theorem for the last coordinate and induction for g. Summing the RMS contraction and splitting the actual full-flip energy gives the same half constant. For n=0 there is one state, zero entropy and no energy.</p></li></ol></section><details class="reader-ledger assumption-ledger"><summary>Full assumptions and notation</summary><h3>All objects and hypotheses</h3><ul><li>Arbitrary natural n, including0; arbitrary signed real h on all Fin n→Bool.</li><li>Literal normalized counting law and full Function.update flip; probability/three L1 domains are outputs. No public inequality, smoothness or domain certificates.</li></ul><p class="muted"></p></details><h2>Mathematical proof</h2><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>1. Use the actual finite law</h4><p>Construct probability from normalized counting measure and actual cardinal2^n. Finite Bochner integrability is derived, and Fin.snoc gives the true balanced last-coordinate integral decomposition.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\mathbb E_{\mu_{n+1}}F=\mathbb E_{\mu_n}\frac{F(x,+)+F(x,-)}2.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>private probability; bernoulli_integral_succ_split; Integrable.of_finite</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>2. Exact entropy chain</h4><p>Let g(x)=sqrt((h(x,+)²+h(x,-)²)/2). Expanding both sides under the same finite law gives an equality: the last-coordinate two-point entropy plus the entropy of g². No conditional entropy bound is assumed.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\operatorname{Ent}_{\mu_{n+1}}(h^2)=\mathbb E_{\mu_n}\operatorname{Ent}_2(h_+^2,h_-^2)+\operatorname{Ent}_{\mu_n}(g^2).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>private rms_sq; entropy_chain</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>3. Genuine RMS contraction</h4><p>The reverse two-dimensional Euclidean norm inequality yields the squared difference bound for g across each retained coordinate. Its proof derives the Cauchy-Schwarz cross term from (ad-bc)²≥0 and actual sqrt square identities.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[(\sqrt{(a^2+b^2)/2}-\sqrt{(c^2+d^2)/2})^2\le\tfrac12((a-c)^2+(b-d)^2).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>private rms_contraction; rms_energy</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>4. Integrate the two bounds with the exact energy split</h4><p>Use the signed two-point theorem for the last coordinate and induction for g. Summing the RMS contraction and splitting the actual full-flip energy gives the same half constant. For n=0 there is one state, zero entropy and no energy.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\operatorname{Ent}(h^2)\le\tfrac12\mathbb E L+\tfrac12\mathbb E D_g\le\tfrac12\mathbb E(L+A)=\tfrac12\mathbb E D_h.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>TwoPointEntropy.two_point_squared_entropy_le_half_sq_sub; private energy_chain; entropy_bound</p></details></div><section class="lean-verification"><h2>Lean verification</h2><p>The exact proposition and proof begin folded so the mathematical argument remains the primary reading path.</p><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-statement" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.FunctionalInequalities.BernoulliLogSobolev.bernoulli_function_logSobolev"><summary>Lean statement · bernoulli_function_logSobolev</summary><p>For every finite n including zero and arbitrary signed real h on all Boolean coordinates, normalized counting measure is probability and all three integrals are genuine L1; homogeneous entropy of h squared is at most half the expectation of the unnormalized sum of full coordinate-flip squares.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem bernoulli_function_logSobolev (n : ℕ) (h : (Fin n → Bool) → ℝ) :
    let μ : Measure (Fin n → Bool) :=
      (Fintype.card (Fin n → Bool) : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ • Measure.count
    let D := fun ε : Fin n → Bool =&gt;
      ∑ j : Fin n, (h ε - h (Function.update ε j (!ε j))) ^ 2
    IsProbabilityMeasure μ ∧
      Integrable (fun ε =&gt; (h ε) ^ 2) μ ∧
      Integrable (fun ε =&gt; (h ε) ^ 2 * Real.log ((h ε) ^ 2)) μ ∧
      Integrable D μ ∧
      ((∫ ε, (h ε) ^ 2 * Real.log ((h ε) ^ 2) ∂μ) -
        (∫ ε, (h ε) ^ 2 ∂μ) * Real.log (∫ ε, (h ε) ^ 2 ∂μ) ≤
      (1 / 2 : ℝ) * ∫ ε, D ε ∂μ)</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-functionalinequalities-bernoullilogsobolev.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-proof" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.FunctionalInequalities.BernoulliLogSobolev.bernoulli_function_logSobolev"><summary>Lean proof · bernoulli_function_logSobolev</summary><p>Use the actual finite law; Exact entropy chain; Genuine RMS contraction; Integrate the two bounds with the exact energy split</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem bernoulli_function_logSobolev (n : ℕ) (h : (Fin n → Bool) → ℝ) :
    let μ : Measure (Fin n → Bool) :=
      (Fintype.card (Fin n → Bool) : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ • Measure.count
    let D := fun ε : Fin n → Bool =&gt;
      ∑ j : Fin n, (h ε - h (Function.update ε j (!ε j))) ^ 2
    IsProbabilityMeasure μ ∧
      Integrable (fun ε =&gt; (h ε) ^ 2) μ ∧
      Integrable (fun ε =&gt; (h ε) ^ 2 * Real.log ((h ε) ^ 2)) μ ∧
      Integrable D μ ∧
      ((∫ ε, (h ε) ^ 2 * Real.log ((h ε) ^ 2) ∂μ) -
        (∫ ε, (h ε) ^ 2 ∂μ) * Real.log (∫ ε, (h ε) ^ 2 ∂μ) ≤
      (1 / 2 : ℝ) * ∫ ε, D ε ∂μ) := by
  dsimp only
  change IsProbabilityMeasure (bernoulliUniform n) ∧
    Integrable (fun ε =&gt; (h ε)^2) (bernoulliUniform n) ∧
    Integrable (fun ε =&gt; (h ε)^2 * log ((h ε)^2)) (bernoulliUniform n) ∧
    Integrable (gradientNormSq n h) (bernoulliUniform n) ∧
    entropy n h ≤ (1/2 : ℝ) * ∫ ε, gradientNormSq n h ε ∂bernoulliUniform n
  exact ⟨inferInstance, Integrable.of_finite, Integrable.of_finite,
    Integrable.of_finite, entropy_bound n h⟩

end AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.FunctionalInequalities.BernoulliLogSobolev</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-functionalinequalities-bernoullilogsobolev.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details></section><section data-source-comparison="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.FunctionalInequalities.BernoulliLogSobolev.bernoulli_function_logSobolev"><h3>Source assumptions versus formal assumptions</h3><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Actual Lean</th><th>Difference kind</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Positive Rothaus and true finite Boolean function-LSI.</td><td>For every finite n including zero and arbitrary signed real h on all Boolean coordinates, normalized counting measure is probability and all three integrals are genuine L1; homogeneous entropy of h squared is at most half the expectation of the unnormalized sum of full coordinate-flip squares.</td><td>same</td><td>Signed squares including zero are internally derived from positive Rothaus; exact arbitrary Boolean function-LSI is unchanged.</td></tr><tr><td>Han tensorization implementation upstream.</td><td>Authored exact entropy-chain/RMS induction.</td><td>same</td><td>Proof route disclosed; no caller certificate or statement weakening.</td></tr><tr><td>Gaussian limits and SPHMC LSI/T2/W2/main/composition.</td><td>Genuine signed two-point and true finite Bool function LSI with exact half coefficient, derived probability/L1 domains; authored direct RMS-induction route. No GaussianLSI/T2/FIRST4.6/W2/bias/main/work/composition. Focused2653 passed actual n0/indicator/signed/normalizedRademacher n2 tests; all6 prints standard3 only. Independent wholeproof/blind/source/exactcommit admission, integration and exposition remain separate.</td><td>unresolved</td><td>All downstream analytic/algorithm/cost dependencies remain open.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Genuine signed two-point and true finite Bool function LSI with exact half coefficient, derived probability/L1 domains; authored direct RMS-induction route. No GaussianLSI/T2/FIRST4.6/W2/bias/main/work/composition. Focused2653 passed actual n0/indicator/signed/normalizedRademacher n2 tests; all6 prints standard3 only. Independent wholeproof/blind/source/exactcommit admission, integration and exposition remain separate.</p><p><strong>Encoder–denoiser:</strong> accepted · equivalent-after-elaboration</p><p>A generalization is not a source correction. Proposed missing conditions require separate independent repair review. No proposed repair silently changes the original theorem.</p></section><details class="reader-ledger boundary-ledger"><summary>Scope and omitted-condition boundaries</summary><ul><li>Genuine signed two-point and true finite Bool function LSI with exact half coefficient, derived probability/L1 domains; authored direct RMS-induction route. No GaussianLSI/T2/FIRST4.6/W2/bias/main/work/composition. Focused2653 passed actual n0/indicator/signed/normalizedRademacher n2 tests; all6 prints standard3 only. Independent wholeproof/blind/source/exactcommit admission, integration and exposition remain separate.</li></ul></details><details class="proof-reader-provenance reader-ledger"><summary>Sources, dependencies, and reuse</summary><h2>Source and reuse</h2><h3>Samplinglib parents called</h3><ul><li><a href="../../../theorems/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-functionalinequalities-twopointentropy-two-point-squared-entropy-le-half-sq-sub.html"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.FunctionalInequalities.TwoPointEntropy.two_point_squared_entropy_le_half_sq_sub</code></a></li></ul><h3>Mathlib API called (external library)</h3><ul><li>MeasureTheory.Integrable.of_finite</li><li>MeasureTheory.integral_fintype</li></ul><h3>Mathematical sources</h3><ul><li><a href="https://github.com/YuanheZ/lean-stat-learning-theory/blob/d0f506f0a695018265dccb33bcb05e2f5ca1c876/SLT/GaussianLSI/BernoulliLSI.lean">bernoulli_logSobolev L1236 onward; actual law L113-L225; authored entropy/RMS induction replaces Han proof route</a> — Genuine signed two-point and true finite Bool function LSI with exact half coefficient, derived probability/L1 domains; authored direct RMS-induction route. No GaussianLSI/T2/FIRST4.6/W2/bias/main/work/composition. Focused2653 passed actual n0/indicator/signed/normalizedRademacher n2 tests; all6 prints standard3 only. Independent wholeproof/blind/source/exactcommit admission, integration and exposition remain separate.</li></ul><p>Samplinglib prose is not a quotation or a source-equivalence certificate. Definitions and aliases are explained as constructions, not counted as new mathematical proofs.</p></details></article><h3>Which proof obligations and prerequisites are covered?</h3><ul><li><span class="status status-orange">Formalized proof edge; source adapter/review separate</span> True finite function entropy/energy producer</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Gaussian limits/compact/cutoff/LSI/T2/W2/main/composition remain open</li></ul></section><section id="gaussian-convolution-derivatives" data-publication-item="gaussian-convolution-derivatives"><h2>Actual posterior score and covariance identities</h2><p>Fan Chen, Sinho Chewi, Jianfeng Lu, Matthew S. Zhang; ASTIS original mathematical restatement of equation4.1, with explicit arbitrary-probability and constant-prefactor generalizations.</p><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.SS1">arXiv:2609.06906v1 · arXiv:2609.06906v1 Section4.1 equation4.1 HTML403-412; Section3.1 unnormalized convolution convention; PBPS2609.06905v1 Section2.2 actual Gaussian augmentation/selected backward quadratic tilt</a> · faithful paraphrase</p><h3>Complete source statement (Samplinglib restatement)</h3><p>At every observation y, R_y is a probability measure and the identity map belongs to L2(R_y). For every direction v, DU(y)[v]=inner(y-mean(R_y),v)/eta. For every v,w, D2U(y)[v,w]=inner(v,w)/eta-covarianceBilin(R_y)(v,w)/eta^2. These are actual first and second Frechet derivatives and actual finite posterior moments; the constant positive prefactor cancels exactly.</p><ul><li>E is a finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel measurable space, including dimension zero. mu is an arbitrary probability measure; eta&gt;0 and C&gt;0 are real numbers. No input density, finite input moments, input potential, smoothness, curvature, normalizer or conditional-version certificate is assumed.</li><li>Z(y)=integral exp(-norm(y-x)^2/(2eta))dmu(x), R_y=mu.tilted(-norm(x-y)^2/(2eta)), U(y)=-log(C Z(y)). C is an arbitrary positive constant prefactor; choosing the actual Gaussian C_eta gives its negative-log probability density. Other C values are not asserted to normalize a density.</li><li>R_y is the actual everywhere-selected posterior of the Gaussian augmentation, previously identified as a measurable Markov/disintegration kernel. Probability and finite second moments are derived here; mean is the Bochner integral, covarianceBilin is the true centered bilinear covariance. The new theorem concerns this selected version, never an arbitrary conditional representative.</li><li>For the source Gibbs input, the actual source unnormalized potential V_eta differs from the normalized density potential U_eta by the already-proved constant log Z_V. The genuine Test derives original Gibbs probability from C2 and positive lower Hessian, proves source V_eta directional derivative/covariance identities by this exact constant, and joins the same augmentation backward kernel. Source Hessian upper bound and eta upper cap are unnecessary for these identities.</li></ul><h3>Actual Gaussian posterior derivative identities</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[R_y(dx)=Z(y)^{-1}e^{-\|x-y\|^2/(2\eta)}\mu(dx),\quad X\in L^2(R_y),\quad DU(y)[v]=\eta^{-1}\langle y-\mathbb E_{R_y}X,v\rangle,\quad D^2U(y)[v,w]=\eta^{-1}\langle v,w\rangle-\eta^{-2}\operatorname{Cov}_{R_y}(\langle v,X\rangle,\langle w,X\rangle).\]</div><h2>Read the formalized proofs</h2><p>Each statement and proof below has its own closed Lean disclosure. Samplinglib parents, Mathlib calls and external mathematical sources are distinguished in each proof.</p><article class="proof-reader" data-authored-declaration="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.GaussianConvolutionRegularity.gaussian_convolution_derivatives"><div class="eyebrow">Samplinglib mathematical lesson</div><h2>Actual posterior score and covariance identities</h2><p><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.GaussianConvolutionRegularity.gaussian_convolution_derivatives</code> · theorem · <a href="../../../lessons/index.html">Teaching coverage</a></p><section class="reader-first-pass" aria-label="First reading"><div class="reader-first-pass-label">Read this first</div><h2>Mathematical statement</h2><p class="reader-claim">At every observation y, R_y is a probability measure and the identity map belongs to L2(R_y). For every direction v, DU(y)[v]=inner(y-mean(R_y),v)/eta. For every v,w, D2U(y)[v,w]=inner(v,w)/eta-covarianceBilin(R_y)(v,w)/eta^2. These are actual first and second Frechet derivatives and actual finite posterior moments; the constant positive prefactor cancels exactly.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation reader-main-equation">\[R_y(dx)=Z(y)^{-1}e^{-\|x-y\|^2/(2\eta)}\mu(dx),\quad X\in L^2(R_y),\quad DU(y)[v]=\eta^{-1}\langle y-\mathbb E_{R_y}X,v\rangle,\quad D^2U(y)[v,w]=\eta^{-1}\langle v,w\rangle-\eta^{-2}\operatorname{Cov}_{R_y}(\langle v,X\rangle,\langle w,X\rangle).\]</div><h3>Proof roadmap</h3><ol class="proof-roadmap"><li><span>1</span><strong>Use genuine dominated Gaussian derivatives</strong><p>Reuse the same canonical kernel calculus. Its positive weight has uniformly bounded first and second operator derivatives. Probability-integrable constants justify differentiating the actual normalizer twice. The existing private regularity proof now returns these proved derivative interfaces; they are not supplied hypotheses.</p></li><li><span>2</span><strong>Derive posterior probability and second moments</strong><p>Gaussian damping gives w&lt;=1 and w norm(x-y)^2&lt;=2eta. The triangle inequality yields w norm(x)^2&lt;=2 norm(y)^2+4eta. Both dominating constants are integrable against the probability input. Positive normalizer and the actual tilted integrability identity derive posterior probability and identity L2, even when the input has no moments.</p></li><li><span>3</span><strong>Identify normalized derivative moments</strong><p>Insert the exact quadratic tilt into the Bochner integral and divide by the positive Gaussian normalizer. Continuous-linear-map evaluation commutes with integrable derivative integrals. The explicit kernel formulas produce actual posterior first and second uncentered moments; splitting the second derivative uses integrability, rather than undefined total integrals.</p></li><li><span>4</span><strong>Differentiate the negative log exactly</strong><p>The positive normalizer justifies log differentiation and differentiating its reciprocal. Apply the product rule to the actual first derivative map. Evaluating the resulting second derivative gives the exact quotient, including its sign and eta powers.</p></li><li><span>5</span><strong>Center the actual finite moments</strong><p>Posterior L2 implies an actual Bochner mean and L2 of every displacement projection. Integral linearity identifies the expected displacement with y minus the mean. The true L2 covariance identity subtracts the product of means; translation and two signs identify displacement covariance with covarianceBilin(R_y). Substitute the normalized moments in the log quotient.</p></li><li><span>6</span><strong>Keep the positive prefactor and source convention</strong><p>For C&gt;0 and Z&gt;0, -log(CZ)=-log Z-log C. This fixed constant has zero derivatives. The genuine source Test uses the existing exact U_eta=V_eta+log Z_V, so the first and second directional identities apply to the source unnormalized V_eta and its same backward Gaussian kernel. The identities give no quantitative covariance inequality or Hessian curvature estimate.</p></li></ol></section><details class="reader-ledger assumption-ledger"><summary>Full assumptions and notation</summary><h3>All objects and hypotheses</h3><ul><li>E is a finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel measurable space, including dimension zero. mu is an arbitrary probability measure; eta&gt;0 and C&gt;0 are real numbers. No input density, finite input moments, input potential, smoothness, curvature, normalizer or conditional-version certificate is assumed.</li><li>Z(y)=integral exp(-norm(y-x)^2/(2eta))dmu(x), R_y=mu.tilted(-norm(x-y)^2/(2eta)), U(y)=-log(C Z(y)). C is an arbitrary positive constant prefactor; choosing the actual Gaussian C_eta gives its negative-log probability density. Other C values are not asserted to normalize a density.</li><li>R_y is the actual everywhere-selected posterior of the Gaussian augmentation, previously identified as a measurable Markov/disintegration kernel. Probability and finite second moments are derived here; mean is the Bochner integral, covarianceBilin is the true centered bilinear covariance. The new theorem concerns this selected version, never an arbitrary conditional representative.</li><li>For the source Gibbs input, the actual source unnormalized potential V_eta differs from the normalized density potential U_eta by the already-proved constant log Z_V. The genuine Test derives original Gibbs probability from C2 and positive lower Hessian, proves source V_eta directional derivative/covariance identities by this exact constant, and joins the same augmentation backward kernel. Source Hessian upper bound and eta upper cap are unnecessary for these identities.</li></ul><p class="muted"></p></details><h2>Mathematical proof</h2><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>1. Use genuine dominated Gaussian derivatives</h4><p>Reuse the same canonical kernel calculus. Its positive weight has uniformly bounded first and second operator derivatives. Probability-integrable constants justify differentiating the actual normalizer twice. The existing private regularity proof now returns these proved derivative interfaces; they are not supplied hypotheses.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[Dw_y[v]=-\eta^{-1}w_y\langle y-x,v\rangle,\quad D^2w_y[v,u]=-\eta^{-1}w_y\langle v,u\rangle+\eta^{-2}w_y\langle y-x,v\rangle\langle y-x,u\rangle,\quad\|Dw_y\|\le(1+2\eta)/\eta,\ \|D^2w_y\|\le3/\eta.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>gaussianNormalizerRegularity; gaussianDeriv; gaussianFirstDeriv; hasFDerivAt_integral_of_dominated_of_fderiv_le; normalizerDerivatives</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>2. Derive posterior probability and second moments</h4><p>Gaussian damping gives w&lt;=1 and w norm(x-y)^2&lt;=2eta. The triangle inequality yields w norm(x)^2&lt;=2 norm(y)^2+4eta. Both dominating constants are integrable against the probability input. Positive normalizer and the actual tilted integrability identity derive posterior probability and identity L2, even when the input has no moments.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[0&lt;w\le1,\quad w\|x-y\|^2\le2\eta,\quad w\|x\|^2\le2\|y\|^2+4\eta\ \Longrightarrow\ R_y\text{ probability},\quad X\in L^2(R_y).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>posteriorMoment; Real.mul_exp_neg_le_exp_neg_one; isProbabilityMeasure_tilted; integrable_tilted_iff; memLp_two_iff_integrable_sq_norm</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>3. Identify normalized derivative moments</h4><p>Insert the exact quadratic tilt into the Bochner integral and divide by the positive Gaussian normalizer. Continuous-linear-map evaluation commutes with integrable derivative integrals. The explicit kernel formulas produce actual posterior first and second uncentered moments; splitting the second derivative uses integrability, rather than undefined total integrals.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[DZ(y)[v]/Z(y)=-\eta^{-1}\mathbb E_{R_y}\langle y-X,v\rangle,\quad D^2Z(y)[v,w]/Z(y)=-\eta^{-1}\langle v,w\rangle+\eta^{-2}\mathbb E_{R_y}[\langle y-X,v\rangle\langle y-X,w\rangle].\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>posteriorIntegral; normalizedFirst; normalizedSecond; integral_tilted; integral_div; ContinuousLinearMap.integral_apply; Integrable.apply_continuousLinearMap; integral_add</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>4. Differentiate the negative log exactly</h4><p>The positive normalizer justifies log differentiation and differentiating its reciprocal. Apply the product rule to the actual first derivative map. Evaluating the resulting second derivative gives the exact quotient, including its sign and eta powers.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[D(-\log Z)[v]=-DZ[v]/Z,\quad D^2(-\log Z)[v,w]=DZ[v]DZ[w]/Z^2-D^2Z[v,w]/Z.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>rawFirst; rawSecond; HasFDerivAt.log; hasDerivAt_inv; HasDerivAt.comp_hasFDerivAt; HasFDerivAt.smul; HasFDerivAt.neg</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>5. Center the actual finite moments</h4><p>Posterior L2 implies an actual Bochner mean and L2 of every displacement projection. Integral linearity identifies the expected displacement with y minus the mean. The true L2 covariance identity subtracts the product of means; translation and two signs identify displacement covariance with covarianceBilin(R_y). Substitute the normalized moments in the log quotient.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\mathbb E\langle y-X,v\rangle=\langle y-\mathbb EX,v\rangle,\quad\operatorname{Cov}(y-X)=\operatorname{Cov}(X),\quad D^2(-\log Z)=\eta^{-1}I-\eta^{-2}\operatorname{Cov}(R_y).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>MemLp.integrable; MemLp.inner_const; integral_inner; integral_sub; covariance_eq_sub; covariance_const_sub_left/right; covarianceBilin_apply_eq_cov</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>6. Keep the positive prefactor and source convention</h4><p>For C&gt;0 and Z&gt;0, -log(CZ)=-log Z-log C. This fixed constant has zero derivatives. The genuine source Test uses the existing exact U_eta=V_eta+log Z_V, so the first and second directional identities apply to the source unnormalized V_eta and its same backward Gaussian kernel. The identities give no quantitative covariance inequality or Hessian curvature estimate.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[U=-\log Z-\log C,\quad U_\eta=V_\eta+\log Z_V\ \Longrightarrow\ DU_\eta=DV_\eta,\quad D^2U_\eta=D^2V_\eta.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>Real.log_mul; fderiv_sub_const; Tests.GaussianConvolutionDerivatives.actual_source_score_covariance; SmoothedGibbsPotential.smoothed_gibbs_potential; GaussianConditionalKernel.exists_tilted_isCondKernel</p></details></div><section class="lean-verification"><h2>Lean verification</h2><p>The exact proposition and proof begin folded so the mathematical argument remains the primary reading path.</p><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-statement" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.GaussianConvolutionRegularity.gaussian_convolution_derivatives"><summary>Lean statement · gaussian_convolution_derivatives</summary><p>Actual selected posterior probability/L2 and all-direction first/second Frechet derivative identities using actual mean and covariance.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem gaussian_convolution_derivatives (μ : Measure E) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ] {η C : ℝ} (hη : 0 &lt; η)
    (hC : 0 &lt; C) :
    let R := fun y : E =&gt; μ.tilted (fun x =&gt; -‖x-y‖^2/(2*η))
    let U := fun y : E =&gt; -Real.log (C*∫ x, Real.exp (-‖y-x‖^2/(2*η)) ∂μ)
    (∀ y, IsProbabilityMeasure (R y) ∧ MemLp (fun x : E =&gt; x) 2 (R y)) ∧
    (∀ y v, fderiv ℝ U y v = inner ℝ (y-∫ x, x ∂(R y)) v/η) ∧
    ∀ y v w, fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ U) y v w =
      inner ℝ v w/η - covarianceBilin (R y) v w/η^2</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-measure-gaussianconvolutionregularity.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-proof" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.GaussianConvolutionRegularity.gaussian_convolution_derivatives"><summary>Lean proof · gaussian_convolution_derivatives</summary><p>Canonical dominated Gaussian derivative integrals, true posterior L2, exact tilted normalized moments, log/reciprocal product derivative, actual centered covariance and positive constant cancellation.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem gaussian_convolution_derivatives (μ : Measure E) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ] {η C : ℝ} (hη : 0 &lt; η)
    (hC : 0 &lt; C) :
    let R := fun y : E =&gt; μ.tilted (fun x =&gt; -‖x-y‖^2/(2*η))
    let U := fun y : E =&gt; -Real.log (C*∫ x, Real.exp (-‖y-x‖^2/(2*η)) ∂μ)
    (∀ y, IsProbabilityMeasure (R y) ∧ MemLp (fun x : E =&gt; x) 2 (R y)) ∧
    (∀ y v, fderiv ℝ U y v = inner ℝ (y-∫ x, x ∂(R y)) v/η) ∧
    ∀ y v w, fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ U) y v w =
      inner ℝ v w/η - covarianceBilin (R y) v w/η^2 := by
  let Z := fun y : E =&gt; ∫ x, gaussianWeight η y x ∂μ
  let D := fun y : E =&gt; ∫ x, gaussianFirst η y x ∂μ
  let H := fun y : E =&gt; ∫ x, gaussianSecond η y x ∂μ
  let R := fun y : E =&gt; μ.tilted (fun x =&gt; -‖x-y‖^2/(2*η))
  let U0 := fun y : E =&gt; -Real.log (Z y)
  have hU : (fun y : E =&gt; -Real.log (C*Z y)) = fun y =&gt; U0 y-Real.log C := by
    funext y
    have hZ := (normalizerDerivatives μ hη).1 y
    rw [Real.log_mul hC.ne&#x27; hZ.ne&#x27;]
    dsimp only [U0]
    ring
  have hDU : fderiv ℝ (fun y : E =&gt; -Real.log (C*Z y)) = fderiv ℝ U0 := by
    rw [hU]
    funext y
    exact fderiv_sub_const _
  refine ⟨fun y =&gt; posteriorMoment μ hη y, ?_, ?_⟩
  · intro y v
    change fderiv ℝ (fun y : E =&gt; -Real.log (C*Z y)) y v = _
    rw [hDU]
    obtain ⟨hR,hLp⟩ := posteriorMoment μ hη y
    haveI : IsProbabilityMeasure (R y) := hR
    have hi : Integrable (fun x : E =&gt; x) (R y) := hLp.integrable (by norm_num)
    have hm : (∫ x, inner ℝ (y-x) v ∂(R y)) = inner ℝ (y-∫ x, x ∂(R y)) v := by
      have he : (fun x : E =&gt; inner ℝ (y-x) v) = fun x =&gt; inner ℝ v (y-x) := by
        funext x
        exact real_inner_comm _ _
      rw [he]
      have hmean : (∫ x, inner ℝ v (y-x) ∂(R y)) =
          inner ℝ v (∫ x, y-x ∂(R y)) := by
        simpa only [Pi.sub_apply] using
          (integral_inner (𝕜 := ℝ) ((integrable_const y).sub hi) v)
      have hdiff : (∫ x, y-x ∂(R y)) = y-∫ x, x ∂(R y) := by
        simpa only [Pi.sub_apply, integral_const, probReal_univ, one_smul] using
          integral_sub (integrable_const y) hi
      rw [hmean,hdiff]
      exact real_inner_comm _ _
    calc
      _ = -(D y v/Z y) := by simpa only [neg_div] using rawFirst μ hη y v
      _ = (∫ x, inner ℝ (y-x) v ∂(R y))/η := by
        rw [normalizedFirst μ hη y v]; ring
      _ = _ := by rw [hm]
  · intro y v w
    change fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ (fun y : E =&gt; -Real.log (C*Z y))) y v w = _
    rw [hDU]
    obtain ⟨hR,hLp⟩ := posteriorMoment μ hη y
    haveI : IsProbabilityMeasure (R y) := hR
    have hi : Integrable (fun x : E =&gt; x) (R y) := hLp.integrable (by norm_num)
    have hξ : MemLp (fun x : E =&gt; y-x) 2 (R y) := (memLp_const y).sub hLp
    have hv := hξ.inner_const (𝕜 := ℝ) v
    have hw := hξ.inner_const (𝕜 := ℝ) w
    have hc : covarianceBilin (R y) v w =
        (∫ x, inner ℝ (y-x) v*inner ℝ (y-x) w ∂(R y)) -
          (∫ x, inner ℝ (y-x) v ∂(R y))*(∫ x, inner ℝ (y-x) w ∂(R y)) := by
      have hcenter : covariance (fun x =&gt; inner ℝ (y-x) v)
          (fun x =&gt; inner ℝ (y-x) w) (R y) = covarianceBilin (R y) v w := by
        simp_rw [inner_sub_left]
        rw [covariance_const_sub_left (hi.inner_const (𝕜 := ℝ) v),
          covariance_const_sub_right (hi.inner_const (𝕜 := ℝ) w), neg_neg]
        rw [covarianceBilin_apply_eq_cov hLp]
        simp_rw [real_inner_comm v, real_inner_comm w]
        rfl
      exact hcenter.symm.trans (by simpa only [Pi.mul_apply] using covariance_eq_sub hv hw)
    rw [rawSecond μ hη y v w]
    change D y v*D y w/Z y^2 - H y v w/Z y = _
    rw [show D y v*D y w/Z y^2 = (D y v/Z y)*(D y w/Z y) by ring]
    rw [normalizedFirst μ hη y v, normalizedFirst μ hη y w,
      normalizedSecond μ hη y v w, hc]
    ring

end
end AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.GaussianConvolutionRegularity</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-measure-gaussianconvolutionregularity.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details></section><section data-source-comparison="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.GaussianConvolutionRegularity.gaussian_convolution_derivatives"><h3>Source assumptions versus formal assumptions</h3><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Actual Lean</th><th>Difference kind</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Section4.1 equation4.1 uses the actual Gibbs posterior and source unnormalized V_eta.</td><td>E is a finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel measurable space, including dimension zero. mu is an arbitrary probability measure; eta&gt;0 and C&gt;0 are real numbers. No input density, finite input moments, input potential, smoothness, curvature, normalizer or conditional-version certificate is assumed.
Z(y)=integral exp(-norm(y-x)^2/(2eta))dmu(x), R_y=mu.tilted(-norm(x-y)^2/(2eta)), U(y)=-log(C Z(y)). C is an arbitrary positive constant prefactor; choosing the actual Gaussian C_eta gives its negative-log probability density. Other C values are not asserted to normalize a density.
R_y is the actual everywhere-selected posterior of the Gaussian augmentation, previously identified as a measurable Markov/disintegration kernel. Probability and finite second moments are derived here; mean is the Bochner integral, covarianceBilin is the true centered bilinear covariance. The new theorem concerns this selected version, never an arbitrary conditional representative.
For the source Gibbs input, the actual source unnormalized potential V_eta differs from the normalized density potential U_eta by the already-proved constant log Z_V. The genuine Test derives original Gibbs probability from C2 and positive lower Hessian, proves source V_eta directional derivative/covariance identities by this exact constant, and joins the same augmentation backward kernel. Source Hessian upper bound and eta upper cap are unnecessary for these identities.</td><td>generalization</td><td>Arbitrary probability input includes the derived source Gibbs law. Every selected posterior moment and derivative is proved; no moment/density/regularity premise is added. The source Test explicitly transports the exact potential constant. Positive C is only a constant-prefactor generalization, not an arbitrary probability-density certificate.</td></tr><tr><td>Equation4.1 states gradient and Hessian matrix identities.</td><td>At every observation y, R_y is a probability measure and the identity map belongs to L2(R_y). For every direction v, DU(y)[v]=inner(y-mean(R_y),v)/eta. For every v,w, D2U(y)[v,w]=inner(v,w)/eta-covarianceBilin(R_y)(v,w)/eta^2. These are actual first and second Frechet derivatives and actual finite posterior moments; the constant positive prefactor cancels exactly.</td><td>same</td><td>The real Hilbert directional first derivative for every v and bilinear second derivative for every v,w are exactly the gradient/Hessian representation in the induced inner product. covarianceBilin is genuine L2 centered covariance.</td></tr><tr><td>Lemma4.1 obtains quantitative curvature using Brascamp-Lieb and Cramer-Rao.</td><td>No covariance inequalities or quantitative source Hessian bounds are asserted.</td><td>unresolved</td><td>Actual everywhere-selected quadratic Gaussian posterior has probability and finite second moments, without input moments; true first/second derivatives of negative log of the Gaussian normalizer equal posterior mean and covariance, including the positive constant prefactor and dimension zero. No Brascamp-Lieb/Cramer-Rao covariance inequalities, source Hessian bounds/higher cumulants, stationarity/algorithm accuracy/history/expected work, PBPS process or either complete main/composition. TV never transfers unbounded expected cost.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Actual everywhere-selected quadratic Gaussian posterior has probability and finite second moments, without input moments; true first/second derivatives of negative log of the Gaussian normalizer equal posterior mean and covariance, including the positive constant prefactor and dimension zero. No Brascamp-Lieb/Cramer-Rao covariance inequalities, source Hessian bounds/higher cumulants, stationarity/algorithm accuracy/history/expected work, PBPS process or either complete main/composition. TV never transfers unbounded expected cost.</p><p><strong>Encoder–denoiser:</strong> accepted · equivalent-after-elaboration</p><p>A generalization is not a source correction. Proposed missing conditions require separate independent repair review. No proposed repair silently changes the original theorem.</p></section><details class="reader-ledger boundary-ledger"><summary>Scope and omitted-condition boundaries</summary><ul><li>Actual everywhere-selected quadratic Gaussian posterior has probability and finite second moments, without input moments; true first/second derivatives of negative log of the Gaussian normalizer equal posterior mean and covariance, including the positive constant prefactor and dimension zero. No Brascamp-Lieb/Cramer-Rao covariance inequalities, source Hessian bounds/higher cumulants, stationarity/algorithm accuracy/history/expected work, PBPS process or either complete main/composition. TV never transfers unbounded expected cost.</li></ul></details><details class="proof-reader-provenance reader-ledger"><summary>Sources, dependencies, and reuse</summary><h2>Source and reuse</h2><h3>Samplinglib parents called</h3><ul></ul><h3>Mathlib API called (external library)</h3><ul><li>hasFDerivAt_integral_of_dominated_of_fderiv_le</li><li>integral_tilted</li><li>integrable_tilted_iff</li><li>isProbabilityMeasure_tilted</li><li>memLp_two_iff_integrable_sq_norm</li><li>ContinuousLinearMap.integral_apply</li><li>Integrable.apply_continuousLinearMap</li><li>HasFDerivAt.log</li><li>hasDerivAt_inv</li><li>HasDerivAt.comp_hasFDerivAt</li><li>integral_inner</li><li>covariance_eq_sub</li><li>covarianceBilin_apply_eq_cov</li><li>Real.log_mul</li><li>fderiv_sub_const</li></ul><h3>Mathematical sources</h3><ul><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.SS1">SPHMC equation4.1 actual posterior score/covariance</a> — Actual everywhere-selected quadratic Gaussian posterior has probability and finite second moments, without input moments; true first/second derivatives of negative log of the Gaussian normalizer equal posterior mean and covariance, including the positive constant prefactor and dimension zero. No Brascamp-Lieb/Cramer-Rao covariance inequalities, source Hessian bounds/higher cumulants, stationarity/algorithm accuracy/history/expected work, PBPS process or either complete main/composition. TV never transfers unbounded expected cost.</li></ul><p>Samplinglib prose is not a quotation or a source-equivalence certificate. Definitions and aliases are explained as constructions, not counted as new mathematical proofs.</p></details></article><h3>Which proof obligations and prerequisites are covered?</h3><ul><li><span class="status status-orange">Formalized proof edge; source adapter/review separate</span> Equation4.1 actual posterior score/covariance, with finite moments and source normalization</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Brascamp-Lieb/Cramer-Rao and source Hessian bounds (remaining)</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Higher regularity, dynamics, errors/work and both complete main/composition (remaining)</li></ul></section><section id="gaussian-convolution-regularity" data-publication-item="gaussian-convolution-regularity"><h2>Actual Gaussian convolution density and C2 normalized potential</h2><p>Fan Chen, Sinho Chewi, Jianfeng Lu, Matthew S. Zhang; ASTIS explicit smoothing-regularity prerequisite, not full equation4.1 or Lemma4.1.</p><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S3.SS1">arXiv:2609.06906v1 / 2609.06905v1 · Section3.1 smoothing definition (HTML232-240); Section4.1 setup (HTML403-412), analytic prerequisite to equation4.1/Lemma4.1; PBPS2609.06905v1 Section2.2 actual augmentation marginal</a> · faithful paraphrase</p><h3>Complete source statement (Samplinglib restatement)</h3><p>The actual Gaussian add-noise probability law equals volume.withDensity(ofReal(C_eta Z_eta)). Its density is strictly positive at every y, Z_eta is globally C2, and the exact negative-log density U_eta=-log(C_eta Z_eta) is globally C2. The input law may be singular and may lack moments.</p><ul><li>E is a finite-dimensional real inner-product space equipped with its Borel measurable space, including dimension zero. eta is a strictly positive real. mu is an arbitrary probability measure on E, possibly singular and without finite moments.</li><li>C_eta=((sqrt(2*pi*eta))^-1)^dim E and Z_eta(y)=integral exp(-norm(y-x)^2/(2*eta)) dmu(x). The actual smoothed law is the measurable pushforward of mu×standard Gaussian by (x,g)-&gt;x+sqrt(eta)g. The potential is exactly -log(C_eta Z_eta), not Gaussian averaging of an input potential.</li><li>All first/second Gaussian derivative domination, Bochner integrability, measurability and strictly positive normalizers are derived. The local CLM dual instances choose existing canonical normed structures and add no mathematical premises. No input density, moment, curvature or supplied regularity certificate is assumed.</li><li>Source Section3.1 defines the unnormalized convolution exp(-V_eta)=exp(-V)*Gaussian. This theorem constructs the exact normalized density potential U_eta=-log(C_eta Z_mu). For Gibbs mu=exp(-V) volume/Z_V, U_eta=V_eta+log Z_V; formalizing that Gibbs specialization and constant-shift adapter remains separate. This normalization difference does not change derivatives, but the two chosen potentials are not asserted literally identical.</li></ul><h3>Actual smoothing and negative-log density</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\pi_\eta=(x+\sqrt\eta\,g)_\#(\mu\otimes\gamma)=C_\eta Z_\eta\,dx,\quad Z_\eta(y)=\int e^{-\|y-x\|^2/(2\eta)}\mu(dx)&gt;0,\quad Z_\eta,U_\eta=-\log(C_\eta Z_\eta)\in C^2.\]</div><h2>Read the formalized proofs</h2><p>Each statement and proof below has its own closed Lean disclosure. Samplinglib parents, Mathlib calls and external mathematical sources are distinguished in each proof.</p><article class="proof-reader" data-authored-declaration="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.GaussianConvolutionRegularity.gaussian_convolution_potential_c2"><div class="eyebrow">Samplinglib mathematical lesson</div><h2>Actual Gaussian convolution density and C2 normalized potential</h2><p><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.GaussianConvolutionRegularity.gaussian_convolution_potential_c2</code> · theorem · <a href="../../../lessons/index.html">Teaching coverage</a></p><section class="reader-first-pass" aria-label="First reading"><div class="reader-first-pass-label">Read this first</div><h2>Mathematical statement</h2><p class="reader-claim">The actual Gaussian add-noise probability law equals volume.withDensity(ofReal(C_eta Z_eta)). Its density is strictly positive at every y, Z_eta is globally C2, and the exact negative-log density U_eta=-log(C_eta Z_eta) is globally C2. The input law may be singular and may lack moments.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation reader-main-equation">\[\pi_\eta=(x+\sqrt\eta\,g)_\#(\mu\otimes\gamma)=C_\eta Z_\eta\,dx,\quad Z_\eta(y)=\int e^{-\|y-x\|^2/(2\eta)}\mu(dx)&gt;0,\quad Z_\eta,U_\eta=-\log(C_\eta Z_\eta)\in C^2.\]</div><h3>Proof roadmap</h3><ol class="proof-roadmap"><li><span>1</span><strong>Differentiate the actual Gaussian weight</strong><p>For u=y-x, w=exp(-norm(u)^2/(2eta)), differentiate the squared norm and exponential as Frechet maps. D_yw[v]=-eta^-1 w inner(u,v), and D_y²w[v,t]=w(eta^-2 inner(u,v)inner(u,t)-eta^-1 inner(v,t)). These are the explicit kernel derivatives, not derivatives of an assumed regularized potential.</p></li><li><span>2</span><strong>Derive probability-integrable domination</strong><p>The Gaussian weight lies in (0,1]. With q=norm(u)^2/(2eta), q exp(-q)&lt;=exp(-1)&lt;=1 gives w norm(u)^2&lt;=2eta. Since norm(u)&lt;=1+norm(u)^2, the first derivative norm is at most (1+2eta)/eta. The second derivative operator norm is at most w norm(u)^2/eta²+w/eta&lt;=3/eta. These constants are uniform in x and y, so the probability input integrates them without any moment condition.</p></li><li><span>3</span><strong>Differentiate the actual normalizer twice</strong><p>Continuity of the displayed derivatives gives their Borel/strong measurability. Apply the dominated parametric-integral Frechet theorem first to w and then to Dw. Dominated continuity of the integral of D²w makes the first derivative C1, hence Z is C2. Probability mass one and everywhere w&gt;0 imply Z(y)&gt;0 at every observation, not merely almost everywhere.</p></li><li><span>4</span><strong>Identify the actual smoothed density</strong><p>Project the existing genuine GaussianAugmentation joint density to its observation coordinate. For any measurable nonnegative test, the pushforward integral and nonnegative Fubini put the x-integral inside the y-integral. The established Bochner integrability identifies integral ofReal(C_eta w) with ofReal(C_eta Z). Equality against all such tests proves the exact volume density of the actual Gaussian add-noise law.</p></li><li><span>5</span><strong>Form the exact normalized potential and expose the source convention</strong><p>The finite-dimensional Gaussian prefactor C_eta is strictly positive for eta&gt;0, including dimension zero. Compose the C2 positive function C_eta Z with log and negate. This yields C2 of the actual normalized negative-log density, with its normalization constant retained. Source Hessian bounds require additional posterior covariance inequalities and are not inferred from C2 regularity. Source Section3.1 defines the unnormalized convolution exp(-V_eta)=exp(-V)*Gaussian. This theorem constructs the exact normalized density potential U_eta=-log(C_eta Z_mu). For Gibbs mu=exp(-V) volume/Z_V, U_eta=V_eta+log Z_V; formalizing that Gibbs specialization and constant-shift adapter remains separate. This normalization difference does not change derivatives, but the two chosen potentials are not asserted literally identical.</p></li></ol></section><details class="reader-ledger assumption-ledger"><summary>Full assumptions and notation</summary><h3>All objects and hypotheses</h3><ul><li>E is a finite-dimensional real inner-product space equipped with its Borel measurable space, including dimension zero. eta is a strictly positive real. mu is an arbitrary probability measure on E, possibly singular and without finite moments.</li><li>C_eta=((sqrt(2*pi*eta))^-1)^dim E and Z_eta(y)=integral exp(-norm(y-x)^2/(2*eta)) dmu(x). The actual smoothed law is the measurable pushforward of mu×standard Gaussian by (x,g)-&gt;x+sqrt(eta)g. The potential is exactly -log(C_eta Z_eta), not Gaussian averaging of an input potential.</li><li>All first/second Gaussian derivative domination, Bochner integrability, measurability and strictly positive normalizers are derived. The local CLM dual instances choose existing canonical normed structures and add no mathematical premises. No input density, moment, curvature or supplied regularity certificate is assumed.</li><li>Source Section3.1 defines the unnormalized convolution exp(-V_eta)=exp(-V)*Gaussian. This theorem constructs the exact normalized density potential U_eta=-log(C_eta Z_mu). For Gibbs mu=exp(-V) volume/Z_V, U_eta=V_eta+log Z_V; formalizing that Gibbs specialization and constant-shift adapter remains separate. This normalization difference does not change derivatives, but the two chosen potentials are not asserted literally identical.</li></ul><p class="muted"></p></details><h2>Mathematical proof</h2><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>1. Differentiate the actual Gaussian weight</h4><p>For u=y-x, w=exp(-norm(u)^2/(2eta)), differentiate the squared norm and exponential as Frechet maps. D_yw[v]=-eta^-1 w inner(u,v), and D_y²w[v,t]=w(eta^-2 inner(u,v)inner(u,t)-eta^-1 inner(v,t)). These are the explicit kernel derivatives, not derivatives of an assumed regularized potential.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[Dw=-\eta^{-1}w\,u^\flat,\qquad D^2w=w(\eta^{-2}u^\flat\otimes u^\flat-\eta^{-1}I).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>gaussianDeriv; gaussianFirstDeriv; realDual; innerSL</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>2. Derive probability-integrable domination</h4><p>The Gaussian weight lies in (0,1]. With q=norm(u)^2/(2eta), q exp(-q)&lt;=exp(-1)&lt;=1 gives w norm(u)^2&lt;=2eta. Since norm(u)&lt;=1+norm(u)^2, the first derivative norm is at most (1+2eta)/eta. The second derivative operator norm is at most w norm(u)^2/eta²+w/eta&lt;=3/eta. These constants are uniform in x and y, so the probability input integrates them without any moment condition.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[0&lt;w\le1,\quad w\|u\|^2\le2\eta,\quad\|Dw\|\le(1+2\eta)/\eta,\quad\|D^2w\|\le3/\eta.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>weight_bounds; first_bound; second_bound; integrable_const</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>3. Differentiate the actual normalizer twice</h4><p>Continuity of the displayed derivatives gives their Borel/strong measurability. Apply the dominated parametric-integral Frechet theorem first to w and then to Dw. Dominated continuity of the integral of D²w makes the first derivative C1, hence Z is C2. Probability mass one and everywhere w&gt;0 imply Z(y)&gt;0 at every observation, not merely almost everywhere.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[DZ(y)=\int Dw(y,x)\,\mu(dx),\quad D^2Z(y)=\int D^2w(y,x)\,\mu(dx),\quad Z(y)&gt;0.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>gaussianNormalizerRegularity; hdZ; hdD; hHC; hDC; hZC; integral_exp_pos; hasFDerivAt_integral_of_dominated_of_fderiv_le; continuous_of_dominated</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>4. Identify the actual smoothed density</h4><p>Project the existing genuine GaussianAugmentation joint density to its observation coordinate. For any measurable nonnegative test, the pushforward integral and nonnegative Fubini put the x-integral inside the y-integral. The established Bochner integrability identifies integral ofReal(C_eta w) with ofReal(C_eta Z). Equality against all such tests proves the exact volume density of the actual Gaussian add-noise law.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\int f\,d\pi_\eta=\int\!\int f(y)C_\eta w(y,x)\,dy\,\mu(dx)=\int f(y)C_\eta Z_\eta(y)\,dy.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>gaussianDensity; GaussianAugmentation.augmentation_eq_withDensity; Measure.ext_of_lintegral; lintegral_prod_symm; ofReal_integral_eq_lintegral_ofReal</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>5. Form the exact normalized potential and expose the source convention</h4><p>The finite-dimensional Gaussian prefactor C_eta is strictly positive for eta&gt;0, including dimension zero. Compose the C2 positive function C_eta Z with log and negate. This yields C2 of the actual normalized negative-log density, with its normalization constant retained. Source Hessian bounds require additional posterior covariance inequalities and are not inferred from C2 regularity. Source Section3.1 defines the unnormalized convolution exp(-V_eta)=exp(-V)*Gaussian. This theorem constructs the exact normalized density potential U_eta=-log(C_eta Z_mu). For Gibbs mu=exp(-V) volume/Z_V, U_eta=V_eta+log Z_V; formalizing that Gibbs specialization and constant-shift adapter remains separate. This normalization difference does not change derivatives, but the two chosen potentials are not asserted literally identical.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[U_\eta(y)=-\log(C_\eta Z_\eta(y)),\qquad C_\eta Z_\eta(y)&gt;0,\qquad U_\eta\in C^2.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>gaussian_convolution_potential_c2; hC; ContDiff.log; ContDiff.neg</p></details></div><section class="lean-verification"><h2>Lean verification</h2><p>The exact proposition and proof begin folded so the mathematical argument remains the primary reading path.</p><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-statement" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.GaussianConvolutionRegularity.gaussian_convolution_potential_c2"><summary>Lean statement · gaussian_convolution_potential_c2</summary><p>Actual Gaussian pushforward equals explicit volume density, with everywhere-positive normalizer and genuine C2 negative-log density.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem gaussian_convolution_potential_c2 (μ : Measure E) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ]
    {η : ℝ} (hη : 0 &lt; η) :
    let C := ((Real.sqrt (2*Real.pi*η))⁻¹)^Module.finrank ℝ E
    let Z := fun y : E =&gt; ∫ x, Real.exp (-‖y-x‖^2/(2*η)) ∂μ
    (μ.prod (stdGaussian E)).map (fun p =&gt; p.1+Real.sqrt η • p.2) =
      (volume : Measure E).withDensity (fun y =&gt; ENNReal.ofReal (C*Z y)) ∧
    (∀ y, 0 &lt; C*Z y) ∧ ContDiff ℝ 2 Z ∧
      ContDiff ℝ 2 (fun y =&gt; -Real.log (C*Z y))</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-measure-gaussianconvolutionregularity.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-proof" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.GaussianConvolutionRegularity.gaussian_convolution_potential_c2"><summary>Lean proof · gaussian_convolution_potential_c2</summary><p>Explicit Gaussian derivative domination, two genuine parametric integral derivatives, dominated continuity, exact augmentation marginal and positive logarithm.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem gaussian_convolution_potential_c2 (μ : Measure E) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ]
    {η : ℝ} (hη : 0 &lt; η) :
    let C := ((Real.sqrt (2*Real.pi*η))⁻¹)^Module.finrank ℝ E
    let Z := fun y : E =&gt; ∫ x, Real.exp (-‖y-x‖^2/(2*η)) ∂μ
    (μ.prod (stdGaussian E)).map (fun p =&gt; p.1+Real.sqrt η • p.2) =
      (volume : Measure E).withDensity (fun y =&gt; ENNReal.ofReal (C*Z y)) ∧
    (∀ y, 0 &lt; C*Z y) ∧ ContDiff ℝ 2 Z ∧
      ContDiff ℝ 2 (fun y =&gt; -Real.log (C*Z y)) := by
  let C := ((Real.sqrt (2*Real.pi*η))⁻¹)^Module.finrank ℝ E
  have hC : 0 &lt; C := by dsimp [C]; positivity
  obtain ⟨hZ,hZC,hV,_hdZ,_hdD⟩ := gaussianNormalizerRegularity μ hη
  exact ⟨gaussianDensity μ hη,fun y =&gt; mul_pos hC (hZ y),hZC,hV C hC⟩</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-measure-gaussianconvolutionregularity.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details></section><section data-source-comparison="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.GaussianConvolutionRegularity.gaussian_convolution_potential_c2"><h3>Source assumptions versus formal assumptions</h3><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Actual Lean</th><th>Difference kind</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Gaussian smoothing pi_eta=pi*N(0,eta I) and its negative-log potential V_eta.</td><td>E is a finite-dimensional real inner-product space equipped with its Borel measurable space, including dimension zero. eta is a strictly positive real. mu is an arbitrary probability measure on E, possibly singular and without finite moments.
C_eta=((sqrt(2*pi*eta))^-1)^dim E and Z_eta(y)=integral exp(-norm(y-x)^2/(2*eta)) dmu(x). The actual smoothed law is the measurable pushforward of mu×standard Gaussian by (x,g)-&gt;x+sqrt(eta)g. The potential is exactly -log(C_eta Z_eta), not Gaussian averaging of an input potential.
All first/second Gaussian derivative domination, Bochner integrability, measurability and strictly positive normalizers are derived. The local CLM dual instances choose existing canonical normed structures and add no mathematical premises. No input density, moment, curvature or supplied regularity certificate is assumed.</td><td>generalization</td><td>Arbitrary probability mu includes the separately certified source Gibbs pi; finite-dimensional Borel domain and eta&gt;0 are exposed. Gaussian-weighted first/second derivatives derive regularity even for singular or non-moment inputs. The prefactor is kept exactly, including d=0.</td></tr><tr><td>Source differentiates smoothing to obtain posterior score/covariance identities and Hessian bounds.</td><td>The actual Gaussian add-noise probability law equals volume.withDensity(ofReal(C_eta Z_eta)). Its density is strictly positive at every y, Z_eta is globally C2, and the exact negative-log density U_eta=-log(C_eta Z_eta) is globally C2. The input law may be singular and may lack moments.</td><td>unresolved</td><td>Closes the genuine Gaussian-smoothed density and global C2 normalizer/negative-log potential prerequisite, for arbitrary probability input and eta&gt;0. It does not yet prove the score/posterior-covariance identities (4.1), Lemma4.1 curvature via Brascamp-Lieb/Cramer-Rao, higher derivative/cumulant estimates, target invariance, numerical bias/history/expected costs, PBPS process, either complete main result or composition. TV proximity does not transfer unbounded cost.</td></tr><tr><td>exp(-V_eta)=exp(-V)*Gaussian is the source unnormalized potential convention.</td><td>Exact probability-density negative log U_eta=-log(C_eta Z_mu).</td><td>generalization</td><td>Source Section3.1 defines the unnormalized convolution exp(-V_eta)=exp(-V)*Gaussian. This theorem constructs the exact normalized density potential U_eta=-log(C_eta Z_mu). For Gibbs mu=exp(-V) volume/Z_V, U_eta=V_eta+log Z_V; formalizing that Gibbs specialization and constant-shift adapter remains separate. This normalization difference does not change derivatives, but the two chosen potentials are not asserted literally identical.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Closes the genuine Gaussian-smoothed density and global C2 normalizer/negative-log potential prerequisite, for arbitrary probability input and eta&gt;0. It does not yet prove the score/posterior-covariance identities (4.1), Lemma4.1 curvature via Brascamp-Lieb/Cramer-Rao, higher derivative/cumulant estimates, target invariance, numerical bias/history/expected costs, PBPS process, either complete main result or composition. TV proximity does not transfer unbounded cost. Source Section3.1 defines the unnormalized convolution exp(-V_eta)=exp(-V)*Gaussian. This theorem constructs the exact normalized density potential U_eta=-log(C_eta Z_mu). For Gibbs mu=exp(-V) volume/Z_V, U_eta=V_eta+log Z_V; formalizing that Gibbs specialization and constant-shift adapter remains separate. This normalization difference does not change derivatives, but the two chosen potentials are not asserted literally identical.</p><p><strong>Encoder–denoiser:</strong> accepted · equivalent-after-elaboration</p><p>A generalization is not a source correction. Proposed missing conditions require separate independent repair review. No proposed repair silently changes the original theorem.</p></section><details class="reader-ledger boundary-ledger"><summary>Scope and omitted-condition boundaries</summary><ul><li>Closes the genuine Gaussian-smoothed density and global C2 normalizer/negative-log potential prerequisite, for arbitrary probability input and eta&gt;0. It does not yet prove the score/posterior-covariance identities (4.1), Lemma4.1 curvature via Brascamp-Lieb/Cramer-Rao, higher derivative/cumulant estimates, target invariance, numerical bias/history/expected costs, PBPS process, either complete main result or composition. TV proximity does not transfer unbounded cost. Source Section3.1 defines the unnormalized convolution exp(-V_eta)=exp(-V)*Gaussian. This theorem constructs the exact normalized density potential U_eta=-log(C_eta Z_mu). For Gibbs mu=exp(-V) volume/Z_V, U_eta=V_eta+log Z_V; formalizing that Gibbs specialization and constant-shift adapter remains separate. This normalization difference does not change derivatives, but the two chosen potentials are not asserted literally identical.</li></ul></details><details class="proof-reader-provenance reader-ledger"><summary>Sources, dependencies, and reuse</summary><h2>Source and reuse</h2><h3>Samplinglib parents called</h3><ul><li><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-examplecases-proximalbps-gaussianaugmentation.html#decl-autosamplingtheory-examplecases-proximalbps-gaussianaugmentation-augment-3eada9025c"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.ProximalBPS.GaussianAugmentation.augmentation_eq_withDensity</code></a></li></ul><h3>Mathlib API called (external library)</h3><ul><li>hasFDerivAt_integral_of_dominated_of_fderiv_le</li><li>continuous_of_dominated</li><li>ContDiff.log</li><li>contDiff_succ_iff_fderiv</li><li>contDiff_one_iff_fderiv</li><li>Real.mul_exp_neg_le_exp_neg_one</li><li>integral_exp_pos</li><li>Measure.ext_of_lintegral</li><li>lintegral_map</li><li>lintegral_prod_symm</li><li>lintegral_withDensity_eq_lintegral_mul</li><li>ofReal_integral_eq_lintegral_ofReal</li><li>ContinuousLinearMap.toNormedAddCommGroup</li><li>ContinuousLinearMap.toNormedSpace</li></ul><h3>Mathematical sources</h3><ul><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S3.SS1">SPHMC actual Gaussian smoothing analytic prerequisite</a> — Closes the genuine Gaussian-smoothed density and global C2 normalizer/negative-log potential prerequisite, for arbitrary probability input and eta&gt;0. It does not yet prove the score/posterior-covariance identities (4.1), Lemma4.1 curvature via Brascamp-Lieb/Cramer-Rao, higher derivative/cumulant estimates, target invariance, numerical bias/history/expected costs, PBPS process, either complete main result or composition. TV proximity does not transfer unbounded cost.</li></ul><p>Samplinglib prose is not a quotation or a source-equivalence certificate. Definitions and aliases are explained as constructions, not counted as new mathematical proofs.</p></details></article><h3>Which proof obligations and prerequisites are covered?</h3><ul><li><span class="status status-green">Independently verified prerequisite</span> Actual Gaussian-smoothed positive density and C2 negative-log potential</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Score/covariance identities and posterior curvature inequalities (remaining)</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Higher regularity, dynamics, accuracy/work and both complete main/composition results (remaining)</li></ul></section><section id="gaussian-lipschitz-exponential" data-publication-item="gaussian-lipschitz-exponential"><h2>Gaussian Lipschitz first moment and signed exponential domain</h2><p>ASTIS authored domain lemma using pinned Mathlib Fernique. This leaf supplies exponential L1; the distinct current standard-Gaussian concentration theorem supplies the sharp bound locally.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/db584cd6d46c92f209a44c0f1c829460d327499d/Mathlib/Probability/Distributions/Gaussian/Fernique.lean">Mathlib db584cd6; SPHMC arXiv2609.06906v1 domain consumer · SPHMC arXiv2609.06906v1 S3.E2 actualGaussian-gradient evaluator; S4.E3 centered directional signed exponential; S2.E1/S4.Ex3 actualproximal; inherited S1.E1 C2 Hessian and S3.SS1.p1.1 eta&gt;0. Domain prerequisites only; the separate current sharp theorem supplies the scoped coefficient.</a> · faithful paraphrase</p><h3>Complete source statement (Samplinglib restatement)</h3><p>For every actual Gaussian mu and globally L-Lipschitz scalar f on the stated Banach/Borel space, f is Bochner integrable and exp(t*(f-int f dmu)) is integrable for every real t. This is a Gaussian-domain background theorem, not the sharp concentration bound.</p><ul><li>Real complete second-countable normed vector space E with Borel measurable structure, Gaussian probability measure mu (possibly noncentered/degenerate).</li><li>Actual globally Lipschitz scalar function f:E-&gt;R with NNReal constant L, including L=0. No differentiability, boundedness, positive dimension, given mean, Laplace certificate or sharp coefficient premise.</li></ul><h3>Gaussian Lipschitz first moment and signed exponential domain</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[f\in L^1(\mu),\qquad e^{t(f-\int f\,d\mu)}\in L^1(\mu)\quad(t\in\mathbb R).\]</div><h2>Read the formalized proofs</h2><p>Each statement and proof below has its own closed Lean disclosure. Samplinglib parents, Mathlib calls and external mathematical sources are distinguished in each proof.</p><article class="proof-reader" data-authored-declaration="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integrable_and_integrable_exp_centered_of_lipschitz"><div class="eyebrow">Samplinglib mathematical lesson</div><h2>Gaussian Lipschitz first moment and signed exponential domain</h2><p><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integrable_and_integrable_exp_centered_of_lipschitz</code> · theorem · <a href="../../../lessons/index.html">Teaching coverage</a></p><section class="reader-first-pass" aria-label="First reading"><div class="reader-first-pass-label">Read this first</div><h2>Mathematical statement</h2><p class="reader-claim">For every actual Gaussian mu and globally L-Lipschitz scalar f on the stated Banach/Borel space, f is Bochner integrable and exp(t*(f-int f dmu)) is integrable for every real t. This is a Gaussian-domain background theorem, not the sharp concentration bound.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation reader-main-equation">\[f\in L^1(\mu),\qquad e^{t(f-\int f\,d\mu)}\in L^1(\mu)\quad(t\in\mathbb R).\]</div><h3>Proof roadmap</h3><ol class="proof-roadmap"><li><span>1</span><strong>Produce the genuine first moment</strong><p>Lipschitz growth bounds the actual scalar observable by L times the norm plus its value at zero. Gaussian first Bochner integrability from pinned Mathlib yields norm L1. Continuity gives measurability, so domination yields scalar L1 and a genuine finite mean.</p></li><li><span>2</span><strong>Use actual Fernique integrability</strong><p>Pinned Gaussian Fernique gives one actual C&gt;0 with exp(C norm(x)^2) integrable. The space is a complete second-countable real Banach Borel space. The Gaussian need not be centered or nondegenerate; zero dimensions and zero Lipschitz constant require no extra branch or premise.</p></li><li><span>3</span><strong>Dominate every signed centered exponential</strong><p>For each real t set b=abs(t)L and D=abs(t)(abs(f(0))+abs(m)). Young inequality gives b norm(x)&lt;=C norm(x)^2+C^-1 b^2. Thus the centered exponential is dominated by a finite scalar times the genuine Fernique integrand. This proves the domain only; its constant depends on C and f(0), and supplies no sharp concentration coefficient.</p></li></ol></section><details class="reader-ledger assumption-ledger"><summary>Full assumptions and notation</summary><h3>All objects and hypotheses</h3><ul><li>Real complete second-countable normed vector space E with Borel measurable structure, Gaussian probability measure mu (possibly noncentered/degenerate).</li><li>Actual globally Lipschitz scalar function f:E-&gt;R with NNReal constant L, including L=0. No differentiability, boundedness, positive dimension, given mean, Laplace certificate or sharp coefficient premise.</li></ul><p class="muted"></p></details><h2>Mathematical proof</h2><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>1. Produce the genuine first moment</h4><p>Lipschitz growth bounds the actual scalar observable by L times the norm plus its value at zero. Gaussian first Bochner integrability from pinned Mathlib yields norm L1. Continuity gives measurability, so domination yields scalar L1 and a genuine finite mean.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[|f(x)|\le L\|x\|+|f(0)|,\quad f\in L^1(\mu),\quad m=\int f\,d\mu.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>IsGaussian.integrable_id; LipschitzWith.dist_le_mul; Integrable.mono&#x27;</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>2. Use actual Fernique integrability</h4><p>Pinned Gaussian Fernique gives one actual C&gt;0 with exp(C norm(x)^2) integrable. The space is a complete second-countable real Banach Borel space. The Gaussian need not be centered or nondegenerate; zero dimensions and zero Lipschitz constant require no extra branch or premise.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\exists C&gt;0:\quad\int e^{C\|x\|^2}\,d\mu(x)&lt;\infty.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>ProbabilityTheory.IsGaussian.exists_integrable_exp_sq</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>3. Dominate every signed centered exponential</h4><p>For each real t set b=abs(t)L and D=abs(t)(abs(f(0))+abs(m)). Young inequality gives b norm(x)&lt;=C norm(x)^2+C^-1 b^2. Thus the centered exponential is dominated by a finite scalar times the genuine Fernique integrand. This proves the domain only; its constant depends on C and f(0), and supplies no sharp concentration coefficient.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[b=|t|L,\ D=|t|(|f(0)|+|m|),\quad e^{t(f(x)-m)}\le e^{D+C^{-1}b^2}e^{C\|x\|^2}.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>two_mul_le_add_mul_sq; Real.exp_le_exp; Integrable.const_mul; Integrable.mono&#x27;</p></details></div><section class="lean-verification"><h2>Lean verification</h2><p>The exact proposition and proof begin folded so the mathematical argument remains the primary reading path.</p><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-statement" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integrable_and_integrable_exp_centered_of_lipschitz"><summary>Lean statement · integrable_and_integrable_exp_centered_of_lipschitz</summary><p>For every actual Gaussian mu and globally L-Lipschitz scalar f on the stated Banach/Borel space, f is Bochner integrable and exp(t*(f-int f dmu)) is integrable for every real t. This is a Gaussian-domain background theorem, not the sharp concentration bound.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem integrable_and_integrable_exp_centered_of_lipschitz
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E]
    [CompleteSpace E] [SecondCountableTopology E] [MeasurableSpace E] [BorelSpace E]
    {μ : MeasureTheory.Measure E} [ProbabilityTheory.IsGaussian μ]
    {L : ℝ≥0} {f : E → ℝ} (hf : LipschitzWith L f) :
    MeasureTheory.Integrable f μ ∧ ∀ t : ℝ,
      MeasureTheory.Integrable
        (fun x =&gt; Real.exp (t * (f x - ∫ z, f z ∂μ))) μ</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-probability-gaussianlipschitzexponential.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-proof" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integrable_and_integrable_exp_centered_of_lipschitz"><summary>Lean proof · integrable_and_integrable_exp_centered_of_lipschitz</summary><p>Produce the genuine first moment; Use actual Fernique integrability; Dominate every signed centered exponential</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem integrable_and_integrable_exp_centered_of_lipschitz
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E]
    [CompleteSpace E] [SecondCountableTopology E] [MeasurableSpace E] [BorelSpace E]
    {μ : MeasureTheory.Measure E} [ProbabilityTheory.IsGaussian μ]
    {L : ℝ≥0} {f : E → ℝ} (hf : LipschitzWith L f) :
    MeasureTheory.Integrable f μ ∧ ∀ t : ℝ,
      MeasureTheory.Integrable
        (fun x =&gt; Real.exp (t * (f x - ∫ z, f z ∂μ))) μ := by
  have hbound (x : E) : |f x| ≤ (L : ℝ)*‖x‖+|f 0| := by
    have hd := hf.dist_le_mul x 0
    simp only [dist_eq_norm, sub_zero, Real.norm_eq_abs] at hd
    calc
      |f x| = |(f x-f 0)+f 0| := by congr 1; ring
      _ ≤ |f x-f 0|+|f 0| := abs_add_le _ _
      _ ≤ (L : ℝ)*‖x‖+|f 0| := add_le_add hd le_rfl
  have hi : Integrable f μ :=
    (((IsGaussian.integrable_id (μ := μ)).norm.const_mul (L : ℝ)).add
      (integrable_const |f 0|)).mono&#x27; hf.continuous.aestronglyMeasurable
        (ae_of_all _ fun x =&gt; by simpa only [Real.norm_eq_abs, Pi.add_apply, id_eq] using hbound x)
  refine ⟨hi,fun t =&gt; ?_⟩
  obtain ⟨C,hC,hint⟩ := IsGaussian.exists_integrable_exp_sq μ
  let b : ℝ := |t| *(L : ℝ)
  let D : ℝ := |t| *(|f 0|+|∫ z, f z ∂μ|)
  have hb : 0 ≤ b := mul_nonneg (abs_nonneg _) L.coe_nonneg
  have hdom (x : E) : t*(f x-∫ z, f z ∂μ) ≤
      D+C⁻¹*b^2+C*‖x‖^2 := by
    have hlin : t*(f x-∫ z, f z ∂μ) ≤ b*‖x‖+D := by
      calc
        _ ≤ |t*(f x-∫ z, f z ∂μ)| := le_abs_self _
        _ = |t| *|f x-∫ z, f z ∂μ| := abs_mul _ _
        _ ≤ |t| *(|f x|+|∫ z, f z ∂μ|) :=
          mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_left (by simpa only [sub_zero, zero_sub, abs_neg] using
            (abs_sub_le (f x) 0 (∫ z, f z ∂μ))) (abs_nonneg _)
        _ ≤ |t| *((L : ℝ)*‖x‖+|f 0|+|∫ z, f z ∂μ|) :=
          mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_left (add_le_add (hbound x) le_rfl) (abs_nonneg _)
        _ = b*‖x‖+D := by dsimp [b,D]; ring
    have hy : 2*‖x‖*b ≤ C*‖x‖^2+C⁻¹*b^2 := two_mul_le_add_mul_sq hC
    have hnon : 0 ≤ b*‖x‖ := mul_nonneg hb (norm_nonneg _)
    nlinarith
  apply (hint.const_mul (Real.exp (D+C⁻¹*b^2))).mono&#x27;
    (by fun_prop)
  filter_upwards with x
  rw [Real.norm_eq_abs,abs_of_pos (Real.exp_pos _),← Real.exp_add]
  exact Real.exp_le_exp.mpr (hdom x)

/- The zero-Lipschitz branch of the sealed sharp target is internal; this is
not an assertion of the remaining positive-dimensional concentration bound. -/</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-probability-gaussianlipschitzexponential.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details></section><section data-source-comparison="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integrable_and_integrable_exp_centered_of_lipschitz"><h3>Source assumptions versus formal assumptions</h3><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Actual Lean</th><th>Difference kind</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Authored Gaussian-domain background implication from pinned Mathlib Fernique; this is not the printed sharp concentration estimate.</td><td>Real complete second-countable normed vector space E with Borel measurable structure, Gaussian probability measure mu (possibly noncentered/degenerate).
Actual globally Lipschitz scalar function f:E-&gt;R with NNReal constant L, including L=0. No differentiability, boundedness, positive dimension, given mean, Laplace certificate or sharp coefficient premise.
For every actual Gaussian mu and globally L-Lipschitz scalar f on the stated Banach/Borel space, f is Bochner integrable and exp(t*(f-int f dmu)) is integrable for every real t. This is a Gaussian-domain background theorem, not the sharp concentration bound.</td><td>same</td><td>Canonical global Lipschitz mathematical input and Gaussian Banach/Borel contracts exactly stated. Actual source consumer produces its direction Lipschitz bound internally; no new source premise.</td></tr><tr><td>SPHMC4.3 sharp centered concentration coefficient eta*normu^2/2.</td><td>This unchanged general Gaussian domain leaf produces the actual first moment and every signed centered exponential L1; it does not itself supply a sharp concentration coefficient. The separate current finite-Hilbert standard-Gaussian theorem integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz and actual proximal oracle prove the scoped sharp (4.3) bound locally, with independent admission stages recorded separately. Bias (4.2), first W2/LSI/T2 bound (4.6), full Lemma4.2, higher smoothing/Picard/Wp/proxy-warmness/initialization, PBPS implementation/work, both main results and actual-input expected-query-cost composition remain open. TV proximity does not transfer unbounded expected cost.</td><td>unresolved</td><td>Fernique supplies domain only; this mapping claims no sharp estimate from the domain leaf. The separate current sharp declaration addresses the scoped coefficient, with full-paper obligations unchanged.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>This unchanged general Gaussian domain leaf produces the actual first moment and every signed centered exponential L1; it does not itself supply a sharp concentration coefficient. The separate current finite-Hilbert standard-Gaussian theorem integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz and actual proximal oracle prove the scoped sharp (4.3) bound locally, with independent admission stages recorded separately. Bias (4.2), first W2/LSI/T2 bound (4.6), full Lemma4.2, higher smoothing/Picard/Wp/proxy-warmness/initialization, PBPS implementation/work, both main results and actual-input expected-query-cost composition remain open. TV proximity does not transfer unbounded expected cost.</p><p><strong>Encoder–denoiser:</strong> accepted · equivalent-after-elaboration</p><p>A generalization is not a source correction. Proposed missing conditions require separate independent repair review. No proposed repair silently changes the original theorem.</p></section><details class="reader-ledger boundary-ledger"><summary>Scope and omitted-condition boundaries</summary><ul><li>This unchanged general Gaussian domain leaf produces the actual first moment and every signed centered exponential L1; it does not itself supply a sharp concentration coefficient. The separate current finite-Hilbert standard-Gaussian theorem integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz and actual proximal oracle prove the scoped sharp (4.3) bound locally, with independent admission stages recorded separately. Bias (4.2), first W2/LSI/T2 bound (4.6), full Lemma4.2, higher smoothing/Picard/Wp/proxy-warmness/initialization, PBPS implementation/work, both main results and actual-input expected-query-cost composition remain open. TV proximity does not transfer unbounded expected cost.</li></ul></details><details class="proof-reader-provenance reader-ledger"><summary>Sources, dependencies, and reuse</summary><h2>Source and reuse</h2><h3>Samplinglib parents called</h3><ul></ul><h3>Mathlib API called (external library)</h3><ul><li>ProbabilityTheory.IsGaussian.integrable_id</li><li>ProbabilityTheory.IsGaussian.exists_integrable_exp_sq</li><li>LipschitzWith.dist_le_mul</li><li>Integrable.mono&#x27;</li><li>two_mul_le_add_mul_sq</li></ul><h3>Mathematical sources</h3><ul><li><a href="https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/db584cd6d46c92f209a44c0f1c829460d327499d/Mathlib/Probability/Distributions/Gaussian/Fernique.lean">Pinned Gaussian domain background; sharp coefficient supplied by separate declaration</a> — This unchanged general Gaussian domain leaf produces the actual first moment and every signed centered exponential L1; it does not itself supply a sharp concentration coefficient. The separate current finite-Hilbert standard-Gaussian theorem integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz and actual proximal oracle prove the scoped sharp (4.3) bound locally, with independent admission stages recorded separately. Bias (4.2), first W2/LSI/T2 bound (4.6), full Lemma4.2, higher smoothing/Picard/Wp/proxy-warmness/initialization, PBPS implementation/work, both main results and actual-input expected-query-cost composition remain open. TV proximity does not transfer unbounded expected cost.</li></ul><p>Samplinglib prose is not a quotation or a source-equivalence certificate. Definitions and aliases are explained as constructions, not counted as new mathematical proofs.</p></details></article><h3>Which proof obligations and prerequisites are covered?</h3><ul><li><span class="status status-orange">Formalized proof edge; source adapter/review separate</span> Genuine firstmoment and allsigned centered Laplace domain</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Sharp coefficient supplied by separate theorem; bias/transport/full Lemma remain open</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Main results and actual-input expected-query-cost composition open</li></ul></section><section id="gaussian-sharp-concentration" data-publication-item="gaussian-sharp-concentration"><h2>Sharp standard Gaussian concentration for every Lipschitz observable</h2><p>ASTIS authored Gaussian rotation/Stein/Herbst and internal approximation background, consumed by SPHMC (4.3).</p><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.E3">arXiv:2609.06906v1 with pinned Mathlib db584cd6 Gaussian background · SPHMC arXiv2609.06906v1 S4.E3 centered Gaussian-gradient-output concentration with exact eta*norm(u)^2/2; S3.E2 actual evaluator/S2.E1/S4.Ex3 proximal; inherited S1.E1 genuine C2 Hessian and S3.SS1.p1.1 every eta&gt;0.</a> · faithful paraphrase</p><h3>Complete source statement (Samplinglib restatement)</h3><p>For every globally L-Lipschitz scalar observable on the stated finite real Hilbert/Borel space, its actual standard Gaussian centered exponential integral is at most exp(L^2*t^2/2) for every signed real t. Actual first moments and all exponential domains are produced by the existing shared domain theorem; the C1 restriction used inside the proof is removed internally.</p><ul><li>Finite-dimensional complete real Hilbert space E with its actual inner product and Borel measurable structure, including dimension zero; gamma is the canonical covariance-identity standard Gaussian.</li><li>Actual globally L-Lipschitz f:E-&gt;R with NNReal L&gt;=0 including zero; every signed t in R. No differentiability, boundedness, given mean, positive dimension or concentration/LSI/domain certificate.</li></ul><h3>Sharp standard Gaussian concentration for every Lipschitz observable</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\gamma=\operatorname{stdGaussian}(E),\quad m=\int f\,d\gamma,\quad\int e^{t(f-m)}\,d\gamma\le e^{L^2t^2/2}\quad(t\in\mathbb R).\]</div><h2>Read the formalized proofs</h2><p>Each statement and proof below has its own closed Lean disclosure. Samplinglib parents, Mathlib calls and external mathematical sources are distinguished in each proof.</p><article class="proof-reader" data-authored-declaration="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz"><div class="eyebrow">Samplinglib mathematical lesson</div><h2>Sharp standard Gaussian concentration for every Lipschitz observable</h2><p><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz</code> · theorem · <a href="../../../lessons/index.html">Teaching coverage</a></p><section class="reader-first-pass" aria-label="First reading"><div class="reader-first-pass-label">Read this first</div><h2>Mathematical statement</h2><p class="reader-claim">For every globally L-Lipschitz scalar observable on the stated finite real Hilbert/Borel space, its actual standard Gaussian centered exponential integral is at most exp(L^2*t^2/2) for every signed real t. Actual first moments and all exponential domains are produced by the existing shared domain theorem; the C1 restriction used inside the proof is removed internally.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation reader-main-equation">\[\gamma=\operatorname{stdGaussian}(E),\quad m=\int f\,d\gamma,\quad\int e^{t(f-m)}\,d\gamma\le e^{L^2t^2/2}\quad(t\in\mathbb R).\]</div><h3>Proof roadmap</h3><ol class="proof-roadmap"><li><span>1</span><strong>Establish the actual Gaussian domains</strong><p>The existing Fernique-domain declaration proves first Bochner integrability and every signed centered exponential L1 from global Lipschitz growth. Shifting the center gives radial exponential domains. Polynomial factors are dominated by a larger radial exponential, also on the actual product Gaussian equipped with its Banach max norm.</p></li><li><span>2</span><strong>Derive Gaussian Stein integration by parts</strong><p>For the temporary C1 case, the canonical ASTIS Gaussian density with respect to the given Hilbert volume has derivative -w(x) inner(x,-). Growth, the genuine Gaussian second moment and the derivative bound by L prove every Haar integration-by-parts integrability input. The resulting Stein equality has no supplied domain certificate.</p></li><li><span>3</span><strong>Rotate two independent standard Gaussians</strong><p>For X,Y independently distributed as gamma, U=cos(theta)X+sin(theta)Y and W=-sin(theta)X+cos(theta)Y have the same product law. Differentiate F(theta)=E[f(X) exp(t(f(U)-m))] using an explicit polynomial times radial-exponential domination valid for every theta. The inverse relation X=cos(theta)U-sin(theta)W is exact.</p></li><li><span>4</span><strong>Apply Stein only to the affine Lipschitz factor</strong><p>After rotation, fix U=u and let a(u) be the Riesz representative of Df(u). Apply Stein in W to the C1 Lipschitz function W-&gt;f(cu-sW). Its derivative is -s Df(cu-sW). The exponential is constant in this conditional variable; it is never asserted globally Lipschitz. Actual product L1 and Fubini justify the conditional calculation.</p></li><li><span>5</span><strong>Recover the covariance bound with correct sign</strong><p>The inner integral has absolute value at most L squared. On theta in [0,pi/2] and t&gt;=0 the factor -sin(theta)t is nonpositive, so the upper bound on the inner integral gives a lower bound on F prime. For C=t L squared M, H=F-cos(theta)C is nondecreasing by the mean-value theorem. Actual derivative existence gives endpoint continuity. Endpoint independence yields the covariance inequality.</p></li><li><span>6</span><strong>Prove the sharp Herbst estimate and both signs</strong><p>All signed exponential domains give integrableExpSet=the full real line. Pinned Mathlib MGF differentiation gives the actual M prime. The covariance estimate implies M prime&lt;=t L squared M on t&gt;=0. The integrating factor exp(-L squared t squared/2)M is nonincreasing and equals one at zero. Apply the result to -f and -t for negative parameters.</p></li><li><span>7</span><strong>Smooth internally without changing the constant</strong><p>Use the actual nonnegative normalized compactly supported smooth bump against canonical Hilbert volume. Its outer radius is epsilon_n=2^-n and its mass is one. The convolution f_n is C1 and remains globally L Lipschitz. The uniform error is at most L epsilon_n. This introduces no differentiability assumption on the public observable.</p></li><li><span>8</span><strong>Pass the actual center and exponential integral to the limit</strong><p>First-moment integrability and the uniform approximation give |m_n-m|&lt;=L epsilon_n. The centered exponents converge pointwise, and every n is dominated by exp(2|t|L) exp(t(f-m)), whose L1 was proved before the sharp estimate. Dominated convergence passes the same sharp bound to f. Zero L and the zero-dimensional carrier are discharged directly as constant observables.</p></li></ol></section><details class="reader-ledger assumption-ledger"><summary>Full assumptions and notation</summary><h3>All objects and hypotheses</h3><ul><li>Finite-dimensional complete real Hilbert space E with its actual inner product and Borel measurable structure, including dimension zero; gamma is the canonical covariance-identity standard Gaussian.</li><li>Actual globally L-Lipschitz f:E-&gt;R with NNReal L&gt;=0 including zero; every signed t in R. No differentiability, boundedness, given mean, positive dimension or concentration/LSI/domain certificate.</li></ul><p class="muted"></p></details><h2>Mathematical proof</h2><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>1. Establish the actual Gaussian domains</h4><p>The existing Fernique-domain declaration proves first Bochner integrability and every signed centered exponential L1 from global Lipschitz growth. Shifting the center gives radial exponential domains. Polynomial factors are dominated by a larger radial exponential, also on the actual product Gaussian equipped with its Banach max norm.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[m=\int f\,d\gamma,\quad e^{t(f-m)}\in L^1(\gamma),\quad (1+\|x\|)^2e^{a\|x\|}\in L^1(\gamma).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>integrable_and_integrable_exp_centered_of_lipschitz; gaussian_radial_exponential_integrable; gaussian_polynomial_radial_integrable</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>2. Derive Gaussian Stein integration by parts</h4><p>For the temporary C1 case, the canonical ASTIS Gaussian density with respect to the given Hilbert volume has derivative -w(x) inner(x,-). Growth, the genuine Gaussian second moment and the derivative bound by L prove every Haar integration-by-parts integrability input. The resulting Stein equality has no supplied domain certificate.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[w(x)=(\sqrt{2\pi})^{-d}e^{-\|x\|^2/2},\quad Dw(x)[v]=-w(x)\langle x,v\rangle,\quad\int\langle x,v\rangle f(x)\,d\gamma=\int Df(x)[v]\,d\gamma.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>standardGaussian_density; standardWeight_hasFDerivAt; standardGaussian_stein_lipschitz_c1; integral_mul_fderiv_eq_neg_fderiv_mul_of_integrable</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>3. Rotate two independent standard Gaussians</h4><p>For X,Y independently distributed as gamma, U=cos(theta)X+sin(theta)Y and W=-sin(theta)X+cos(theta)Y have the same product law. Differentiate F(theta)=E[f(X) exp(t(f(U)-m))] using an explicit polynomial times radial-exponential domination valid for every theta. The inverse relation X=cos(theta)U-sin(theta)W is exact.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[U_\theta=cX+sY,\ W_\theta=-sX+cY,\quad F^{\prime}(\theta)=t\,\mathbb E[f(X)e^{t(f(U_\theta)-m)}Df(U_\theta)[W_\theta]].\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>standardGaussian_rotation_integral; rotX_hasDerivAt; rotated_laplace_integral_hasDerivAt; rotation_inverse_first</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>4. Apply Stein only to the affine Lipschitz factor</h4><p>After rotation, fix U=u and let a(u) be the Riesz representative of Df(u). Apply Stein in W to the C1 Lipschitz function W-&gt;f(cu-sW). Its derivative is -s Df(cu-sW). The exponential is constant in this conditional variable; it is never asserted globally Lipschitz. Actual product L1 and Fubini justify the conditional calculation.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[F^{\prime}(\theta)=-s t\int e^{t(f(u)-m)}\!\left[\int Df(cu-sv)[a(u)]\,d\gamma(v)\right]d\gamma(u),\quad\|a(u)\|\le L.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>standardGaussian_stein_affine; affine_stein_laplace_slice; rotated_laplace_derivative_integral_identity</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>5. Recover the covariance bound with correct sign</h4><p>The inner integral has absolute value at most L squared. On theta in [0,pi/2] and t&gt;=0 the factor -sin(theta)t is nonpositive, so the upper bound on the inner integral gives a lower bound on F prime. For C=t L squared M, H=F-cos(theta)C is nondecreasing by the mean-value theorem. Actual derivative existence gives endpoint continuity. Endpoint independence yields the covariance inequality.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[M=\int e^{t(f-m)}d\gamma,\quad F^{\prime}\ge-\sin\theta\,tL^2M,\quad H^{\prime}\ge0,\quad\operatorname{Cov}_\gamma(f,e^{t(f-m)})\le tL^2M.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>affine_directional_derivative_integral_norm_le; rotated_laplace_derivative_integral_lower_bound; gaussian_laplace_covariance_bound; monotoneOn_of_hasDerivWithinAt_nonneg</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>6. Prove the sharp Herbst estimate and both signs</h4><p>All signed exponential domains give integrableExpSet=the full real line. Pinned Mathlib MGF differentiation gives the actual M prime. The covariance estimate implies M prime&lt;=t L squared M on t&gt;=0. The integrating factor exp(-L squared t squared/2)M is nonincreasing and equals one at zero. Apply the result to -f and -t for negative parameters.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[M^{\prime}(t)\le tL^2M(t),\quad\left(e^{-L^2t^2/2}M(t)\right)^{\prime}\le0,\quad M(t)\le e^{L^2t^2/2}\quad(t\in\mathbb R).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>gaussian_centered_integrableExpSet; gaussian_centered_laplace_hasDerivAt; gaussian_centered_laplace_bound_c1; hasDerivAt_mgf; antitoneOn_of_hasDerivWithinAt_nonpos</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>7. Smooth internally without changing the constant</h4><p>Use the actual nonnegative normalized compactly supported smooth bump against canonical Hilbert volume. Its outer radius is epsilon_n=2^-n and its mass is one. The convolution f_n is C1 and remains globally L Lipschitz. The uniform error is at most L epsilon_n. This introduces no differentiability assumption on the public observable.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[f_n=\phi_n*f,\quad\phi_n\ge0,\quad\int\phi_n\,dx=1,\quad\operatorname{Lip}(f_n)\le L,\quad\|f_n-f\|_\infty\le L2^{-n}.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>normed_bump_convolution_lipschitz; normed_bump_convolution_error; smoothApprox_contDiff; smoothApprox_lipschitz</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>8. Pass the actual center and exponential integral to the limit</h4><p>First-moment integrability and the uniform approximation give |m_n-m|&lt;=L epsilon_n. The centered exponents converge pointwise, and every n is dominated by exp(2|t|L) exp(t(f-m)), whose L1 was proved before the sharp estimate. Dominated convergence passes the same sharp bound to f. Zero L and the zero-dimensional carrier are discharged directly as constant observables.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[|m_n-m|\le L2^{-n},\quad e^{t(f_n-m_n)}\le e^{2|t|L}e^{t(f-m)},\quad\int e^{t(f-m)}d\gamma\le e^{L^2t^2/2}.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>smoothApprox_mean_error; smoothApprox_centered_exp_integral_tendsto; centered_exp_integral_of_lipschitz_zero; centered_exp_integral_of_subsingleton; integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz</p></details></div><section class="lean-verification"><h2>Lean verification</h2><p>The exact proposition and proof begin folded so the mathematical argument remains the primary reading path.</p><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-statement" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz"><summary>Lean statement · integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz</summary><p>For every globally L-Lipschitz scalar observable on the stated finite real Hilbert/Borel space, its actual standard Gaussian centered exponential integral is at most exp(L^2*t^2/2) for every signed real t. Actual first moments and all exponential domains are produced by the existing shared domain theorem; the C1 restriction used inside the proof is removed internally.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E]
    [CompleteSpace E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] [MeasurableSpace E] [BorelSpace E]
    {L : ℝ≥0} {f : E → ℝ} (hf : LipschitzWith L f) (t : ℝ) :
    (∫ x, Real.exp (t * (f x - ∫ z, f z ∂ProbabilityTheory.stdGaussian E))
      ∂ProbabilityTheory.stdGaussian E) ≤ Real.exp ((L : ℝ)^2 * t^2 / 2)</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-probability-gaussianlipschitzexponential.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-proof" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz"><summary>Lean proof · integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz</summary><p>Establish the actual Gaussian domains; Derive Gaussian Stein integration by parts; Rotate two independent standard Gaussians; Apply Stein only to the affine Lipschitz factor; Recover the covariance bound with correct sign; Prove the sharp Herbst estimate and both signs; Smooth internally without changing the constant; Pass the actual center and exponential integral to the limit</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E]
    [CompleteSpace E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] [MeasurableSpace E] [BorelSpace E]
    {L : ℝ≥0} {f : E → ℝ} (hf : LipschitzWith L f) (t : ℝ) :
    (∫ x, Real.exp (t * (f x - ∫ z, f z ∂ProbabilityTheory.stdGaussian E))
      ∂ProbabilityTheory.stdGaussian E) ≤ Real.exp ((L : ℝ)^2 * t^2 / 2) := by
  by_cases hL : L = 0
  · subst L
    rw [centered_exp_integral_of_lipschitz_zero hf t]
    norm_num
  · rcases subsingleton_or_nontrivial E with hE | hE
    · letI : Subsingleton E := hE
      rw [centered_exp_integral_of_subsingleton f t]
      exact Real.one_le_exp_iff.mpr (by positivity)
    · exact le_of_tendsto&#x27; (smoothApprox_centered_exp_integral_tendsto hf t)
        (fun n =&gt; gaussian_centered_laplace_bound_c1
          (smoothApprox_contDiff hf n) (smoothApprox_lipschitz hf n) t)

end AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential
end</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-probability-gaussianlipschitzexponential.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details></section><section data-source-comparison="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz"><h3>Source assumptions versus formal assumptions</h3><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Actual Lean</th><th>Difference kind</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>SPHMC (4.3) uses standard Gaussian concentration for the actual gradient observable.</td><td>Finite-dimensional complete real Hilbert space E with its actual inner product and Borel measurable structure, including dimension zero; gamma is the canonical covariance-identity standard Gaussian.
Actual globally L-Lipschitz f:E-&gt;R with NNReal L&gt;=0 including zero; every signed t in R. No differentiability, boundedness, given mean, positive dimension or concentration/LSI/domain certificate.
For every globally L-Lipschitz scalar observable on the stated finite real Hilbert/Borel space, its actual standard Gaussian centered exponential integral is at most exp(L^2*t^2/2) for every signed real t. Actual first moments and all exponential domains are produced by the existing shared domain theorem; the C1 restriction used inside the proof is removed internally.</td><td>source-implicit</td><td>Canonical background implication independently proves the printed sharp coefficient; its public Lipschitz input is produced inside the actual source consumer. All internal smoothness/domain hypotheses are discharged.</td></tr><tr><td>Primary does not detail the Gaussian concentration proof.</td><td>Actual Gaussian rotation, conditional Stein, MVT/Herbst and normalized approximation proof in pinned ASTIS/Mathlib; no LSI certificate or upstream SLT port.</td><td>same</td><td>Authored background route establishes the exact standard-Gaussian inequality with unchanged coefficient and assumptions; not attributed as the printed author proof.</td></tr><tr><td>Other source bounds and both complete papers.</td><td>Locally compiled sharp centered Gaussian-output estimate (4.3), retained exact positive-step proximal/evaluator and (4.4)-(4.5), genuine first Bochner moments and all signed exponential domains. Independent mathematics/blind/source/publication/exact-commit/integration admission pending. Bias (4.2), first W2 inequality in (4.6), LSI/T2 adapter, full Lemma4.2, higher smoothing/Picard/Wp/proxy-warmness/initialization, PBPS invariance/nonexplosion/hypocoercivity/implementation/work, both main results and actual-input precision/expected-query-cost composition remain open. Centers are actual Gaussian output means. TV proximity does not transfer unbounded expected cost.</td><td>unresolved</td><td>Only the scoped background/consumer estimate is addressed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Locally compiled sharp centered Gaussian-output estimate (4.3), retained exact positive-step proximal/evaluator and (4.4)-(4.5), genuine first Bochner moments and all signed exponential domains. Independent mathematics/blind/source/publication/exact-commit/integration admission pending. Bias (4.2), first W2 inequality in (4.6), LSI/T2 adapter, full Lemma4.2, higher smoothing/Picard/Wp/proxy-warmness/initialization, PBPS invariance/nonexplosion/hypocoercivity/implementation/work, both main results and actual-input precision/expected-query-cost composition remain open. Centers are actual Gaussian output means. TV proximity does not transfer unbounded expected cost.</p><p><strong>Encoder–denoiser:</strong> accepted · equivalent-after-elaboration</p><p>A generalization is not a source correction. Proposed missing conditions require separate independent repair review. No proposed repair silently changes the original theorem.</p></section><details class="reader-ledger boundary-ledger"><summary>Scope and omitted-condition boundaries</summary><ul><li>Locally compiled sharp centered Gaussian-output estimate (4.3), retained exact positive-step proximal/evaluator and (4.4)-(4.5), genuine first Bochner moments and all signed exponential domains. Independent mathematics/blind/source/publication/exact-commit/integration admission pending. Bias (4.2), first W2 inequality in (4.6), LSI/T2 adapter, full Lemma4.2, higher smoothing/Picard/Wp/proxy-warmness/initialization, PBPS invariance/nonexplosion/hypocoercivity/implementation/work, both main results and actual-input precision/expected-query-cost composition remain open. Centers are actual Gaussian output means. TV proximity does not transfer unbounded expected cost.</li></ul></details><details class="proof-reader-provenance reader-ledger"><summary>Sources, dependencies, and reuse</summary><h2>Source and reuse</h2><h3>Samplinglib parents called</h3><ul><li><a href="../../../theorems/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-probability-gaussianlipschitzexponential-integrable-and-integrable-exp-centered-of-lipschitz.html"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integrable_and_integrable_exp_centered_of_lipschitz</code></a></li><li><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-measure-isotropicgaussiandensity.html#decl-autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-measure-isotropicgaussiandensity-map--958a008371"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.IsotropicGaussianDensity.map_sqrt_smul_stdGaussian_eq_withDensity</code></a></li><li><a href="../../../theorems/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-probability-stdgaussianmoment-integrable-norm-sq-and-integral-stdgaussian.html"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.StdGaussianMoment.integrable_norm_sq_and_integral_stdGaussian</code></a></li></ul><h3>Mathlib API called (external library)</h3><ul><li>ProbabilityTheory.IsGaussian.map_rotation_eq_self</li><li>integral_mul_fderiv_eq_neg_fderiv_mul_of_integrable</li><li>hasDerivAt_integral_of_dominated_loc_of_deriv_le</li><li>MeasureTheory.integral_prod</li><li>ProbabilityTheory.hasDerivAt_mgf</li><li>ProbabilityTheory.integrable_pow_mul_exp_of_mem_interior_integrableExpSet</li><li>monotoneOn_of_hasDerivWithinAt_nonneg</li><li>antitoneOn_of_hasDerivWithinAt_nonpos</li><li>ContDiffBump.hasCompactSupport_normed</li><li>ContDiffBump.contDiff_normed</li><li>MeasureTheory.tendsto_integral_of_dominated_convergence</li></ul><h3>Mathematical sources</h3><ul><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.E3">Exact Gaussian background for sharp (4.3)</a> — Locally compiled sharp centered Gaussian-output estimate (4.3), retained exact positive-step proximal/evaluator and (4.4)-(4.5), genuine first Bochner moments and all signed exponential domains. Independent mathematics/blind/source/publication/exact-commit/integration admission pending. Bias (4.2), first W2 inequality in (4.6), LSI/T2 adapter, full Lemma4.2, higher smoothing/Picard/Wp/proxy-warmness/initialization, PBPS invariance/nonexplosion/hypocoercivity/implementation/work, both main results and actual-input precision/expected-query-cost composition remain open. Centers are actual Gaussian output means. TV proximity does not transfer unbounded expected cost.</li></ul><p>Samplinglib prose is not a quotation or a source-equivalence certificate. Definitions and aliases are explained as constructions, not counted as new mathematical proofs.</p></details></article><h3>Which proof obligations and prerequisites are covered?</h3><ul><li><span class="status status-orange">Formalized proof edge; source adapter/review separate</span> Exact sharp centered bound and genuine Gaussian domains</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Bias/transport/full algorithms/main/work remain open</li></ul></section><section id="gaussian-sqrt-density-domain" data-publication-item="gaussian-sqrt-density-domain"><h2>Actual Gaussian-Gibbs square-root density and energy domains</h2><p>ASTIS authored analytic background for the printed GaussianLSI/T2 route, not a printed standalone theorem.</p><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.Ex8">arXiv:2609.06906v1 with pinned Mathlib db584cd6 · S4.Ex8 actual residual/standardized posterior; S4.SS1.p4.2-p4.3 omitted classical domains before FIRST S4.E6 GaussianLSI/T2.</a> · faithful paraphrase</p><h3>Complete source statement (Samplinglib restatement)</h3><p>For the actual covariance-identity Gaussian tilted by the normalized convex C2 residual, produce its positive finite partition, literal normalized positive density and C2 square root, actual volume Gibbs equality and probability, f and gradientf L2gamma, gradientrho L2r, entropy L1, explicit derivative identities and exact one-quarter Dirichlet-Fisher energy.</p><ul><li>Complete finite-dimensional real Hilbert E with canonical volume and Borel structure; dimension zero included.</li><li>Actual global C2 rho, rho(0)=0, gradientrho(0)=0 and Hessian0..L for NNReal L including0. Probability/positive normalizer/entropy/gradient and L2 domains are conclusions.</li></ul><h3>Actual Gaussian-Gibbs square-root density and energy domains</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[Z=\int e^{-\rho}d\gamma\in(0,1],\quad r=q\gamma\propto e^{-\|u\|^2/2-\rho}du,\quad q=e^{-\rho}/Z,\quad f=\sqrt q,\quad\int\|\nabla f\|^2d\gamma=\tfrac14\int\|\nabla\rho\|^2dr.\]</div><h2>Read the formalized proofs</h2><p>Each statement and proof below has its own closed Lean disclosure. Samplinglib parents, Mathlib calls and external mathematical sources are distinguished in each proof.</p><article class="proof-reader" data-authored-declaration="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.GaussianSqrtDensityDomain.gaussian_sqrt_density_domain"><div class="eyebrow">Samplinglib mathematical lesson</div><h2>Actual Gaussian-Gibbs square-root density and energy domains</h2><p><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.GaussianSqrtDensityDomain.gaussian_sqrt_density_domain</code> · theorem · <a href="../../../lessons/index.html">Teaching coverage</a></p><section class="reader-first-pass" aria-label="First reading"><div class="reader-first-pass-label">Read this first</div><h2>Mathematical statement</h2><p class="reader-claim">For the actual covariance-identity Gaussian tilted by the normalized convex C2 residual, produce its positive finite partition, literal normalized positive density and C2 square root, actual volume Gibbs equality and probability, f and gradientf L2gamma, gradientrho L2r, entropy L1, explicit derivative identities and exact one-quarter Dirichlet-Fisher energy.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation reader-main-equation">\[Z=\int e^{-\rho}d\gamma\in(0,1],\quad r=q\gamma\propto e^{-\|u\|^2/2-\rho}du,\quad q=e^{-\rho}/Z,\quad f=\sqrt q,\quad\int\|\nabla f\|^2d\gamma=\tfrac14\int\|\nabla\rho\|^2dr.\]</div><h3>Proof roadmap</h3><ol class="proof-roadmap"><li><span>1</span><strong>Derive the nonnegative residual and gradient growth</strong><p>Hessian0..L produces convexity and L-Lipschitz gradient through the canonical curvature provider. The normalized stationary origin then yields rho&gt;=0 and gradientnorm&lt;=Lnormu. No extra convexity or growth premise is introduced; L0 and dimension0 are included.</p></li><li><span>2</span><strong>Normalize against the actual standard Gaussian</strong><p>The real exponential is positive and bounded by1. Gaussian probability gives its L1 and strictly positive finite integral Z&lt;=1. The literal tilt is exactly withDensity(exp(-rho)/Z), integrates to1 and is a genuine probability.</p></li><li><span>3</span><strong>Identify the canonical volume Gibbs law</strong><p>Use the compiled covariance-identity Gaussian density against canonical volume. Its actual mass1 forces the ordinary quadratic partition to be finite and positive; normalize that exact density. Consecutive tilts then identify the Gaussian-relative residual law with the volume Gibbs potential norm²/2+rho.</p></li><li><span>4</span><strong>Differentiate the explicit positive square root</strong><p>The everywhere chosen f=exp(-rho/2)/sqrtZ is C2, strictly positive and satisfies f²=q. Ordinary Frechet chain rules and the Hilbert Riesz identification yield its gradient and the log-density gradient. No arbitrary pointwise RN representative is differentiated.</p></li><li><span>5</span><strong>Produce the finite gradient and entropy domains</strong><p>The actual Gaussian second moment dominates exp(-rho)normgradρ² by L²normu². This gives gradρ L2r and gradf L2gamma after multiplying by the proved normalizer. f L2 follows from q L1. The bound rho exp(-rho)&lt;=1 and logq=-rho-logZ give entropy L1 without assuming its finiteness.</p></li><li><span>6</span><strong>Integrate the exact Dirichlet-Fisher identity</strong><p>Integrate normgradf²=(1/4)qnormgradρ² against gamma and rewrite the actual tilted integral. The domains have already been proved, so both energies are finite. This is the Euclidean Hilbert energy with the exact factor1/4; no LSI or transport inequality is asserted.</p></li></ol></section><details class="reader-ledger assumption-ledger"><summary>Full assumptions and notation</summary><h3>All objects and hypotheses</h3><ul><li>Complete finite-dimensional real Hilbert E with canonical volume and Borel structure; dimension zero included.</li><li>Actual global C2 rho, rho(0)=0, gradientrho(0)=0 and Hessian0..L for NNReal L including0. Probability/positive normalizer/entropy/gradient and L2 domains are conclusions.</li></ul><p class="muted"></p></details><h2>Mathematical proof</h2><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>1. Derive the nonnegative residual and gradient growth</h4><p>Hessian0..L produces convexity and L-Lipschitz gradient through the canonical curvature provider. The normalized stationary origin then yields rho&gt;=0 and gradientnorm&lt;=Lnormu. No extra convexity or growth premise is introduced; L0 and dimension0 are included.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\rho(0)=0,\quad\nabla\rho(0)=0,\quad0\preceq\nabla^2\rho\preceq LI\ \Longrightarrow\ \rho\ge0,\ \|\nabla\rho(u)\|\le L\|u\|.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>QuadraticRegularization.strongConvexOn_and_lipschitzWith_gradient_add_quadratic; StrongConvexFirstOrder.firstOrder_lower_bound_of_strongConvexOn</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>2. Normalize against the actual standard Gaussian</h4><p>The real exponential is positive and bounded by1. Gaussian probability gives its L1 and strictly positive finite integral Z&lt;=1. The literal tilt is exactly withDensity(exp(-rho)/Z), integrates to1 and is a genuine probability.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\gamma=\mathcal N(0,I),\quad Z=\int e^{-\rho}d\gamma\in(0,1],\quad q=e^{-\rho}/Z,\quad r=q\gamma,\quad\int q\,d\gamma=1.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>weight_normalization; isProbabilityMeasure_tilted; integral_div</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>3. Identify the canonical volume Gibbs law</h4><p>Use the compiled covariance-identity Gaussian density against canonical volume. Its actual mass1 forces the ordinary quadratic partition to be finite and positive; normalize that exact density. Consecutive tilts then identify the Gaussian-relative residual law with the volume Gibbs potential norm²/2+rho.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\gamma=Z_0^{-1}e^{-\|u\|^2/2}du,\quad r=Z_Q^{-1}e^{-\|u\|^2/2-\rho(u)}du.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>IsotropicGaussianDensity.map_sqrt_smul_stdGaussian_eq_withDensity; integral_withDensity_eq_integral_toReal_smul; tilted_tilted</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>4. Differentiate the explicit positive square root</h4><p>The everywhere chosen f=exp(-rho/2)/sqrtZ is C2, strictly positive and satisfies f²=q. Ordinary Frechet chain rules and the Hilbert Riesz identification yield its gradient and the log-density gradient. No arbitrary pointwise RN representative is differentiated.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[f=\frac{e^{-\rho/2}}{\sqrt Z},\quad f^2=q,\quad\nabla f=-\tfrac12f\nabla\rho,\quad\nabla\log q=-\nabla\rho.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>density_calculus; HasFDerivAt.exp; hasGradientAt_iff_hasFDerivAt; Real.log_div</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>5. Produce the finite gradient and entropy domains</h4><p>The actual Gaussian second moment dominates exp(-rho)normgradρ² by L²normu². This gives gradρ L2r and gradf L2gamma after multiplying by the proved normalizer. f L2 follows from q L1. The bound rho exp(-rho)&lt;=1 and logq=-rho-logZ give entropy L1 without assuming its finiteness.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[e^{-\rho}\|\nabla\rho\|^2\le L^2\|u\|^2,\quad\int\|u\|^2d\gamma=d,\quad0\le\rho e^{-\rho}\le1,\quad q\log q=-Z^{-1}\rho e^{-\rho}-q\log Z.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>StdGaussianMoment.integrable_norm_sq_and_integral_stdGaussian; integrable_tilted_iff; memLp_two_iff_integrable_sq_norm; entropy_integrable</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>6. Integrate the exact Dirichlet-Fisher identity</h4><p>Integrate normgradf²=(1/4)qnormgradρ² against gamma and rewrite the actual tilted integral. The domains have already been proved, so both energies are finite. This is the Euclidean Hilbert energy with the exact factor1/4; no LSI or transport inequality is asserted.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\int\|\nabla f\|^2d\gamma=\frac14\int q\|\nabla\rho\|^2d\gamma=\frac14\int\|\nabla\rho\|^2dr.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>integral_tilted; integral_const_mul; integral_congr_ae</p></details></div><section class="lean-verification"><h2>Lean verification</h2><p>The exact proposition and proof begin folded so the mathematical argument remains the primary reading path.</p><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-statement" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.GaussianSqrtDensityDomain.gaussian_sqrt_density_domain"><summary>Lean statement · gaussian_sqrt_density_domain</summary><p>For the actual covariance-identity Gaussian tilted by the normalized convex C2 residual, produce its positive finite partition, literal normalized positive density and C2 square root, actual volume Gibbs equality and probability, f and gradientf L2gamma, gradientrho L2r, entropy L1, explicit derivative identities and exact one-quarter Dirichlet-Fisher energy.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem gaussian_sqrt_density_domain
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E]
    [CompleteSpace E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] [MeasurableSpace E] [BorelSpace E]
    {rho : E → ℝ} {L : ℝ≥0} (hrho : ContDiff ℝ 2 rho)
    (hrho0 : rho 0 = 0) (hgrad0 : gradient rho 0 = 0)
    (hH : ∀ u v : E, 0 ≤ fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ rho) u v v ∧
      fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ rho) u v v ≤ (L : ℝ)*‖v‖^2) :
    let gamma := stdGaussian E
    let Z := ∫ u, Real.exp (-rho u) ∂gamma
    let q := fun u =&gt; Real.exp (-rho u)/Z
    let f := fun u =&gt; Real.exp (-rho u/2)/Real.sqrt Z
    let r := gamma.tilted (fun u =&gt; -rho u)
    0 &lt; Z ∧ Z ≤ 1 ∧
      r = (volume : Measure E).tilted (fun u =&gt; -(‖u‖^2/2+rho u)) ∧
      r = gamma.withDensity (fun u =&gt; ENNReal.ofReal (q u)) ∧
      IsProbabilityMeasure r ∧
      (∀ u, 0 &lt; q u ∧ f u^2 = q u) ∧
      (∫ u, q u ∂gamma) = 1 ∧
      ContDiff ℝ 2 f ∧ MemLp f 2 gamma ∧
      MemLp (gradient f) 2 gamma ∧ MemLp (gradient rho) 2 r ∧
      Integrable (fun u =&gt; q u*Real.log (q u)) gamma ∧
      (∀ u, gradient f u = -(f u/2) • gradient rho u) ∧
      (∀ u, gradient (fun z =&gt; Real.log (q z)) u = -gradient rho u) ∧
      (∫ u, ‖gradient f u‖^2 ∂gamma) =
        (1/4 : ℝ)*(∫ u, ‖gradient rho u‖^2 ∂r)</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-measure-gaussiansqrtdensitydomain.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-proof" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.GaussianSqrtDensityDomain.gaussian_sqrt_density_domain"><summary>Lean proof · gaussian_sqrt_density_domain</summary><p>Derive the nonnegative residual and gradient growth; Normalize against the actual standard Gaussian; Identify the canonical volume Gibbs law; Differentiate the explicit positive square root; Produce the finite gradient and entropy domains; Integrate the exact Dirichlet-Fisher identity</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem gaussian_sqrt_density_domain
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E]
    [CompleteSpace E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] [MeasurableSpace E] [BorelSpace E]
    {rho : E → ℝ} {L : ℝ≥0} (hrho : ContDiff ℝ 2 rho)
    (hrho0 : rho 0 = 0) (hgrad0 : gradient rho 0 = 0)
    (hH : ∀ u v : E, 0 ≤ fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ rho) u v v ∧
      fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ rho) u v v ≤ (L : ℝ)*‖v‖^2) :
    let gamma := stdGaussian E
    let Z := ∫ u, Real.exp (-rho u) ∂gamma
    let q := fun u =&gt; Real.exp (-rho u)/Z
    let f := fun u =&gt; Real.exp (-rho u/2)/Real.sqrt Z
    let r := gamma.tilted (fun u =&gt; -rho u)
    0 &lt; Z ∧ Z ≤ 1 ∧
      r = (volume : Measure E).tilted (fun u =&gt; -(‖u‖^2/2+rho u)) ∧
      r = gamma.withDensity (fun u =&gt; ENNReal.ofReal (q u)) ∧
      IsProbabilityMeasure r ∧
      (∀ u, 0 &lt; q u ∧ f u^2 = q u) ∧
      (∫ u, q u ∂gamma) = 1 ∧
      ContDiff ℝ 2 f ∧ MemLp f 2 gamma ∧
      MemLp (gradient f) 2 gamma ∧ MemLp (gradient rho) 2 r ∧
      Integrable (fun u =&gt; q u*Real.log (q u)) gamma ∧
      (∀ u, gradient f u = -(f u/2) • gradient rho u) ∧
      (∀ u, gradient (fun z =&gt; Real.log (q z)) u = -gradient rho u) ∧
      (∫ u, ‖gradient f u‖^2 ∂gamma) =
        (1/4 : ℝ)*(∫ u, ‖gradient rho u‖^2 ∂r) := by
  let gamma := stdGaussian E
  let Z := ∫ u, Real.exp (-rho u) ∂gamma
  let q := fun u =&gt; Real.exp (-rho u)/Z
  let f := fun u =&gt; Real.exp (-rho u/2)/Real.sqrt Z
  let r := gamma.tilted (fun u =&gt; -rho u)
  obtain ⟨hn,hg⟩ := rho_nonneg_and_gradient_growth hrho hrho0 hgrad0 hH
  obtain ⟨hw,hZ,hZone⟩ := weight_normalization hrho.continuous hn
  obtain ⟨hf,hfq,hdf,hdlog⟩ := density_calculus hrho hZ
  have hprob : IsProbabilityMeasure r := isProbabilityMeasure_tilted hw
  have hq : Integrable q gamma := hw.div_const Z
  have hqone : (∫ u, q u ∂gamma) = 1 := by
    rw [integral_div]
    exact div_self hZ.ne&#x27;
  have hflp : MemLp f 2 gamma := by
    apply (memLp_two_iff_integrable_sq_norm hf.continuous.aestronglyMeasurable).mpr
    apply hq.congr
    exact ae_of_all _ (fun u =&gt; by
      change q u = ‖f u‖^2
      rw [Real.norm_eq_abs,sq_abs]
      exact (hfq u).2.symm)
  have hwg := weighted_gradient_square_integrable hrho hn hg
  have hgc := Analysis.Calculus.Gradient.continuous_gradient_of_contDiff_one
    (hrho.of_le (by norm_num))
  have hir : Integrable (fun u =&gt; ‖gradient rho u‖^2) r :=
    (integrable_tilted_iff hw (fun u =&gt; ‖gradient rho u‖^2)).mpr
      (by simpa only [smul_eq_mul] using hwg)
  have hrlp : MemLp (gradient rho) 2 r :=
    (memLp_two_iff_integrable_sq_norm hgc.aestronglyMeasurable).mpr hir
  have hpoint (u : E) : ‖gradient f u‖^2 =
      (1/(4*Z))*(Real.exp (-rho u)*‖gradient rho u‖^2) := by
    rw [hdf u,norm_smul,mul_pow,Real.norm_eq_abs,sq_abs,neg_sq,div_pow,(hfq u).2]
    change (Real.exp (-rho u)/Z)/(2:ℝ)^2*‖gradient rho u‖^2 = _
    ring
  have hfg : Integrable (fun u =&gt; ‖gradient f u‖^2) gamma :=
    (hwg.const_mul (1/(4*Z))).congr (ae_of_all _ (fun u =&gt; (hpoint u).symm))
  have hfgc := Analysis.Calculus.Gradient.continuous_gradient_of_contDiff_one
    (hf.of_le (by norm_num))
  have hfglp : MemLp (gradient f) 2 gamma :=
    (memLp_two_iff_integrable_sq_norm hfgc.aestronglyMeasurable).mpr hfg
  have he := entropy_integrable hrho.continuous hn hZ
  have henergy : (∫ u, ‖gradient f u‖^2 ∂gamma) =
      (1/4 : ℝ)*(∫ u, ‖gradient rho u‖^2 ∂r) := by
    rw [integral_tilted,← integral_const_mul]
    apply integral_congr_ae
    exact ae_of_all _ (fun u =&gt; by
      change ‖gradient f u‖^2 = (1/4 : ℝ)*(Real.exp (-rho u)/Z) • ‖gradient rho u‖^2
      rw [hpoint u]
      simp only [smul_eq_mul]
      ring)
  exact ⟨hZ,hZone,actual_gaussian_tilt_volume rho,rfl,hprob,hfq,hqone,hf,hflp,
    hfglp,hrlp,he,hdf,hdlog,henergy⟩

end
end AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.GaussianSqrtDensityDomain</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-measure-gaussiansqrtdensitydomain.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details></section><section data-source-comparison="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.GaussianSqrtDensityDomain.gaussian_sqrt_density_domain"><h3>Source assumptions versus formal assumptions</h3><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Actual Lean</th><th>Difference kind</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Standing normalized C2/curvature and eta&gt;0 on finite Euclidean space.</td><td>Complete finite-dimensional real Hilbert E with canonical volume and Borel structure; dimension zero included.
Actual global C2 rho, rho(0)=0, gradientrho(0)=0 and Hessian0..L for NNReal L including0. Probability/positive normalizer/entropy/gradient and L2 domains are conclusions.</td><td>source-implicit</td><td>Convex normalized rho hypotheses are internally derived from unchanged source V for the actual consumer; no domain certificate enters public source inputs.</td></tr><tr><td>S4.Ex8 and implicit density domains before GaussianLSI/T2.</td><td>For the actual covariance-identity Gaussian tilted by the normalized convex C2 residual, produce its positive finite partition, literal normalized positive density and C2 square root, actual volume Gibbs equality and probability, f and gradientf L2gamma, gradientrho L2r, entropy L1, explicit derivative identities and exact one-quarter Dirichlet-Fisher energy.</td><td>source-implicit</td><td>Supply missing actual explicit-representative regularity and finite integrals as producer conclusions, without asserting GaussianLSI/T2 or changing source laws.</td></tr><tr><td>Covariance identity Gaussian and Euclidean energy.</td><td>Actual stdGaussian with canonical real Hilbert gradient, exact1/4.</td><td>same</td><td>No sup-norm coordinate Fisher replacement.</td></tr><tr><td>FIRST4.6 W2/LSI/T2, weakSobolev/KL identification, bias4.2, algorithms/main/work/composition.</td><td>Actual positive Gaussian relative-density and classical C2/L2 square-root/entropy/log-gradient/Dirichlet=Fisher/4 domains, genuinely consumed by the source standardized true RGO. No Gaussian LSI/T2/KL weak representative/Sobolev adapter/W2/bias/fullLemma/main/work/composition credit. Focused3748 passed with E0/L0 and actual eta2y3 posterior tests, standard3 axioms only. Independent proof/blind/source/commit review and integration pending; postmerge purification, renderedQA and Copy/Download remain open.</td><td>unresolved</td><td>This packet only supplies classical actual-density and energy domains; no inequality, algorithm or paper completion.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Actual positive Gaussian relative-density and classical C2/L2 square-root/entropy/log-gradient/Dirichlet=Fisher/4 domains, genuinely consumed by the source standardized true RGO. No Gaussian LSI/T2/KL weak representative/Sobolev adapter/W2/bias/fullLemma/main/work/composition credit. Focused3748 passed with E0/L0 and actual eta2y3 posterior tests, standard3 axioms only. Independent proof/blind/source/commit review and integration pending; postmerge purification, renderedQA and Copy/Download remain open.</p><p><strong>Encoder–denoiser:</strong> accepted · equivalent-after-elaboration</p><p>A generalization is not a source correction. Proposed missing conditions require separate independent repair review. No proposed repair silently changes the original theorem.</p></section><details class="reader-ledger boundary-ledger"><summary>Scope and omitted-condition boundaries</summary><ul><li>Actual positive Gaussian relative-density and classical C2/L2 square-root/entropy/log-gradient/Dirichlet=Fisher/4 domains, genuinely consumed by the source standardized true RGO. No Gaussian LSI/T2/KL weak representative/Sobolev adapter/W2/bias/fullLemma/main/work/composition credit. Focused3748 passed with E0/L0 and actual eta2y3 posterior tests, standard3 axioms only. Independent proof/blind/source/commit review and integration pending; postmerge purification, renderedQA and Copy/Download remain open.</li></ul></details><details class="proof-reader-provenance reader-ledger"><summary>Sources, dependencies, and reuse</summary><h2>Source and reuse</h2><h3>Samplinglib parents called</h3><ul><li><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-quadraticregularization.html#decl-autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-quadraticregularization-stro-17eea540ae"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.QuadraticRegularization.strongConvexOn_and_lipschitzWith_gradient_add_quadratic</code></a></li><li><a href="../../../theorems/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-strongconvexfirstorder-firstorder-lower-bound-of-strongconvexon.html"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.StrongConvexFirstOrder.firstOrder_lower_bound_of_strongConvexOn</code></a></li><li><a href="../../../theorems/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-calculus-gradient-continuous-gradient-of-contdiff-one.html"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.Calculus.Gradient.continuous_gradient_of_contDiff_one</code></a></li><li><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-measure-isotropicgaussiandensity.html#decl-autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-measure-isotropicgaussiandensity-map--958a008371"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.IsotropicGaussianDensity.map_sqrt_smul_stdGaussian_eq_withDensity</code></a></li><li><a href="../../../theorems/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-probability-stdgaussianmoment-integrable-norm-sq-and-integral-stdgaussian.html"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.StdGaussianMoment.integrable_norm_sq_and_integral_stdGaussian</code></a></li></ul><h3>Mathlib API called (external library)</h3><ul><li>MeasureTheory.isProbabilityMeasure_tilted</li><li>MeasureTheory.integrable_tilted_iff</li><li>MeasureTheory.integral_tilted</li><li>MeasureTheory.memLp_two_iff_integrable_sq_norm</li><li>Real.log_div</li><li>Real.add_one_le_exp</li></ul><h3>Mathematical sources</h3><ul><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.Ex8">Actual source residual and omitted analytic prerequisites</a> — Actual positive Gaussian relative-density and classical C2/L2 square-root/entropy/log-gradient/Dirichlet=Fisher/4 domains, genuinely consumed by the source standardized true RGO. No Gaussian LSI/T2/KL weak representative/Sobolev adapter/W2/bias/fullLemma/main/work/composition credit. Focused3748 passed with E0/L0 and actual eta2y3 posterior tests, standard3 axioms only. Independent proof/blind/source/commit review and integration pending; postmerge purification, renderedQA and Copy/Download remain open.</li></ul><p>Samplinglib prose is not a quotation or a source-equivalence certificate. Definitions and aliases are explained as constructions, not counted as new mathematical proofs.</p></details></article><h3>Which proof obligations and prerequisites are covered?</h3><ul><li><span class="status status-orange">Formalized proof edge; source adapter/review separate</span> Actual relative density, square-root/entropy and finite gradient energy domains</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> ActualLSI/T2/KL/weakSobolev/W2/bias/algorithm/work/main/composition remain open</li></ul></section><section id="gibbs-covariance-lower" data-publication-item="gibbs-covariance-lower"><h2>Actual Gibbs covariance lower</h2><p>Fan Chen, Sinho Chewi, Jianfeng Lu, Matthew S. Zhang; ASTIS original restatement of the Cramer-Rao half, with explicit alpha/beta and eta generalizations. Auxiliary author book Theorem3.5.8/Cor3.5.9, pinned 20261006 SHA2568818e8fb40c07bd70651bafe10e8ec4500197c836928330b90025ca47da52fae.</p><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.SS1">arXiv:2609.06906v1 · SPHMC arXiv:2609.06906v1 Section4.1 Lemma4.1 Cramer-Rao half; equation4.1; Chewi auxiliary 20261006 main.pdf Theorem3.5.8/Corollary3.5.9 zero-based pages124-125</a> · faithful paraphrase</p><h3>Complete source statement (Samplinglib restatement)</h3><p>The actual Gibbs mu=volume.tilted(-U) is probability and id belongs to L2(mu). For every direction v, covarianceBilin(mu)(v,v)&gt;=norm(v)^2/beta. This is the linear-observable covariance corollary of Cramer-Rao, not the full nonlinear-observable theorem or a matrix-inverse result.</p><ul><li>E is a complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel measurable space and canonical volume, including dimension zero. alpha,beta are NNReal, 0&lt;alpha&lt;=beta. U or V is genuinely C2; actual Hessian at every point and direction lies between alpha*norm(v)^2 and beta*norm(v)^2.</li><li>Every Gibbs law is the actual volume.tilted(-potential). Probability, finite second moments, score integrability, centered score pairing and covariance bounds are derived. No minimizer, finite input moments, partition constant, derivative/score/covariance or well-behaved certificate is supplied.</li></ul><h3>Actual Gibbs covariance lower</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\mu(dx)=Z_U^{-1}e^{-U(x)}dx,\quad X\in L^2(\mu),\quad \forall v,\ \operatorname{Cov}_{\mu}(\langle v,X\rangle,\langle v,X\rangle)\ge\beta^{-1}\|v\|^2.\]</div><h2>Read the formalized proofs</h2><p>Each statement and proof below has its own closed Lean disclosure. Samplinglib parents, Mathlib calls and external mathematical sources are distinguished in each proof.</p><article class="proof-reader" data-authored-declaration="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GibbsGradientMoment.gibbs_covariance_lower"><div class="eyebrow">Samplinglib mathematical lesson</div><h2>Actual Gibbs covariance lower</h2><p><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GibbsGradientMoment.gibbs_covariance_lower</code> · theorem · <a href="../../../lessons/index.html">Teaching coverage</a></p><section class="reader-first-pass" aria-label="First reading"><div class="reader-first-pass-label">Read this first</div><h2>Mathematical statement</h2><p class="reader-claim">The actual Gibbs mu=volume.tilted(-U) is probability and id belongs to L2(mu). For every direction v, covarianceBilin(mu)(v,v)&gt;=norm(v)^2/beta. This is the linear-observable covariance corollary of Cramer-Rao, not the full nonlinear-observable theorem or a matrix-inverse result.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation reader-main-equation">\[\mu(dx)=Z_U^{-1}e^{-U(x)}dx,\quad X\in L^2(\mu),\quad \forall v,\ \operatorname{Cov}_{\mu}(\langle v,X\rangle,\langle v,X\rangle)\ge\beta^{-1}\|v\|^2.\]</div><h3>Proof roadmap</h3><ol class="proof-roadmap"><li><span>1</span><strong>Derive actual Gibbs probability and score second moment</strong><p>The existing canonical Gibbs gradient theorem derives the positive integrable exponential weight and actual score-square integrability from C2 Hessian bounds, using genuine weighted domination and full-space score integration by parts. Reuse that producer, without supplying a probability or moment certificate.</p></li><li><span>2</span><strong>Derive position L2 without a minimizer</strong><p>Strong gradient monotonicity between zero and x gives alpha norm(x)&lt;=norm(gradient U(x))+norm(gradient U(0)). Square this and dominate by the integrable score square plus a constant under the actual probability law. Thus id is genuinely L2 and its Bochner mean exists. Zero norm is handled separately.</p></li><li><span>3</span><strong>Prove centered linear-score pairing with all IBP products integrable</strong><p>For a unit direction v put p=E_mu X, q(x)=inner(x-p,v), a(x)=DU(x)[v]. Actual position and score L2 imply q, a, qa are integrable as required. The full-space integration-by-parts API is applied only after deriving the weighted q, weight times derivative q, and derivative weight times q integrability. Since Dq[v]=1 and D(exp(-U))[v]=-exp(-U)a, normalize by the true positive Z_U to obtain E_mu qa=1. No mean-score-zero hypothesis is needed.</p></li><li><span>4</span><strong>Bound the actual directional score second moment</strong><p>The existing genuine score IBP identifies the weighted score square with the actual Hessian directional integral. The upper Hessian beta bound and probability normalization give E_mu a^2&lt;=beta. All score-square, Hessian and product integrability are proved before integral algebra.</p></li><li><span>5</span><strong>Integrate an actual nonnegative square</strong><p>Expand the integrable nonnegative square (a-beta q)^2. Combining E qa=1 with E a^2&lt;=beta yields beta^2 E q^2&gt;=beta. Positivity of beta follows from 0&lt;alpha&lt;=beta, so divide to get variance&gt;=1/beta. Actual covarianceBilin is identified with the centered q square using proved id L2.</p></li><li><span>6</span><strong>Scale every direction and include dimension zero</strong><p>Handle v=0 directly. For v nonzero use u=v/norm(v), apply the unit-direction result and use the true covariance bilinear map scaling. This quantifies all v without assuming a unit vector exists, so dimension zero is included. This does not establish general nonlinear Cramer-Rao or any Brascamp-Lieb upper covariance.</p></li></ol></section><details class="reader-ledger assumption-ledger"><summary>Full assumptions and notation</summary><h3>All objects and hypotheses</h3><ul><li>E is a complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel measurable space and canonical volume, including dimension zero. alpha,beta are NNReal, 0&lt;alpha&lt;=beta. U or V is genuinely C2; actual Hessian at every point and direction lies between alpha*norm(v)^2 and beta*norm(v)^2.</li><li>Every Gibbs law is the actual volume.tilted(-potential). Probability, finite second moments, score integrability, centered score pairing and covariance bounds are derived. No minimizer, finite input moments, partition constant, derivative/score/covariance or well-behaved certificate is supplied.</li></ul><p class="muted"></p></details><h2>Mathematical proof</h2><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>1. Derive actual Gibbs probability and score second moment</h4><p>The existing canonical Gibbs gradient theorem derives the positive integrable exponential weight and actual score-square integrability from C2 Hessian bounds, using genuine weighted domination and full-space score integration by parts. Reuse that producer, without supplying a probability or moment certificate.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[0&lt;Z_U&lt;\infty,\quad \mu\text{ probability},\quad\mathbb E_\mu\|\nabla U\|^2\le\beta d.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>gibbs_gradient_moment; weighted_gradient; directional_ibp</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>2. Derive position L2 without a minimizer</h4><p>Strong gradient monotonicity between zero and x gives alpha norm(x)&lt;=norm(gradient U(x))+norm(gradient U(0)). Square this and dominate by the integrable score square plus a constant under the actual probability law. Thus id is genuinely L2 and its Bochner mean exists. Zero norm is handled separately.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\|x\|^2\le\frac{2}{\alpha^2}(\|\nabla U(x)\|^2+\|\nabla U(0)\|^2),\quad X\in L^2(\mu).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>gibbs_position_l2; StrongConvexFirstOrder.gradient_inner_lower_bound_of_strongConvexOn; memLp_two_iff_integrable_sq_norm</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>3. Prove centered linear-score pairing with all IBP products integrable</h4><p>For a unit direction v put p=E_mu X, q(x)=inner(x-p,v), a(x)=DU(x)[v]. Actual position and score L2 imply q, a, qa are integrable as required. The full-space integration-by-parts API is applied only after deriving the weighted q, weight times derivative q, and derivative weight times q integrability. Since Dq[v]=1 and D(exp(-U))[v]=-exp(-U)a, normalize by the true positive Z_U to obtain E_mu qa=1. No mean-score-zero hypothesis is needed.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\mathbb E_\mu[q a]=1,\quad q=\langle X-\mathbb EX,v\rangle,\quad a=DU(X)[v],\quad\|v\|=1.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>coordinate_position_ibp; integral_mul_fderiv_eq_neg_fderiv_mul_of_integrable; integrable_tilted_iff; integral_tilted</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>4. Bound the actual directional score second moment</h4><p>The existing genuine score IBP identifies the weighted score square with the actual Hessian directional integral. The upper Hessian beta bound and probability normalization give E_mu a^2&lt;=beta. All score-square, Hessian and product integrability are proved before integral algebra.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\mathbb E_\mu a^2=\mathbb E_\mu D^2U(X)[v,v]\le\beta.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>directional_ibp; integral_mono; integral_tilted; gibbs_covariance_unit</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>5. Integrate an actual nonnegative square</h4><p>Expand the integrable nonnegative square (a-beta q)^2. Combining E qa=1 with E a^2&lt;=beta yields beta^2 E q^2&gt;=beta. Positivity of beta follows from 0&lt;alpha&lt;=beta, so divide to get variance&gt;=1/beta. Actual covarianceBilin is identified with the centered q square using proved id L2.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[0\le\mathbb E(a-\beta q)^2=\mathbb Ea^2-2\beta+\beta^2\mathbb Eq^2\le-\beta+\beta^2\mathbb Eq^2\ \Longrightarrow\ \operatorname{Cov}_\mu(v,v)\ge\beta^{-1}.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>gibbs_covariance_unit; integral_add; integral_sub; covarianceBilin_apply</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>6. Scale every direction and include dimension zero</h4><p>Handle v=0 directly. For v nonzero use u=v/norm(v), apply the unit-direction result and use the true covariance bilinear map scaling. This quantifies all v without assuming a unit vector exists, so dimension zero is included. This does not establish general nonlinear Cramer-Rao or any Brascamp-Lieb upper covariance.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\operatorname{Cov}_\mu(v,v)=\|v\|^2\operatorname{Cov}_\mu(v/\|v\|,v/\|v\|)\ge\|v\|^2/\beta.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>gibbs_covariance_lower; map_smul; smul_apply</p></details></div><section class="lean-verification"><h2>Lean verification</h2><p>The exact proposition and proof begin folded so the mathematical argument remains the primary reading path.</p><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-statement" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GibbsGradientMoment.gibbs_covariance_lower"><summary>Lean statement · gibbs_covariance_lower</summary><p>The actual Gibbs mu=volume.tilted(-U) is probability and id belongs to L2(mu). For every direction v, covarianceBilin(mu)(v,v)&gt;=norm(v)^2/beta. This is the linear-observable covariance corollary of Cramer-Rao, not the full nonlinear-observable theorem or a matrix-inverse result.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem gibbs_covariance_lower [CompleteSpace E] {U : E → ℝ} {α β : ℝ≥0}
    (hα : 0 &lt; α) (hαβ : α ≤ β) (hU : ContDiff ℝ 2 U)
    (hH : ∀ x v : E, (α : ℝ)*‖v‖^2 ≤ fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ U) x v v ∧
      fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ U) x v v ≤ (β : ℝ)*‖v‖^2) :
    let μ := (volume : Measure E).tilted (fun x =&gt; -U x)
    IsProbabilityMeasure μ ∧ MemLp id 2 μ ∧
      ∀ v : E, ‖v‖^2/(β:ℝ) ≤ ProbabilityTheory.covarianceBilin μ v v</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-gibbsgradientmoment.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-proof" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GibbsGradientMoment.gibbs_covariance_lower"><summary>Lean proof · gibbs_covariance_lower</summary><p>Actual Gibbs integrability and score L2, position L2 via gradient monotonicity, centered-linear and score IBP, nonnegative scalar square, bilinear scaling; actual quadratic posterior and exact source potential Hessian adapter.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem gibbs_covariance_lower [CompleteSpace E] {U : E → ℝ} {α β : ℝ≥0}
    (hα : 0 &lt; α) (hαβ : α ≤ β) (hU : ContDiff ℝ 2 U)
    (hH : ∀ x v : E, (α : ℝ)*‖v‖^2 ≤ fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ U) x v v ∧
      fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ U) x v v ≤ (β : ℝ)*‖v‖^2) :
    let μ := (volume : Measure E).tilted (fun x =&gt; -U x)
    IsProbabilityMeasure μ ∧ MemLp id 2 μ ∧
      ∀ v : E, ‖v‖^2/(β:ℝ) ≤ ProbabilityTheory.covarianceBilin μ v v := by
  let μ := (volume : Measure E).tilted (fun x =&gt; -U x)
  have hp := gibbs_position_l2 hα hαβ hU hH
  refine ⟨hp.1,hp.2,?_⟩
  intro v
  by_cases hvzero : v=0
  · subst v
    simp
  · have hn : ‖v‖≠0 := norm_ne_zero_iff.mpr hvzero
    let u := ‖v‖⁻¹ • v
    have hu : ‖u‖=1 := by
      dsimp only [u]
      rw [norm_smul,Real.norm_eq_abs,abs_inv,abs_of_nonneg (norm_nonneg v),inv_mul_cancel₀ hn]
    have hv : ‖v‖ • u = v := by
      dsimp only [u]
      rw [smul_smul,mul_inv_cancel₀ hn,one_smul]
    have hc := gibbs_covariance_unit hα hαβ hU hH u hu
    have hscale : ProbabilityTheory.covarianceBilin μ v v =
        ‖v‖^2*ProbabilityTheory.covarianceBilin μ u u := by
      calc
        ProbabilityTheory.covarianceBilin μ v v =
            ProbabilityTheory.covarianceBilin μ (‖v‖ • u) (‖v‖ • u) := by rw [hv]
        _ = _ := by
          simp only [map_smul,smul_apply,smul_eq_mul]
          ring
    rw [hscale,div_eq_mul_inv]
    exact mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_left hc (sq_nonneg _)


end AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GibbsGradientMoment</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-gibbsgradientmoment.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details></section><section data-source-comparison="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GibbsGradientMoment.gibbs_covariance_lower"><h3>Source assumptions versus formal assumptions</h3><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Actual Lean</th><th>Difference kind</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Source V has kappa^-1 I&lt;=Hess V&lt;=I, 0&lt;eta&lt;=1; Euclidean space.</td><td>E is a complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel measurable space and canonical volume, including dimension zero. alpha,beta are NNReal, 0&lt;alpha&lt;=beta. U or V is genuinely C2; actual Hessian at every point and direction lies between alpha*norm(v)^2 and beta*norm(v)^2.
Every Gibbs law is the actual volume.tilted(-potential). Probability, finite second moments, score integrability, centered score pairing and covariance bounds are derived. No minimizer, finite input moments, partition constant, derivative/score/covariance or well-behaved certificate is supplied.</td><td>generalization</td><td>Actual arbitrary positive alpha&lt;=beta and eta&gt;0 yield the exact source upper constant for beta=1. Finite-dimensional Borel Hilbert incl dimension zero. All hidden IBP/moment/normalization conditions proved, no source assumption added.</td></tr><tr><td>Cramer-Rao covariance lower is invoked in the upper-Hessian half of Lemma4.1. Author book Theorem3.5.8 proves an arbitrary smooth-observable inequality under well-behaved regularity.</td><td>The actual Gibbs mu=volume.tilted(-U) is probability and id belongs to L2(mu). For every direction v, covarianceBilin(mu)(v,v)&gt;=norm(v)^2/beta. This is the linear-observable covariance corollary of Cramer-Rao, not the full nonlinear-observable theorem or a matrix-inverse result.</td><td>same</td><td>Prove only the linear-observable covariance corollary and its real source consumer. Direct centered-linear score pairing plus nonnegative scalar square avoids a matrix inverse and mean-score assumption; all integrability derived. Full nonlinear Cramer-Rao and BL are separate.</td></tr><tr><td>Lemma4.1 has two-sided curvature.</td><td>Actual Gibbs all-direction covariance lower bound and its actual RGO/source V_eta Hessian upper consumer only. No Brascamp-Lieb covariance upper/Hessian lower, full Lemma4.1, general nonlinear Cramer-Rao, higher regularity, exact stationarity, approximate/stochastic/history work, PBPS process or either complete main/composition. TV never transfers unbounded expected cost.</td><td>unresolved</td><td>Only covariance lower/Hessian upper is established; other half requires a genuine BL producer.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Actual Gibbs all-direction covariance lower bound and its actual RGO/source V_eta Hessian upper consumer only. No Brascamp-Lieb covariance upper/Hessian lower, full Lemma4.1, general nonlinear Cramer-Rao, higher regularity, exact stationarity, approximate/stochastic/history work, PBPS process or either complete main/composition. TV never transfers unbounded expected cost.</p><p><strong>Encoder–denoiser:</strong> accepted · equivalent-after-elaboration</p><p>A generalization is not a source correction. Proposed missing conditions require separate independent repair review. No proposed repair silently changes the original theorem.</p></section><details class="reader-ledger boundary-ledger"><summary>Scope and omitted-condition boundaries</summary><ul><li>Actual Gibbs all-direction covariance lower bound and its actual RGO/source V_eta Hessian upper consumer only. No Brascamp-Lieb covariance upper/Hessian lower, full Lemma4.1, general nonlinear Cramer-Rao, higher regularity, exact stationarity, approximate/stochastic/history work, PBPS process or either complete main/composition. TV never transfers unbounded expected cost.</li></ul></details><details class="proof-reader-provenance reader-ledger"><summary>Sources, dependencies, and reuse</summary><h2>Source and reuse</h2><h3>Samplinglib parents called</h3><ul><li><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-gibbsgradientmoment.html#decl-autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-gibbsgradientmoment-gibbs-gr-45d2759e97"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GibbsGradientMoment.gibbs_gradient_moment</code></a></li><li><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-quadraticregularization.html#decl-autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-quadraticregularization-stro-17eea540ae"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.QuadraticRegularization.strongConvexOn_and_lipschitzWith_gradient_add_quadratic</code></a></li><li><a href="../../../theorems/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-strongconvexfirstorder-gradient-inner-lower-bound-of-strongconvexon.html"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.StrongConvexFirstOrder.gradient_inner_lower_bound_of_strongConvexOn</code></a></li></ul><h3>Mathlib API called (external library)</h3><ul><li>integral_mul_fderiv_eq_neg_fderiv_mul_of_integrable</li><li>integrable_tilted_iff</li><li>integral_tilted</li><li>memLp_two_iff_integrable_sq_norm</li><li>memLp_two_iff_integrable_sq</li><li>covarianceBilin_apply</li><li>integral_add</li><li>integral_sub</li><li>integral_const_mul</li><li>map_smul</li><li>smul_apply</li></ul><h3>Mathematical sources</h3><ul><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.SS1">SPHMC Lemma4.1 Cramer-Rao half</a> — Actual Gibbs all-direction covariance lower bound and its actual RGO/source V_eta Hessian upper consumer only. No Brascamp-Lieb covariance upper/Hessian lower, full Lemma4.1, general nonlinear Cramer-Rao, higher regularity, exact stationarity, approximate/stochastic/history work, PBPS process or either complete main/composition. TV never transfers unbounded expected cost.</li><li><a href="https://chewisinho.github.io/main.pdf">Auxiliary pinned author book Theorem3.5.8 / Corollary3.5.9</a> — Linear-observable corollary only; genuine hidden regularity proved; not a Lean dependency.</li></ul><p>Samplinglib prose is not a quotation or a source-equivalence certificate. Definitions and aliases are explained as constructions, not counted as new mathematical proofs.</p></details></article><h3>Which proof obligations and prerequisites are covered?</h3><ul><li><span class="status status-orange">Formalized proof edge; source adapter/review separate</span> Cramer-Rao actual covariance lower and source Hessian upper</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Brascamp-Lieb covariance upper/Hessian lower (remaining)</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Higher regularity, stationarity/errors/work and both complete main/composition (remaining)</li></ul></section><section id="gibbs-gradient-mean" data-publication-item="gibbs-gradient-mean"><h2>Actual Gibbs gradient has zero mean</h2><p>ASTIS authored full-space Gibbs score cancellation background consumed by true source posterior; not a separately numbered printed theorem.</p><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.Ex9">arXiv:2609.06906v1 with pinned Mathlib db584cd6 · Section3.1 and literal unnumbered S4.Ex9 (not numbered S4.E9/(4.9)); source normalized Hessian in Section2</a> · faithful paraphrase</p><h3>Complete source statement (Samplinglib restatement)</h3><p>The actual normalized Gibbs volume tilt exp(-U) is a probability measure, its genuine vector gradient is Bochner integrable, and its mean is zero under the stated C2 positive-lower/finite-upper Hessian assumptions.</p><ul><li>Complete finite-dimensional real Hilbert E with canonical volume and Borel structure; dimension zero included.</li><li>Actual C2 potential U with positive NNReal alpha lower Hessian bound and finite NNReal beta upper bound, no alpha&lt;=beta assumption. No supplied probability, partition, minimizer, regularity/domain/IBP certificate or mean identity.</li></ul><h3>Actual Gibbs gradient has zero mean</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\mu=(dx)^{e^{-U}},\quad\mu(E)=1,\quad\nabla U\in L^1(\mu),\quad\int\nabla U\,d\mu=0.\]</div><h2>Read the formalized proofs</h2><p>Each statement and proof below has its own closed Lean disclosure. Samplinglib parents, Mathlib calls and external mathematical sources are distinguished in each proof.</p><article class="proof-reader" data-authored-declaration="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GibbsGradientMean.integrable_gradient_and_integral_eq_zero"><div class="eyebrow">Samplinglib mathematical lesson</div><h2>Actual Gibbs gradient has zero mean</h2><p><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GibbsGradientMean.integrable_gradient_and_integral_eq_zero</code> · theorem · <a href="../../../lessons/index.html">Teaching coverage</a></p><section class="reader-first-pass" aria-label="First reading"><div class="reader-first-pass-label">Read this first</div><h2>Mathematical statement</h2><p class="reader-claim">The actual normalized Gibbs volume tilt exp(-U) is a probability measure, its genuine vector gradient is Bochner integrable, and its mean is zero under the stated C2 positive-lower/finite-upper Hessian assumptions.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation reader-main-equation">\[\mu=(dx)^{e^{-U}},\quad\mu(E)=1,\quad\nabla U\in L^1(\mu),\quad\int\nabla U\,d\mu=0.\]</div><h3>Proof roadmap</h3><ol class="proof-roadmap"><li><span>1</span><strong>Produce the genuine Gibbs domains</strong><p>Internally widen the upper bound to max(alpha,beta) only when invoking the existing score-square producer. Its actual probability and gradient norm-square L1 give vector MemLp2 and Bochner L1. The shared theorem has no alpha&lt;=beta premise; the dimension-zero case is retained.</p></li><li><span>2</span><strong>Recover ordinary full-space weighted integrability</strong><p>The canonical curvature-to-strong-convexity and Gibbs integrability providers yield integrable exp(-U) against ordinary volume. The actual tilted-law equivalence converts gradient L1 into exp(-U) times gradient L1 against this same volume. Its directional inner product is integrable for every v.</p></li><li><span>3</span><strong>Integrate the derivative against the constant function</strong><p>C2 gives the ordinary Frechet chain rule Dw[v]=-w inner(gradientU,v). Apply full-space directional integration by parts to w and the constant1. All three required integrable products have been derived; the constant derivative is zero. No pointwise boundary-value or cutoff condition is assumed.</p></li><li><span>4</span><strong>Normalize and reconstruct the zero vector mean</strong><p>The canonical tilt integral divides each directional cancellation by the produced finite positive partition. Inner-product/integral commutation and real Hilbert extensionality identify the vector mean as zero. All objects use the same actual Gibbs measure.</p></li></ol></section><details class="reader-ledger assumption-ledger"><summary>Full assumptions and notation</summary><h3>All objects and hypotheses</h3><ul><li>Complete finite-dimensional real Hilbert E with canonical volume and Borel structure; dimension zero included.</li><li>Actual C2 potential U with positive NNReal alpha lower Hessian bound and finite NNReal beta upper bound, no alpha&lt;=beta assumption. No supplied probability, partition, minimizer, regularity/domain/IBP certificate or mean identity.</li></ul><p class="muted"></p></details><h2>Mathematical proof</h2><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>1. Produce the genuine Gibbs domains</h4><p>Internally widen the upper bound to max(alpha,beta) only when invoking the existing score-square producer. Its actual probability and gradient norm-square L1 give vector MemLp2 and Bochner L1. The shared theorem has no alpha&lt;=beta premise; the dimension-zero case is retained.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\bar\beta=\max(\alpha,\beta),\quad\mu\propto e^{-U}dx,\quad\nabla U\in L^2(\mu)\subset L^1(\mu).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>GibbsGradientMoment.gibbs_gradient_moment; memLp_two_iff_integrable_sq_norm; MemLp.integrable</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>2. Recover ordinary full-space weighted integrability</h4><p>The canonical curvature-to-strong-convexity and Gibbs integrability providers yield integrable exp(-U) against ordinary volume. The actual tilted-law equivalence converts gradient L1 into exp(-U) times gradient L1 against this same volume. Its directional inner product is integrable for every v.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[w=e^{-U},\quad w\in L^1(dx),\quad w\nabla U\in L^1(dx),\quad w\langle\nabla U,v\rangle\in L^1(dx).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>QuadraticRegularization.strongConvexOn_and_lipschitzWith_gradient_add_quadratic; StrongConvexGibbsIntegrability.integrable_exp_neg_of_strongConvexOn; integrable_tilted_iff; Integrable.inner_const</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>3. Integrate the derivative against the constant function</h4><p>C2 gives the ordinary Frechet chain rule Dw[v]=-w inner(gradientU,v). Apply full-space directional integration by parts to w and the constant1. All three required integrable products have been derived; the constant derivative is zero. No pointwise boundary-value or cutoff condition is assumed.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[Dw(x)[v]=-w(x)\langle\nabla U(x),v\rangle,\quad\int w\langle\nabla U,v\rangle dx=0.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>integral_mul_fderiv_eq_neg_fderiv_mul_of_integrable; fderiv_const_apply</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>4. Normalize and reconstruct the zero vector mean</h4><p>The canonical tilt integral divides each directional cancellation by the produced finite positive partition. Inner-product/integral commutation and real Hilbert extensionality identify the vector mean as zero. All objects use the same actual Gibbs measure.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\int\langle v,\nabla U\rangle d\mu=0\quad(\forall v),\qquad\int\nabla U\,d\mu=0.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>integral_tilted; integral_div; integral_eq_zero_of_forall_integral_inner_eq_zero</p></details></div><section class="lean-verification"><h2>Lean verification</h2><p>The exact proposition and proof begin folded so the mathematical argument remains the primary reading path.</p><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-statement" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GibbsGradientMean.integrable_gradient_and_integral_eq_zero"><summary>Lean statement · integrable_gradient_and_integral_eq_zero</summary><p>The actual normalized Gibbs volume tilt exp(-U) is a probability measure, its genuine vector gradient is Bochner integrable, and its mean is zero under the stated C2 positive-lower/finite-upper Hessian assumptions.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem integrable_gradient_and_integral_eq_zero
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E]
    [CompleteSpace E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] [MeasurableSpace E] [BorelSpace E]
    {U : E → ℝ} {α β : ℝ≥0} (hα : 0 &lt; α) (hU : ContDiff ℝ 2 U)
    (hH : ∀ x v : E, (α : ℝ)*‖v‖^2 ≤ fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ U) x v v ∧
      fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ U) x v v ≤ (β : ℝ)*‖v‖^2) :
    let μ := (volume : Measure E).tilted (fun x =&gt; -U x)
    IsProbabilityMeasure μ ∧ Integrable (gradient U) μ ∧
      (∫ x, gradient U x ∂μ) = 0</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-gibbsgradientmean.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-proof" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GibbsGradientMean.integrable_gradient_and_integral_eq_zero"><summary>Lean proof · integrable_gradient_and_integral_eq_zero</summary><p>Produce the genuine Gibbs domains; Recover ordinary full-space weighted integrability; Integrate the derivative against the constant function; Normalize and reconstruct the zero vector mean</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem integrable_gradient_and_integral_eq_zero
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E]
    [CompleteSpace E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] [MeasurableSpace E] [BorelSpace E]
    {U : E → ℝ} {α β : ℝ≥0} (hα : 0 &lt; α) (hU : ContDiff ℝ 2 U)
    (hH : ∀ x v : E, (α : ℝ)*‖v‖^2 ≤ fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ U) x v v ∧
      fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ U) x v v ≤ (β : ℝ)*‖v‖^2) :
    let μ := (volume : Measure E).tilted (fun x =&gt; -U x)
    IsProbabilityMeasure μ ∧ Integrable (gradient U) μ ∧
      (∫ x, gradient U x ∂μ) = 0 := by
  let μ := (volume : Measure E).tilted (fun x =&gt; -U x)
  have hupper : ∀ x v : E,
      (α : ℝ)*‖v‖^2 ≤ fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ U) x v v ∧
      fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ U) x v v ≤ ((max α β : ℝ≥0) : ℝ)*‖v‖^2 := by
    intro x v
    refine ⟨(hH x v).1,(hH x v).2.trans ?_⟩
    exact mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_right (by exact_mod_cast le_max_right α β) (sq_nonneg _)
  have hm := GibbsGradientMoment.gibbs_gradient_moment hα (le_max_left α β) hU hupper
  have hμ : IsProbabilityMeasure μ := hm.1
  let : IsProbabilityMeasure μ := hμ
  have hc : Continuous (gradient U) :=
    Calculus.Gradient.continuous_gradient_of_contDiff_one (hU.of_le (by norm_num))
  have hlp : MemLp (gradient U) 2 μ :=
    (memLp_two_iff_integrable_sq_norm hc.aestronglyMeasurable).mpr hm.2.1
  have hg : Integrable (gradient U) μ := hlp.integrable (by norm_num)
  have hd : Differentiable ℝ U := hU.differentiable (by norm_num)
  have hs := QuadraticRegularization.strongConvexOn_and_lipschitzWith_gradient_add_quadratic
    (r := 0) hU hH (0 : E)
  simp only [NNReal.coe_zero,zero_div,zero_mul,add_zero] at hs
  have hi := StrongConvexGibbsIntegrability.integrable_exp_neg_of_strongConvexOn
    (show (0 : ℝ) &lt; α from hα) hd hs.1
  have hweighted : Integrable (fun x =&gt; Real.exp (-U x) • gradient U x)
      (volume : Measure E) := (integrable_tilted_iff hi _).mp hg
  refine ⟨hμ,hg,integral_eq_zero_of_forall_integral_inner_eq_zero ℝ _ hg ?_⟩
  intro v
  change (∫ x, inner ℝ v (gradient U x) ∂μ) = 0
  rw [show (fun x =&gt; inner ℝ v (gradient U x)) =
      (fun x =&gt; inner ℝ (gradient U x) v) by funext x; exact real_inner_comm _ _]
  let f := fun x =&gt; Real.exp (-U x)
  have hf : Differentiable ℝ f := hd.neg.exp
  have hfd (x : E) : fderiv ℝ f x v = -f x*inner ℝ (gradient U x) v := by
    rw [show fderiv ℝ f x = _ from (hd x).hasFDerivAt.neg.exp.fderiv]
    simp only [smul_apply,neg_apply,smul_eq_mul,Pi.neg_apply]
    rw [← inner_gradient_left]
    dsimp only [f]
    ring
  have hwv : Integrable (fun x =&gt; f x*inner ℝ (gradient U x) v)
      (volume : Measure E) := by
    simpa only [real_inner_smul_left,f] using hweighted.inner_const (𝕜 := ℝ) v
  have hdf : Integrable (fun x =&gt; fderiv ℝ f x v*(1 : ℝ))
      (volume : Measure E) := by
    convert hwv.neg using 1
    ext x
    rw [hfd]
    change (-f x*inner ℝ (gradient U x) v)*1 = -(f x*inner ℝ (gradient U x) v)
    ring
  have hb := integral_mul_fderiv_eq_neg_fderiv_mul_of_integrable hdf
    (by simp) (by simpa only [mul_one] using hi)
    (fun x _ =&gt; hf x) (fun x _ =&gt; differentiableAt_const (1 : ℝ))
  have hzero : (∫ x, f x*inner ℝ (gradient U x) v ∂(volume : Measure E)) = 0 := by
    simpa [hfd,neg_mul,integral_neg] using hb.symm
  rw [show μ = (volume : Measure E).tilted (fun x =&gt; -U x) from rfl,integral_tilted]
  rw [show (fun x =&gt; (Real.exp (-U x)/(∫ z,Real.exp (-U z))) •
      inner ℝ (gradient U x) v) = (fun x =&gt; (f x*inner ℝ (gradient U x) v)/
        (∫ z,Real.exp (-U z))) by funext x; simp only [smul_eq_mul,f]; ring]
  rw [integral_div,hzero,zero_div]

end AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GibbsGradientMean</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-gibbsgradientmean.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details></section><section data-source-comparison="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GibbsGradientMean.integrable_gradient_and_integral_eq_zero"><h3>Source assumptions versus formal assumptions</h3><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Actual Lean</th><th>Difference kind</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Standing normalized C2/Hessian and positiveeta hypotheses; finite Euclidean realization.</td><td>Complete finite-dimensional real Hilbert E with canonical volume and Borel structure; dimension zero included.
Actual C2 potential U with positive NNReal alpha lower Hessian bound and finite NNReal beta upper bound, no alpha&lt;=beta assumption. No supplied probability, partition, minimizer, regularity/domain/IBP certificate or mean identity.</td><td>source-implicit</td><td>Generic background instantiated internally on Wy; no convenience assumptions enter the source anchor.</td></tr><tr><td>Printed source conditional expected-gradient and standardized identities.</td><td>The actual normalized Gibbs volume tilt exp(-U) is a probability measure, its genuine vector gradient is Bochner integrable, and its mean is zero under the stated C2 positive-lower/finite-upper Hessian assumptions.</td><td>same</td><td>Everywhere actual posterior and exact literal normalization are internally produced; no arbitrary conditional representative or substitute Gaussian law.</td></tr><tr><td>Source does not detail every noncompact analytic domain.</td><td>Actual Gibbs integrability, ordinary weighted fullspace IBP, genuine Gaussian convolution derivatives and same affine-law/gradient L2 parents.</td><td>source-implicit</td><td>All source-gap prerequisites appear as producer dependencies, never public theorem binders.</td></tr><tr><td>Bias4.2/first4.6W2/fullLemma and both main results/work/composition.</td><td>Only actual Gibbs gradient mean zero and source3.1/S4.Ex9 genuine smoothed-score/posterior/standardized-chain identity. No supplied means/moments/normalizers/IBP/gradient equality/selector; exact eta&gt;0. No bias4.2, FIRST4.6W2/LSI/T2, fullLemma4.2, algorithm/work/main/composition credit. Actual RGO law is not Gaussian gradient-output or HMC. Focused3751 passed, standard3 only; independent math/blind/source/exactcommit/publication/integration admission pending. RenderedQA, Copy/Download implementation and postmerge purification remain open.</td><td>unresolved</td><td>This packet is only the exact mean identity and deterministic evaluator chain.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Only actual Gibbs gradient mean zero and source3.1/S4.Ex9 genuine smoothed-score/posterior/standardized-chain identity. No supplied means/moments/normalizers/IBP/gradient equality/selector; exact eta&gt;0. No bias4.2, FIRST4.6W2/LSI/T2, fullLemma4.2, algorithm/work/main/composition credit. Actual RGO law is not Gaussian gradient-output or HMC. Focused3751 passed, standard3 only; independent math/blind/source/exactcommit/publication/integration admission pending. RenderedQA, Copy/Download implementation and postmerge purification remain open.</p><p><strong>Encoder–denoiser:</strong> accepted · equivalent-after-elaboration</p><p>A generalization is not a source correction. Proposed missing conditions require separate independent repair review. No proposed repair silently changes the original theorem.</p></section><details class="reader-ledger boundary-ledger"><summary>Scope and omitted-condition boundaries</summary><ul><li>Only actual Gibbs gradient mean zero and source3.1/S4.Ex9 genuine smoothed-score/posterior/standardized-chain identity. No supplied means/moments/normalizers/IBP/gradient equality/selector; exact eta&gt;0. No bias4.2, FIRST4.6W2/LSI/T2, fullLemma4.2, algorithm/work/main/composition credit. Actual RGO law is not Gaussian gradient-output or HMC. Focused3751 passed, standard3 only; independent math/blind/source/exactcommit/publication/integration admission pending. RenderedQA, Copy/Download implementation and postmerge purification remain open.</li></ul></details><details class="proof-reader-provenance reader-ledger"><summary>Sources, dependencies, and reuse</summary><h2>Source and reuse</h2><h3>Samplinglib parents called</h3><ul><li><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-gibbsgradientmoment.html#decl-autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-gibbsgradientmoment-gibbs-gr-45d2759e97"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GibbsGradientMoment.gibbs_gradient_moment</code></a></li><li><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-strongconvexgibbsintegrability.html#decl-autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-strongconvexgibbsintegrabili-51ed1587ae"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.StrongConvexGibbsIntegrability.integrable_exp_neg_of_strongConvexOn</code></a></li><li><a href="../../../theorems/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-calculus-gradient-continuous-gradient-of-contdiff-one.html"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.Calculus.Gradient.continuous_gradient_of_contDiff_one</code></a></li><li><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-quadraticregularization.html#decl-autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-quadraticregularization-stro-17eea540ae"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.QuadraticRegularization.strongConvexOn_and_lipschitzWith_gradient_add_quadratic</code></a></li></ul><h3>Mathlib API called (external library)</h3><ul><li>integral_mul_fderiv_eq_neg_fderiv_mul_of_integrable</li><li>MeasureTheory.integrable_tilted_iff</li><li>MeasureTheory.integral_tilted</li><li>integral_eq_zero_of_forall_integral_inner_eq_zero</li></ul><h3>Mathematical sources</h3><ul><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.Ex9">Exact source mean identity and its internal analytic background</a> — Only actual Gibbs gradient mean zero and source3.1/S4.Ex9 genuine smoothed-score/posterior/standardized-chain identity. No supplied means/moments/normalizers/IBP/gradient equality/selector; exact eta&gt;0. No bias4.2, FIRST4.6W2/LSI/T2, fullLemma4.2, algorithm/work/main/composition credit. Actual RGO law is not Gaussian gradient-output or HMC. Focused3751 passed, standard3 only; independent math/blind/source/exactcommit/publication/integration admission pending. RenderedQA, Copy/Download implementation and postmerge purification remain open.</li></ul><p>Samplinglib prose is not a quotation or a source-equivalence certificate. Definitions and aliases are explained as constructions, not counted as new mathematical proofs.</p></details></article><h3>Which proof obligations and prerequisites are covered?</h3><ul><li><span class="status status-orange">Formalized proof edge; source adapter/review separate</span> True Gibbs/posterior gradient mean identity</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Bias/transport/algorithms/main/work remain open</li></ul></section><section id="gibbs-linear-covariance-upper" data-publication-item="gibbs-linear-covariance-upper"><h2>Actual Gibbs linear covariance upper</h2><p>Fan Chen, Sinho Chewi, Jianfeng Lu, Matthew S. Zhang; canonical isotropic BOTH-curvature node.</p><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.Thmtheorem1">arXiv:2609.06906v1 · SPHMC arXiv:2609.06906v1 Sections1-3 standing beta=1/alpha=kappa^-1/RGO/smoothing and Section4.1 S4.E1, Lemma4.1 S4.Thmtheorem1, S4.Ex4-6; actual unreflected posterior covariance and source unnormalized V_eta. Primary-first Smoothed_Hessian_lower_source_preread.md.</a> · faithful paraphrase</p><h3>Complete source statement (Samplinglib restatement)</h3><p>For each direction a, the genuine covariance bilinear form of the normalized BOTH-curvature Gibbs law satisfies Cov_mu(a,a)&lt;=norma^2/m, using the actual linear observable, constant gradient and proven C1 Poincare.</p><ul><li>Finite real Hilbert/Borel full E including0, actual C2 W, true exp(-W) volume integrability and positive partition, BOTH global mI&lt;=HessW&lt;=MI with0&lt;m&lt;=M.</li><li>Genuine vector id in L2 of normalized tilted Gibbs law. This canonical node keeps the moment premise explicit to rule out totalized covariance outside its integrable domain; the actual paper consumer produces it internally. No covariance/Poincare certificate input.</li></ul><h3>Actual Gibbs linear covariance upper</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\mu=Z^{-1}e^{-W}dx,\quad mI\preceq D^2W\preceq MI,\quad X\in L^2(\mu)\Longrightarrow \operatorname{Cov}_\mu(a,a)\le \|a\|^2/m.\]</div><h2>Read the formalized proofs</h2><p>Each statement and proof below has its own closed Lean disclosure. Samplinglib parents, Mathlib calls and external mathematical sources are distinguished in each proof.</p><article class="proof-reader" data-authored-declaration="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.FunctionalInequalities.GibbsLinearCovarianceUpper.gibbs_linear_covariance_upper"><div class="eyebrow">Samplinglib mathematical lesson</div><h2>Actual Gibbs linear covariance upper</h2><p><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.FunctionalInequalities.GibbsLinearCovarianceUpper.gibbs_linear_covariance_upper</code> · theorem · <a href="../../../lessons/index.html">Teaching coverage</a></p><section class="reader-first-pass" aria-label="First reading"><div class="reader-first-pass-label">Read this first</div><h2>Mathematical statement</h2><p class="reader-claim">For each direction a, the genuine covariance bilinear form of the normalized BOTH-curvature Gibbs law satisfies Cov_mu(a,a)&lt;=norma^2/m, using the actual linear observable, constant gradient and proven C1 Poincare.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation reader-main-equation">\[\mu=Z^{-1}e^{-W}dx,\quad mI\preceq D^2W\preceq MI,\quad X\in L^2(\mu)\Longrightarrow \operatorname{Cov}_\mu(a,a)\le \|a\|^2/m.\]</div><h3>Proof roadmap</h3><ol class="proof-roadmap"><li><span>1</span><strong>Normalize the actual Gibbs measure</strong><p>True exponential integrability gives probability. The canonical moment premise is real vectorL2, rather than a certificate of a covariance estimate.</p></li><li><span>2</span><strong>Take the genuine linear observable</strong><p>The continuous linear functional f(x)=inner(a,x) is C1. Actual vectorL2 yields scalarL2 by MemLp.const_inner.</p></li><li><span>3</span><strong>Identify its actual gradient</strong><p>The real Riesz isometry and true continuous-linear derivative show gradientf is constanta. It therefore has genuine weighted vectorL2.</p></li><li><span>4</span><strong>Use the proved curvature Poincare producer</strong><p>The accepted original-gradient/C1 theorem derives actual integrability and m*variance&lt;=energy, using BOTH curvature bounds. Poincare.Satisfies is not assumed.</p></li><li><span>5</span><strong>Preserve exact normalization</strong><p>The genuine constant gradient and probability mass1 make the Dirichlet integral exactly norma^2, with no missing energy prefactor.</p></li><li><span>6</span><strong>Bridge actual covariance and variance</strong><p>covarianceBilin_self requires the real vectorL2 premise; variance_eq_integral matches the genuine centered-square definition. Divide by positivem.</p></li></ol></section><details class="reader-ledger assumption-ledger"><summary>Full assumptions and notation</summary><h3>All objects and hypotheses</h3><ul><li>Finite real Hilbert/Borel full E including0, actual C2 W, true exp(-W) volume integrability and positive partition, BOTH global mI&lt;=HessW&lt;=MI with0&lt;m&lt;=M.</li><li>Genuine vector id in L2 of normalized tilted Gibbs law. This canonical node keeps the moment premise explicit to rule out totalized covariance outside its integrable domain; the actual paper consumer produces it internally. No covariance/Poincare certificate input.</li></ul><p class="muted"></p></details><h2>Mathematical proof</h2><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>1. Normalize the actual Gibbs measure</h4><p>True exponential integrability gives probability. The canonical moment premise is real vectorL2, rather than a certificate of a covariance estimate.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\mu=Z^{-1}e^{-W}dx,\quad \mu(E)=1,\quad X\in L^2(\mu).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>isProbabilityMeasure_tilted</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>2. Take the genuine linear observable</h4><p>The continuous linear functional f(x)=inner(a,x) is C1. Actual vectorL2 yields scalarL2 by MemLp.const_inner.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[f_a(x)=\langle a,x\rangle,\quad f_a\in C^1\cap L^2(\mu).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>ContinuousLinearMap.contDiff; MemLp.const_inner</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>3. Identify its actual gradient</h4><p>The real Riesz isometry and true continuous-linear derivative show gradientf is constanta. It therefore has genuine weighted vectorL2.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\nabla f_a(x)=a,\quad\nabla f_a\in L^2(\mu).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>toDual_gradient; ContinuousLinearMap.fderiv; memLp_const</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>4. Use the proved curvature Poincare producer</h4><p>The accepted original-gradient/C1 theorem derives actual integrability and m*variance&lt;=energy, using BOTH curvature bounds. Poincare.Satisfies is not assumed.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[m\operatorname{Var}_\mu(f_a)\le\int\|\nabla f_a\|^2d\mu.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>GibbsC1Poincare.gibbs_c1_variance_poincare</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>5. Preserve exact normalization</h4><p>The genuine constant gradient and probability mass1 make the Dirichlet integral exactly norma^2, with no missing energy prefactor.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\int\|\nabla f_a\|^2d\mu=\|a\|^2.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>integral_const; probReal_univ; Poincare.dirichletEnergy</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>6. Bridge actual covariance and variance</h4><p>covarianceBilin_self requires the real vectorL2 premise; variance_eq_integral matches the genuine centered-square definition. Divide by positivem.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\operatorname{Cov}_\mu(a,a)=\operatorname{Var}_\mu(f_a)\le\|a\|^2/m.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>covarianceBilin_self; variance_eq_integral; le_div_iff₀</p></details></div><section class="lean-verification"><h2>Lean verification</h2><p>The exact proposition and proof begin folded so the mathematical argument remains the primary reading path.</p><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-statement" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.FunctionalInequalities.GibbsLinearCovarianceUpper.gibbs_linear_covariance_upper"><summary>Lean statement · gibbs_linear_covariance_upper</summary><p>For each direction a, the genuine covariance bilinear form of the normalized BOTH-curvature Gibbs law satisfies Cov_mu(a,a)&lt;=norma^2/m, using the actual linear observable, constant gradient and proven C1 Poincare.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem gibbs_linear_covariance_upper
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E]
    [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] [MeasurableSpace E] [BorelSpace E] [CompleteSpace E]
    (W : E → ℝ) (hW : ContDiff ℝ 2 W)
    (hI : Integrable (fun x =&gt; Real.exp (-W x)))
    (hZ : 0 &lt; ∫ x, Real.exp (-W x))
    (m M : ℝ) (hm : 0 &lt; m) (hmM : m ≤ M)
    (hlower : ∀ x a, m*‖a‖^2 ≤ fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ W) x a a)
    (hupper : ∀ x a, fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ W) x a a ≤ M*‖a‖^2)
    (hX : MemLp id 2 ((volume : Measure E).tilted (fun x =&gt; -W x))) :
    let μ := (volume : Measure E).tilted (fun x =&gt; -W x)
    ∀ a : E, ProbabilityTheory.covarianceBilin μ a a ≤ ‖a‖^2/m</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-functionalinequalities-gibbslinearcovarianceupper.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-proof" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.FunctionalInequalities.GibbsLinearCovarianceUpper.gibbs_linear_covariance_upper"><summary>Lean proof · gibbs_linear_covariance_upper</summary><p>Normalize the actual Gibbs measure; Take the genuine linear observable; Identify its actual gradient; Use the proved curvature Poincare producer; Preserve exact normalization; Bridge actual covariance and variance</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem gibbs_linear_covariance_upper
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E]
    [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] [MeasurableSpace E] [BorelSpace E] [CompleteSpace E]
    (W : E → ℝ) (hW : ContDiff ℝ 2 W)
    (hI : Integrable (fun x =&gt; Real.exp (-W x)))
    (hZ : 0 &lt; ∫ x, Real.exp (-W x))
    (m M : ℝ) (hm : 0 &lt; m) (hmM : m ≤ M)
    (hlower : ∀ x a, m*‖a‖^2 ≤ fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ W) x a a)
    (hupper : ∀ x a, fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ W) x a a ≤ M*‖a‖^2)
    (hX : MemLp id 2 ((volume : Measure E).tilted (fun x =&gt; -W x))) :
    let μ := (volume : Measure E).tilted (fun x =&gt; -W x)
    ∀ a : E, ProbabilityTheory.covarianceBilin μ a a ≤ ‖a‖^2/m := by
  let μ := (volume : Measure E).tilted (fun x =&gt; -W x)
  let : IsProbabilityMeasure μ := isProbabilityMeasure_tilted hI
  dsimp only
  intro a
  let f : E → ℝ := fun x =&gt; inner ℝ a x
  have hf : ContDiff ℝ 1 f := (innerSL ℝ a).contDiff
  have hp : MemLp f 2 μ := hX.const_inner a
  have hgrad : gradient f = fun _ =&gt; a := by
    funext x
    apply (toDual ℝ E).injective
    rw [toDual_gradient]
    exact (innerSL ℝ a).fderiv
  have hq : MemLp (gradient f) 2 μ := hgrad ▸ memLp_const a
  obtain ⟨_, hb⟩ := GibbsC1Poincare.gibbs_c1_variance_poincare
    W hW hI hZ m M hm hmM hlower hupper f hf hp hq
  have henergy : Poincare.dirichletEnergy μ f = ‖a‖^2 := by
    simp only [Poincare.dirichletEnergy, hgrad]
    simp
  rw [henergy] at hb
  have hcov : covarianceBilin μ a a = Poincare.variance μ f := by
    rw [covarianceBilin_self hX a, variance_eq_integral hp.aemeasurable]
    rfl
  rw [hcov]
  exact (le_div_iff₀ hm).2 (by simpa only [mul_comm] using hb)

end
end AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.FunctionalInequalities.GibbsLinearCovarianceUpper</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-functionalinequalities-gibbslinearcovarianceupper.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details></section><section data-source-comparison="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.FunctionalInequalities.GibbsLinearCovarianceUpper.gibbs_linear_covariance_upper"><h3>Source assumptions versus formal assumptions</h3><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Actual Lean</th><th>Difference kind</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Isotropic covariance estimate invoked through Brascamp-Lieb; source standing curvature beta1/alpha=kappa^-1.</td><td>Finite real Hilbert/Borel full E including0, actual C2 W, true exp(-W) volume integrability and positive partition, BOTH global mI&lt;=HessW&lt;=MI with0&lt;m&lt;=M.
Genuine vector id in L2 of normalized tilted Gibbs law. This canonical node keeps the moment premise explicit to rule out totalized covariance outside its integrable domain; the actual paper consumer produces it internally. No covariance/Poincare certificate input.</td><td>generalization</td><td>Canonical B explicitly includes actual vectorL2 and BOTH curvature, not full anisotropic BL. Source A has no added proof premise and produces genuine moments/normalizers internally.</td></tr><tr><td>Printed covariance upper invocation and reverse-Cramer-Rao lower yield both Hessian bounds.</td><td>For each direction a, the genuine covariance bilinear form of the normalized BOTH-curvature Gibbs law satisfies Cov_mu(a,a)&lt;=norma^2/m, using the actual linear observable, constant gradient and proven C1 Poincare.</td><td>source-implicit</td><td>Poincare-derived isotropic covariance route is a sufficient authored OR-route; it does not certify full BL or external book theorem.</td></tr><tr><td>Higher regularity, algorithms and both main/cost composition.</td><td>Canonical BOTH-curvature Gibbs linear covariance upper with genuine vectorL2, actual positive partition and probability normalization; not full anisotropic Brascamp-Lieb. Actual source beta=1 alpha=kappa^-1, 1&lt;=kappa, 0&lt;eta&lt;=1; genuine posterior law R_y, internally produced probability/vectorL2/positive partitions/C2 and normalized-U versus unnormalized-V_eta constant shift. Derive true covariance upper and both Hessian bounds (kappa+eta)^-1 and (1+eta)^-1, then true 1-Lipschitz gradient. Finite real Hilbert/Borel including zero dimension is disclosed representation extension. No input covariance/PI/oracle/moment/normalizer certificate on source Anchor. No joint measurable posterior kernel selector, higher smoothing derivatives, algorithm/Wp/proxy-warmness/main/querycost/composition. PBPS reflected quarter law and source-score remain separate. TV never transfers unbounded expected cost.</td><td>unresolved</td><td>Only actual analytic Lemma4.1 and its canonical covariance prerequisite are covered.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Canonical BOTH-curvature Gibbs linear covariance upper with genuine vectorL2, actual positive partition and probability normalization; not full anisotropic Brascamp-Lieb. Actual source beta=1 alpha=kappa^-1, 1&lt;=kappa, 0&lt;eta&lt;=1; genuine posterior law R_y, internally produced probability/vectorL2/positive partitions/C2 and normalized-U versus unnormalized-V_eta constant shift. Derive true covariance upper and both Hessian bounds (kappa+eta)^-1 and (1+eta)^-1, then true 1-Lipschitz gradient. Finite real Hilbert/Borel including zero dimension is disclosed representation extension. No input covariance/PI/oracle/moment/normalizer certificate on source Anchor. No joint measurable posterior kernel selector, higher smoothing derivatives, algorithm/Wp/proxy-warmness/main/querycost/composition. PBPS reflected quarter law and source-score remain separate. TV never transfers unbounded expected cost.</p><p><strong>Encoder–denoiser:</strong> accepted · equivalent-after-elaboration</p><p>A generalization is not a source correction. Proposed missing conditions require separate independent repair review. No proposed repair silently changes the original theorem.</p></section><details class="reader-ledger boundary-ledger"><summary>Scope and omitted-condition boundaries</summary><ul><li>Canonical BOTH-curvature Gibbs linear covariance upper with genuine vectorL2, actual positive partition and probability normalization; not full anisotropic Brascamp-Lieb. Actual source beta=1 alpha=kappa^-1, 1&lt;=kappa, 0&lt;eta&lt;=1; genuine posterior law R_y, internally produced probability/vectorL2/positive partitions/C2 and normalized-U versus unnormalized-V_eta constant shift. Derive true covariance upper and both Hessian bounds (kappa+eta)^-1 and (1+eta)^-1, then true 1-Lipschitz gradient. Finite real Hilbert/Borel including zero dimension is disclosed representation extension. No input covariance/PI/oracle/moment/normalizer certificate on source Anchor. No joint measurable posterior kernel selector, higher smoothing derivatives, algorithm/Wp/proxy-warmness/main/querycost/composition. PBPS reflected quarter law and source-score remain separate. TV never transfers unbounded expected cost.</li></ul></details><details class="proof-reader-provenance reader-ledger"><summary>Sources, dependencies, and reuse</summary><h2>Source and reuse</h2><h3>Samplinglib parents called</h3><ul><li><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-functionalinequalities-gibbsc1poincare.html#decl-autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-functionalinequalities-gibbsc1poincar-beafcfbb8f"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.FunctionalInequalities.GibbsC1Poincare.gibbs_c1_variance_poincare</code></a></li></ul><h3>Mathlib API called (external library)</h3><ul><li>covarianceBilin_self</li><li>variance_eq_integral</li><li>MemLp.const_inner</li><li>toDual_gradient</li><li>ContinuousLinearMap.fderiv</li><li>integral_const</li><li>le_div_iff₀</li></ul><h3>Mathematical sources</h3><ul><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.Thmtheorem1">SPHMC actual Lemma4.1 and explicit covariance prerequisite</a> — Canonical BOTH-curvature Gibbs linear covariance upper with genuine vectorL2, actual positive partition and probability normalization; not full anisotropic Brascamp-Lieb. Actual source beta=1 alpha=kappa^-1, 1&lt;=kappa, 0&lt;eta&lt;=1; genuine posterior law R_y, internally produced probability/vectorL2/positive partitions/C2 and normalized-U versus unnormalized-V_eta constant shift. Derive true covariance upper and both Hessian bounds (kappa+eta)^-1 and (1+eta)^-1, then true 1-Lipschitz gradient. Finite real Hilbert/Borel including zero dimension is disclosed representation extension. No input covariance/PI/oracle/moment/normalizer certificate on source Anchor. No joint measurable posterior kernel selector, higher smoothing derivatives, algorithm/Wp/proxy-warmness/main/querycost/composition. PBPS reflected quarter law and source-score remain separate. TV never transfers unbounded expected cost.</li></ul><p>Samplinglib prose is not a quotation or a source-equivalence certificate. Definitions and aliases are explained as constructions, not counted as new mathematical proofs.</p></details></article><h3>Which proof obligations and prerequisites are covered?</h3><ul><li><span class="status status-green">Independently verified prerequisite</span> Actual Lemma4.1 analytic bounds</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Full anisotropic BL remaining</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Both main results and actual-input expectedcost composition remaining</li></ul></section><section id="gradient-ae-congruence-obstruction" data-publication-item="gradient-ae-congruence-obstruction"><h2>Why canonical a.e. representatives do not automatically carry classical scores</h2><p>Lee--Shen--Tian differentiate an explicit smooth ratio; SPHMC cites that contraction. ASTIS records the separate formal representative-safety prerequisite; no author endorsement implied.</p><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.03106v4">arXiv:2010.03106v4, as cited by SPHMC arXiv:2609.06906v1 · Lee--Shen--Tian Lemma 2, equation (11); SPHMC Lemma 4.17</a> · faithful paraphrase</p><h3>Complete source statement (Samplinglib restatement)</h3><p>For the restricted-Gaussian fibers, the explicit pointwise normalized log-density ratio has affine score (y-y&#x27;)/eta. A faithful canonical-Fisher formalization must keep that smooth representative explicit: arbitrary almost-everywhere equality does not by itself transport classical gradients.</p><ul><li>Classical total gradients are used.</li><li>The counterexample is over the Dirac probability measure at zero.</li></ul><h3>explicit restricted-fiber score</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\nabla\log\frac{d\mu_y}{d\mu_{y&#x27;}}(x)=\frac{y-y&#x27;}{\eta}\]</div><h3>Dirac counterexample</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[f=0,\ g(x)=x,\ \mu=\delta_0:\quad f=g\ \mu\text{-a.e.},\qquad \nabla f\ne\nabla g\ \mu\text{-a.e.}\]</div><h2>Read the formalized proofs</h2><p>Each statement and proof below has its own closed Lean disclosure. Samplinglib parents, Mathlib calls and external mathematical sources are distinguished in each proof.</p><article class="proof-reader" data-authored-declaration="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GradientAECongruence.not_gradient_ae_congr_for_arbitrary_measure"><div class="eyebrow">Samplinglib mathematical lesson</div><h2>Almost-everywhere equality does not determine classical gradients</h2><p><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GradientAECongruence.not_gradient_ae_congr_for_arbitrary_measure</code> · theorem · <a href="../../../lessons/index.html">Teaching coverage</a></p><section class="reader-first-pass" aria-label="First reading"><div class="reader-first-pass-label">Read this first</div><h2>Mathematical statement</h2><p class="reader-claim">It is false that, for every measure mu and every two real functions f and g, f=g mu-almost everywhere forces their classical Mathlib gradients to agree mu-almost everywhere.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation reader-main-equation">\[\neg\,\forall \mu,f,g,\quad f=g\ \mu\text{-a.e.}\Longrightarrow \nabla f=\nabla g\ \mu\text{-a.e.}\]</div><h3>Proof roadmap</h3><ol class="proof-roadmap"><li><span>1</span><strong>Choose two representatives that agree at the atom</strong><p>Let mu be the Dirac measure at zero, f be the zero function, and g(x)=x. Almost-everywhere equality for a Dirac measure means equality at its atom, where both functions have value zero.</p></li><li><span>2</span><strong>Compute the two classical gradients</strong><p>The zero function has gradient zero. The identity function has derivative and hence gradient one at zero.</p></li><li><span>3</span><strong>Contradict the proposed a.e. gradient congruence</strong><p>If the unrestricted rule existed, applying it to these representatives would force 0=1 at the atom, a contradiction.</p></li></ol></section><details class="reader-ledger assumption-ledger"><summary>Full assumptions and notation</summary><h3>All objects and hypotheses</h3><ul><li>No regularity assumptions are imposed because the statement refutes an unrestricted congruence rule.</li><li>The counterexample uses the Dirac probability measure at zero.</li><li>The gradients are Mathlib&#x27;s total classical gradients, not Sobolev weak gradients.</li></ul><p class="muted"></p></details><h2>Mathematical proof</h2><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>1. Choose two representatives that agree at the atom</h4><p>Let mu be the Dirac measure at zero, f be the zero function, and g(x)=x. Almost-everywhere equality for a Dirac measure means equality at its atom, where both functions have value zero.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\mu=\delta_0,\qquad f(x)=0,\qquad g(x)=x,\qquad f(0)=g(0)=0.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>The proof rewrites the a.e. filter with ae_dirac_eq.</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>2. Compute the two classical gradients</h4><p>The zero function has gradient zero. The identity function has derivative and hence gradient one at zero.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\nabla f(0)=0,\qquad \nabla g(0)=1.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>gradient_fun_const and hasDerivAt_id.hasGradientAt.gradient provide the two computations.</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>3. Contradict the proposed a.e. gradient congruence</h4><p>If the unrestricted rule existed, applying it to these representatives would force 0=1 at the atom, a contradiction.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[f=g\ \delta_0\text{-a.e.}\quad\text{but}\quad\nabla f\ne\nabla g\ \delta_0\text{-a.e.}\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>The claimed gradient a.e. equality is again reduced to its value at zero with ae_dirac_eq.</p></details></div><section class="lean-verification"><h2>Lean verification</h2><p>The exact proposition and proof begin folded so the mathematical argument remains the primary reading path.</p><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-statement" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GradientAECongruence.not_gradient_ae_congr_for_arbitrary_measure"><summary>Lean statement · not_gradient_ae_congr_for_arbitrary_measure</summary><p>The theorem negates a universal implication over all real measures and all pairs of real functions. It uses an explicit Dirac counterexample rather than an axiom or meta-level warning.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem not_gradient_ae_congr_for_arbitrary_measure :
    ¬ ∀ (μ : Measure ℝ) (f g : ℝ → ℝ),
      f =ᵐ[μ] g → ∀ᵐ x ∂μ, gradient f x = gradient g x</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-gradientaecongruence.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-proof" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GradientAECongruence.not_gradient_ae_congr_for_arbitrary_measure"><summary>Lean proof · not_gradient_ae_congr_for_arbitrary_measure</summary><p>Instantiate the universal rule with delta_0, the zero function and the identity; reduce both a.e. propositions to the atom and compute the two gradients.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem not_gradient_ae_congr_for_arbitrary_measure :
    ¬ ∀ (μ : Measure ℝ) (f g : ℝ → ℝ),
      f =ᵐ[μ] g → ∀ᵐ x ∂μ, gradient f x = gradient g x := by
  intro h
  let f : ℝ → ℝ := fun _ =&gt; 0
  let g : ℝ → ℝ := fun x =&gt; x
  have hfg : f =ᵐ[Measure.dirac 0] g := by
    rw [ae_dirac_eq]
    rfl
  have hgrad := h (Measure.dirac 0) f g hfg
  have hgrad0 : gradient f 0 = gradient g 0 := by
    rw [ae_dirac_eq] at hgrad
    exact hgrad
  have hf0 : gradient f 0 = 0 := by
    simp [f]
  have hg0 : gradient g 0 = 1 := by
    exact (hasDerivAt_id (𝕜 := ℝ) 0).hasGradientAt.gradient
  rw [hf0, hg0] at hgrad0
  norm_num at hgrad0

end AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GradientAECongruence</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-gradientaecongruence.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details></section><section data-source-comparison="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.GradientAECongruence.not_gradient_ae_congr_for_arbitrary_measure"><h3>Source assumptions versus formal assumptions</h3><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Actual Lean</th><th>Difference kind</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lee--Shen--Tian differentiate an explicit pointwise smooth normalized log-density ratio and obtain (y-y&#x27;)/eta.</td><td>Mathlib&#x27;s llr is a specific Radon--Nikodym-based function, but its available identification with the explicit smooth ratio is only almost everywhere; the bound theorem rules out using that fact alone to transport classical gradients.</td><td>API-limitation</td><td>Classical derivatives depend on local pointwise behavior, not only on a measure-theoretic equivalence class.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Rules out one unsound canonical-score adapter only; no SPHMC analytic conclusion is promoted.</p><p><strong>Encoder–denoiser:</strong> accepted · equivalent-after-elaboration</p><p>A generalization is not a source correction. Proposed missing conditions require separate independent repair review. No proposed repair silently changes the original theorem.</p></section><details class="reader-ledger boundary-ledger"><summary>Scope and omitted-condition boundaries</summary><ul><li>The theorem does not say the paper&#x27;s explicit smooth score calculation is invalid.</li><li>It does not say Mathlib&#x27;s particular llr has an incorrect gradient.</li><li>It does not rule out a full-support or absolutely-continuous result under added regularity, gradient transport from pointwise/local equality, an RGO-specific adapter, or Sobolev weak gradients.</li><li>It does not prove LSI, T2, Fisher evaluation, Wasserstein contraction or a main result.</li></ul></details><details class="proof-reader-provenance reader-ledger"><summary>Sources, dependencies, and reuse</summary><h2>Source and reuse</h2><h3>Samplinglib parents called</h3><ul></ul><h3>Mathlib API called (external library)</h3><ul><li>MeasureTheory.ae_dirac_eq</li><li>gradient_fun_const</li><li>hasDerivAt_id</li><li>HasDerivAt.hasGradientAt</li></ul><h3>Mathematical sources</h3><ul><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1">Chen, Chewi, Lu and Zhang, SPHMC v1, Lemma 4.17</a> — The lemma states the restricted-Gaussian contraction and cites the prior relative-score proof; the obstruction concerns only the formal representative adapter.</li><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.03106v4">Lee, Shen and Tian, Lemma 2, equation (11)</a> — Direct source for differentiating the explicit normalized density ratio and obtaining the affine score (y-y&#x27;)/eta.</li></ul><p>Samplinglib prose is not a quotation or a source-equivalence certificate. Definitions and aliases are explained as constructions, not counted as new mathematical proofs.</p></details></article><h3>Which proof obligations and prerequisites are covered?</h3><ul><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Preserve the explicit smooth score representative rather than transporting classical gradients through arbitrary a.e. equality</li></ul></section><section id="hessian-secant-operator" data-publication-item="hessian-secant-operator"><h2>The genuine Hessian integral represents the full gradient difference</h2><p>Fan Chen, Sinho Chewi, Jianfeng Lu and Matthew S. Zhang; scoped actual Hessian secant constructor, with explicit shared Hilbert/signed generalization.</p><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.SS3">arXiv:2609.06906v1 · Lemma4.6 proof, actual H(x,xprime) and inherited curvature, HTML568-572</a> · faithful paraphrase</p><h3>Complete source statement (Samplinglib restatement)</h3><p>For every complete real inner-product space E, every globally C2 real f with signed global genuine-Hessian diagonal bounds alpha,beta, and all x,y, let phi(t) be the Riesz operator of D2f(y+t(x-y)) and H=integral_0^1 phi(t) dt in ordinary Lebesgue measure. Then phi is interval integrable, gradient f x-gradient f y=H(x-y), H is symmetric, all v satisfy alpha||v||^2&lt;=inner(Hv,v)&lt;=beta||v||^2, and ||H||op&lt;=max(|alpha|,|beta|). In finite Euclidean space this specializes to the source actual H with alpha=1/(2kappa),beta=1; V_eta smoothness/curvature must still be proved separately.</p><ul><li>E is a complete real inner-product space (a real Hilbert space), including dimension zero. f:E-&gt;R is globally C2 in the Frechet sense. x,y and every test vector range over all E.</li><li>alpha,beta are real and may be signed. For every z,v, alpha||v||^2 &lt;= D2f(z)[v,v] &lt;= beta||v||^2. These are bounds on the genuine nested Frechet derivative, not on an independently supplied operator.</li><li>H(x,y) is the ordinary Lebesgue Bochner integral on[0,1] of the Riesz-represented D2f(y+t(x-y)). Its symmetry, continuity/measurability/integrability and secant identity are conclusions derived from C2, not hypotheses. Completeness permits the Riesz identification and Bochner operator integral.</li></ul><h3>Actual Hessian secant operator</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[H(x,y)=\int_0^1[D^2f(y+t(x-y))]^{\sharp}\,dt,\quad\nabla f(x)-\nabla f(y)=H(x,y)(x-y),\quad\alpha I\preceq H\preceq\beta I,\quad\|H\|_{\rm op}\le\max(|\alpha|,|\beta|).\]</div><h2>Read the formalized proofs</h2><p>Each statement and proof below has its own closed Lean disclosure. Samplinglib parents, Mathlib calls and external mathematical sources are distinguished in each proof.</p><article class="proof-reader" data-authored-declaration="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.HessianSecantOperator.hessian_secant_operator"><div class="eyebrow">Samplinglib mathematical lesson</div><h2>The genuine Hessian integral represents the full gradient difference</h2><p><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.HessianSecantOperator.hessian_secant_operator</code> · theorem · <a href="../../../lessons/index.html">Teaching coverage</a></p><section class="reader-first-pass" aria-label="First reading"><div class="reader-first-pass-label">Read this first</div><h2>Mathematical statement</h2><p class="reader-claim">For every complete real inner-product space E, every globally C2 real f with signed global genuine-Hessian diagonal bounds alpha,beta, and all x,y, let phi(t) be the Riesz operator of D2f(y+t(x-y)) and H=integral_0^1 phi(t) dt in ordinary Lebesgue measure. Then phi is interval integrable, gradient f x-gradient f y=H(x-y), H is symmetric, all v satisfy alpha||v||^2&lt;=inner(Hv,v)&lt;=beta||v||^2, and ||H||op&lt;=max(|alpha|,|beta|). In finite Euclidean space this specializes to the source actual H with alpha=1/(2kappa),beta=1; V_eta smoothness/curvature must still be proved separately.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation reader-main-equation">\[H(x,y)=\int_0^1[D^2f(y+t(x-y))]^{\sharp}\,dt,\quad\nabla f(x)-\nabla f(y)=H(x,y)(x-y),\quad\alpha I\preceq H\preceq\beta I,\quad\|H\|_{\rm op}\le\max(|\alpha|,|\beta|).\]</div><h3>Proof roadmap</h3><ol class="proof-roadmap"><li><span>1</span><strong>Construct the actual operator and derive regularity</strong><p>Represent each genuine second derivative by the continuous Riesz map, using Mathlib continuousLinearMapOfBilin. C2 makes Df C1 and D2f continuous. Composing with the affine segment and fixed continuous Riesz map gives a continuous operator-valued phi; hence its ordinary unit-interval Bochner integral exists. No matrix or integrability certificate is supplied.</p></li><li><span>2</span><strong>Commute genuine integral evaluation and arbitrary pairing</strong><p>Continuous evaluation and the fixed continuous linear functional inner(w,.) commute with the actual Bochner integral. Real symmetry of the inner product turns this into the displayed all-vector identity. Both integral commutations use the derived interval integrability.</p></li><li><span>3</span><strong>Derive the full gradient secant identity by FTC</strong><p>Put v=x-y. For any w, differentiating t -&gt; Df(y+t v)[w] gives D2f(y+t v)[v,w]. Its continuity supplies scalar integrability. The ordinary FTC gives the difference Df(x)[w]-Df(y)[w]; Riesz identifies it with inner(gradient f x-gradient f y,w). Equality against every w yields the full vector equality, not merely its diagonal pairing.</p></li><li><span>4</span><strong>Inherit genuine symmetry</strong><p>C2 supplies symmetry of the actual second derivative. The all-vector pairing identity shows inner(Hv,w)=inner(Hw,v)=inner(v,Hw). This does not assume self-adjointness of an unrelated matrix.</p></li><li><span>5</span><strong>Integrate the actual two diagonal bounds</strong><p>For each v, integrate the constant bounds alpha||v||^2 and beta||v||^2 against ordinary Lebesgue measure on the unit interval. Integral monotonicity and hinner give the inherited inequalities. Signed moduli and dimension zero require no extra positivity or order hypothesis.</p></li><li><span>6</span><strong>Control the actual operator norm</strong><p>For the symmetric H, Mathlib identifies its norm with the supremum of absolute Rayleigh quotients. For nonzero v, the previous signed inequalities bound their absolute value by max(|alpha|,|beta|); v=0 gives zero. This proves the stated norm bound even on the zero space. In the paper alpha=1/(2kappa),beta=1,kappa&gt;=1 gives norm&lt;=1 once its curvature premises are produced.</p></li></ol></section><details class="reader-ledger assumption-ledger"><summary>Full assumptions and notation</summary><h3>All objects and hypotheses</h3><ul><li>E is a complete real inner-product space (a real Hilbert space), including dimension zero. f:E-&gt;R is globally C2 in the Frechet sense. x,y and every test vector range over all E.</li><li>alpha,beta are real and may be signed. For every z,v, alpha||v||^2 &lt;= D2f(z)[v,v] &lt;= beta||v||^2. These are bounds on the genuine nested Frechet derivative, not on an independently supplied operator.</li><li>H(x,y) is the ordinary Lebesgue Bochner integral on[0,1] of the Riesz-represented D2f(y+t(x-y)). Its symmetry, continuity/measurability/integrability and secant identity are conclusions derived from C2, not hypotheses. Completeness permits the Riesz identification and Bochner operator integral.</li></ul><p class="muted"></p></details><h2>Mathematical proof</h2><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>1. Construct the actual operator and derive regularity</h4><p>Represent each genuine second derivative by the continuous Riesz map, using Mathlib continuousLinearMapOfBilin. C2 makes Df C1 and D2f continuous. Composing with the affine segment and fixed continuous Riesz map gives a continuous operator-valued phi; hence its ordinary unit-interval Bochner integral exists. No matrix or integrability certificate is supplied.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\phi(t)=[D^2f(y+t(x-y))]^\sharp,\qquad\langle\phi(t)v,w\rangle=D^2f(y+t(x-y))[v,w].\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>hf.fderiv_right; hfd.continuous_fderiv; continuousLinearMapOfBilin; hc; hi</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>2. Commute genuine integral evaluation and arbitrary pairing</h4><p>Continuous evaluation and the fixed continuous linear functional inner(w,.) commute with the actual Bochner integral. Real symmetry of the inner product turns this into the displayed all-vector identity. Both integral commutations use the derived interval integrability.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\langle Hv,w\rangle=\int_0^1D^2f(y+t(x-y))[v,w]\,dt.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>hinner; ContinuousLinearMap.intervalIntegral_apply; innerSL; intervalIntegral_comp_comm</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>3. Derive the full gradient secant identity by FTC</h4><p>Put v=x-y. For any w, differentiating t -&gt; Df(y+t v)[w] gives D2f(y+t v)[v,w]. Its continuity supplies scalar integrability. The ordinary FTC gives the difference Df(x)[w]-Df(y)[w]; Riesz identifies it with inner(gradient f x-gradient f y,w). Equality against every w yields the full vector equality, not merely its diagonal pairing.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\int_0^1D^2f(y+t v)[v,w]dt=Df(x)[w]-Df(y)[w]=\langle\nabla f(x)-\nabla f(y),w\rangle.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>hgrad; hd; hdcont; integral_eq_sub_of_hasDerivAt; gradient; ext_inner_right</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>4. Inherit genuine symmetry</h4><p>C2 supplies symmetry of the actual second derivative. The all-vector pairing identity shows inner(Hv,w)=inner(Hw,v)=inner(v,Hw). This does not assume self-adjointness of an unrelated matrix.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[D^2f(z)[v,w]=D^2f(z)[w,v]\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\langle Hv,w\rangle=\langle v,Hw\rangle.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>hsym; hf.contDiffAt.isSymmSndFDerivAt; integral_congr; real_inner_comm</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>5. Integrate the actual two diagonal bounds</h4><p>For each v, integrate the constant bounds alpha||v||^2 and beta||v||^2 against ordinary Lebesgue measure on the unit interval. Integral monotonicity and hinner give the inherited inequalities. Signed moduli and dimension zero require no extra positivity or order hypothesis.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\alpha\|v\|^2=\int_0^1\alpha\|v\|^2dt\le\langle Hv,v\rangle\le\int_0^1\beta\|v\|^2dt=\beta\|v\|^2.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>hbound; intervalIntegral.integral_mono_on; intervalIntegrable_const; hvcont</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>6. Control the actual operator norm</h4><p>For the symmetric H, Mathlib identifies its norm with the supremum of absolute Rayleigh quotients. For nonzero v, the previous signed inequalities bound their absolute value by max(|alpha|,|beta|); v=0 gives zero. This proves the stated norm bound even on the zero space. In the paper alpha=1/(2kappa),beta=1,kappa&gt;=1 gives norm&lt;=1 once its curvature premises are produced.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\|H\|_{\rm op}=\sup_v\left|\frac{\langle Hv,v\rangle}{\|v\|^2}\right|\le\max(|\alpha|,|\beta|).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>H.norm_eq_iSup_rayleighQuotient; hbound; norm_pos_iff; abs_le; ciSup_le</p></details></div><section class="lean-verification"><h2>Lean verification</h2><p>The exact proposition and proof begin folded so the mathematical argument remains the primary reading path.</p><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-statement" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.HessianSecantOperator.hessian_secant_operator"><summary>Lean statement · hessian_secant_operator</summary><p>Exact nested genuine Frechet derivative, Riesz operator and ordinary unit-interval Bochner integral; all-vector identity and signed bounds.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem hessian_secant_operator {f : E → ℝ} {α β : ℝ}
    (hf : ContDiff ℝ 2 f)
    (hlo : ∀ z v, α * ‖v‖ ^ 2 ≤ (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ f) z v) v)
    (hup : ∀ z v, (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ f) z v) v ≤ β * ‖v‖ ^ 2)
    (x y : E) :
    let φ := fun t : ℝ =&gt; continuousLinearMapOfBilin
      (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ f) (y + t • (x - y)))
    let H := ∫ t : ℝ in 0..1, φ t
    IntervalIntegrable φ volume 0 1 ∧
      gradient f x - gradient f y = H (x - y) ∧
      H.IsSymmetric ∧
      (∀ v, α * ‖v‖ ^ 2 ≤ inner ℝ (H v) v ∧
        inner ℝ (H v) v ≤ β * ‖v‖ ^ 2) ∧
      ‖H‖ ≤ max |α| |β|</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-hessiansecantoperator.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-proof" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.HessianSecantOperator.hessian_secant_operator"><summary>Lean proof · hessian_secant_operator</summary><p>Continuous Riesz composition, operator/inner integral commutation, arbitrary-pairing FTC, genuine C2 symmetry, integral order and symmetric Rayleigh norm.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem hessian_secant_operator {f : E → ℝ} {α β : ℝ}
    (hf : ContDiff ℝ 2 f)
    (hlo : ∀ z v, α * ‖v‖ ^ 2 ≤ (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ f) z v) v)
    (hup : ∀ z v, (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ f) z v) v ≤ β * ‖v‖ ^ 2)
    (x y : E) :
    let φ := fun t : ℝ =&gt; continuousLinearMapOfBilin
      (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ f) (y + t • (x - y)))
    let H := ∫ t : ℝ in 0..1, φ t
    IntervalIntegrable φ volume 0 1 ∧
      gradient f x - gradient f y = H (x - y) ∧
      H.IsSymmetric ∧
      (∀ v, α * ‖v‖ ^ 2 ≤ inner ℝ (H v) v ∧
        inner ℝ (H v) v ≤ β * ‖v‖ ^ 2) ∧
      ‖H‖ ≤ max |α| |β| := by
  let φ := fun t : ℝ =&gt; continuousLinearMapOfBilin
    (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ f) (y + t • (x - y)))
  let H := ∫ t : ℝ in 0..1, φ t
  change IntervalIntegrable φ volume 0 1 ∧ _
  have hfd : ContDiff ℝ 1 (fderiv ℝ f) := hf.fderiv_right (by norm_num)
  have hc : Continuous φ := by
    unfold φ continuousLinearMapOfBilin
    exact continuous_const.clm_comp ((hfd.continuous_fderiv (by norm_num)).comp
      (continuous_const.add (continuous_id.smul continuous_const)))
  have hi : IntervalIntegrable φ volume 0 1 := hc.intervalIntegrable 0 1
  have hinner (v w : E) : inner ℝ (H v) w =
      ∫ t : ℝ in 0..1, (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ f) (y + t • (x - y)) v) w := by
    change inner ℝ ((∫ t : ℝ in 0..1, φ t) v) w = _
    rw [ContinuousLinearMap.intervalIntegral_apply hi v, real_inner_comm]
    change (innerSL ℝ w) (∫ t : ℝ in 0..1, φ t v) = _
    rw [← (innerSL ℝ w).intervalIntegral_comp_comm
      (((hc.clm_apply continuous_const : Continuous (fun t =&gt; φ t v))).intervalIntegrable
        (μ := volume) 0 1)]
    apply intervalIntegral.integral_congr
    intro t _
    exact (real_inner_comm w (φ t v)).symm.trans
      (continuousLinearMapOfBilin_apply _ v w)
  have hgrad : gradient f x - gradient f y = H (x - y) := by
    apply ext_inner_right ℝ
    intro w
    rw [hinner]
    have hd (t : ℝ) : HasDerivAt
        (fun s : ℝ =&gt; fderiv ℝ f (y + s • (x - y)) w)
        ((fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ f) (y + t • (x - y)) (x - y)) w) t := by
      convert ((hfd.differentiable_one _).hasFDerivAt.comp_hasDerivAt t
        (((hasDerivAt_id t).smul_const (x - y)).const_add y)).clm_apply
        (hasDerivAt_const t w) using 1 &lt;;&gt; first | rfl | simp
    have hdcont : Continuous (fun t : ℝ =&gt;
        (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ f) (y + t • (x - y)) (x - y)) w) :=
      (((hfd.continuous_fderiv (by norm_num)).comp
        (continuous_const.add (continuous_id.smul continuous_const))).clm_apply
          continuous_const).clm_apply continuous_const
    simpa [inner_sub_left, gradient, Function.comp_def] using
      (intervalIntegral.integral_eq_sub_of_hasDerivAt
        (fun t _ =&gt; hd t) (hdcont.intervalIntegrable 0 1)).symm
  have hsym : H.IsSymmetric := by
    intro v w
    change inner ℝ (H v) w = inner ℝ v (H w)
    have hw : inner ℝ v (H w) =
        ∫ t : ℝ in 0..1, (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ f) (y + t • (x - y)) w) v :=
      (real_inner_comm v (H w)).symm.trans (hinner w v)
    rw [hinner, hw]
    apply intervalIntegral.integral_congr
    intro t _
    exact hf.contDiffAt.isSymmSndFDerivAt (by norm_num) v w
  have hbound (v : E) : α * ‖v‖ ^ 2 ≤ inner ℝ (H v) v ∧
      inner ℝ (H v) v ≤ β * ‖v‖ ^ 2 := by
    have hvcont : Continuous (fun t : ℝ =&gt;
        (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ f) (y + t • (x - y)) v) v) :=
      (((hfd.continuous_fderiv (by norm_num)).comp
        (continuous_const.add (continuous_id.smul continuous_const))).clm_apply
          continuous_const).clm_apply continuous_const
    rw [hinner]
    constructor
    · simpa using intervalIntegral.integral_mono_on (by norm_num : (0 : ℝ) ≤ 1)
        (intervalIntegrable_const : IntervalIntegrable (fun _ : ℝ =&gt; α * ‖v‖ ^ 2) volume 0 1)
        (hvcont.intervalIntegrable 0 1) (fun t _ =&gt; hlo _ v)
    · simpa using intervalIntegral.integral_mono_on (by norm_num : (0 : ℝ) ≤ 1)
        (hvcont.intervalIntegrable 0 1)
        (intervalIntegrable_const : IntervalIntegrable (fun _ : ℝ =&gt; β * ‖v‖ ^ 2) volume 0 1)
        (fun t _ =&gt; hup _ v)
  refine ⟨hi, hgrad, hsym, hbound, ?_⟩
  rw [H.norm_eq_iSup_rayleighQuotient hsym]
  apply ciSup_le
  intro v
  change |inner ℝ (H v) v / ‖v‖ ^ 2| ≤ max |α| |β|
  by_cases hv : v = 0
  · simp [hv]
  · simp only [abs_div, abs_pow, abs_norm]
    apply (div_le_iff₀ (sq_pos_of_pos (norm_pos_iff.mpr hv))).mpr
    have hl := (hbound v).1
    have hu := (hbound v).2
    have hl&#x27; := mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_right
      ((neg_le_abs α).trans (le_max_left |α| |β|)) (sq_nonneg ‖v‖)
    have hu&#x27; := mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_right
      ((le_abs_self β).trans (le_max_right |α| |β|)) (sq_nonneg ‖v‖)
    exact abs_le.mpr ⟨by nlinarith, by nlinarith⟩

end AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.HessianSecantOperator</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-hessiansecantoperator.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details></section><section data-source-comparison="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.HessianSecantOperator.hessian_secant_operator"><h3>Source assumptions versus formal assumptions</h3><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Actual Lean</th><th>Difference kind</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Actual finite-dimensional Hessian integral of C2 V_eta.</td><td>E is a complete real inner-product space (a real Hilbert space), including dimension zero. f:E-&gt;R is globally C2 in the Frechet sense. x,y and every test vector range over all E.</td><td>generalization</td><td>Complete real Hilbert spaces include every source Euclidean dimension, even zero; completeness supplies Riesz/Bochner API and is automatic in finite dimensions.</td></tr><tr><td>alpha=1/(2kappa), upper1 from Lemma4.1.</td><td>alpha,beta are real and may be signed. For every z,v, alpha||v||^2 &lt;= D2f(z)[v,v] &lt;= beta||v||^2. These are bounds on the genuine nested Frechet derivative, not on an independently supplied operator.</td><td>generalization</td><td>Signed global genuine-Hessian bounds generalize the source parameters. No stronger condition is imposed; source V_eta bounds are still a separate prerequisite.</td></tr><tr><td>The genuine matrix segment integral produces the gradient difference and symmetric bounds.</td><td>H(x,y) is the ordinary Lebesgue Bochner integral on[0,1] of the Riesz-represented D2f(y+t(x-y)). Its symmetry, continuity/measurability/integrability and secant identity are conclusions derived from C2, not hypotheses. Completeness permits the Riesz identification and Bochner operator integral. For every complete real inner-product space E, every globally C2 real f with signed global genuine-Hessian diagonal bounds alpha,beta, and all x,y, let phi(t) be the Riesz operator of D2f(y+t(x-y)) and H=integral_0^1 phi(t) dt in ordinary Lebesgue measure. Then phi is interval integrable, gradient f x-gradient f y=H(x-y), H is symmetric, all v satisfy alpha||v||^2&lt;=inner(Hv,v)&lt;=beta||v||^2, and ||H||op&lt;=max(|alpha|,|beta|). In finite Euclidean space this specializes to the source actual H with alpha=1/(2kappa),beta=1; V_eta smoothness/curvature must still be proved separately.</td><td>same</td><td>Riesz expresses the exact real Euclidean matrix Hessian; all integrability and symmetry are derived. Ordinary Lebesgue unit interval and direction x-y are unchanged.</td></tr><tr><td>Lemma4.6 averaged H and kernel expansion/contraction.</td><td>Only the actual Hessian-segment constructor in Lemma4.6. The source smoothed-potential C2/curvature producer, actual Picard-node weighted average H, first-order endpoint expansion/operator remainder, deterministic K contraction, stochastic Khat/Kbar, repeated history, PBPS and both full main results/composition remain independent. The signed Hilbert generalization is background, not an extra assumption on the finite-dimensional source theorem. No probability/cost guarantee or implemented kernel is asserted here.</td><td>unresolved</td><td>This edge is the segment constructor only. It cannot serve as a supplied contraction certificate or smoothed-potential producer.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Only the actual Hessian-segment constructor in Lemma4.6. The source smoothed-potential C2/curvature producer, actual Picard-node weighted average H, first-order endpoint expansion/operator remainder, deterministic K contraction, stochastic Khat/Kbar, repeated history, PBPS and both full main results/composition remain independent. The signed Hilbert generalization is background, not an extra assumption on the finite-dimensional source theorem. No probability/cost guarantee or implemented kernel is asserted here.</p><p><strong>Encoder–denoiser:</strong> accepted · equivalent-after-elaboration</p><p>A generalization is not a source correction. Proposed missing conditions require separate independent repair review. No proposed repair silently changes the original theorem.</p></section><details class="reader-ledger boundary-ledger"><summary>Scope and omitted-condition boundaries</summary><ul><li>Only the actual Hessian-segment constructor in Lemma4.6. The source smoothed-potential C2/curvature producer, actual Picard-node weighted average H, first-order endpoint expansion/operator remainder, deterministic K contraction, stochastic Khat/Kbar, repeated history, PBPS and both full main results/composition remain independent. The signed Hilbert generalization is background, not an extra assumption on the finite-dimensional source theorem. No probability/cost guarantee or implemented kernel is asserted here.</li></ul></details><details class="proof-reader-provenance reader-ledger"><summary>Sources, dependencies, and reuse</summary><h2>Source and reuse</h2><h3>Samplinglib parents called</h3><ul></ul><h3>Mathlib API called (external library)</h3><ul><li>InnerProductSpace.continuousLinearMapOfBilin</li><li>InnerProductSpace.continuousLinearMapOfBilin_apply</li><li>ContDiff.fderiv_right</li><li>ContDiff.continuous_fderiv</li><li>ContinuousLinearMap.intervalIntegral_apply</li><li>ContinuousLinearMap.intervalIntegral_comp_comm</li><li>intervalIntegral.integral_eq_sub_of_hasDerivAt</li><li>ContDiffAt.isSymmSndFDerivAt</li><li>intervalIntegral.integral_mono_on</li><li>ContinuousLinearMap.norm_eq_iSup_rayleighQuotient</li><li>ext_inner_right</li></ul><h3>Mathematical sources</h3><ul><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.SS3">SPHMC Lemma4.6 actual Hessian constructor</a> — Only the actual Hessian-segment constructor in Lemma4.6. The source smoothed-potential C2/curvature producer, actual Picard-node weighted average H, first-order endpoint expansion/operator remainder, deterministic K contraction, stochastic Khat/Kbar, repeated history, PBPS and both full main results/composition remain independent. The signed Hilbert generalization is background, not an extra assumption on the finite-dimensional source theorem. No probability/cost guarantee or implemented kernel is asserted here.</li></ul><p>Samplinglib prose is not a quotation or a source-equivalence certificate. Definitions and aliases are explained as constructions, not counted as new mathematical proofs.</p></details></article><h3>Which proof obligations and prerequisites are covered?</h3><ul><li><span class="status status-orange">Formalized proof edge; source adapter/review separate</span> Actual Hessian integral: gradient difference, symmetry, bounds and norm</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Smoothed potential, actual averaged H/expansion and both main/composition (remaining)</li></ul></section><section id="companion-hessian-strong-convexity-prerequisite" data-publication-item="companion-hessian-strong-convexity-prerequisite"><h2>Supporting Hessian-to-strong-convexity implication</h2><p>Fan Chen, Sinho Chewi, Jianfeng Lu and Matthew S. Zhang provide the C² potential and Hessian-sandwich setting in the two cited v1 papers. The supporting implication and line-restriction exposition are ASTIS&#x27;s mathematical expansion, not copied prose, a source correction or either full sampler theorem.</p><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1">arXiv:2609.06906v1; shared assumption in arXiv:2609.06905v1 · Introduction equation (1.1) in both companion papers; lower-Hessian analytic prerequisite only</a> · faithful paraphrase</p><h3>Complete source statement (Samplinglib restatement)</h3><p>Both companion papers work with a C² Euclidean potential V and constants 0&lt;α≤β such that αI≼∇²V(x)≼βI at every x. The supporting lower-curvature implication selected here is: if D²V(x)[v,v]≥α‖v‖² for all points x and directions v, then V(ax+by)≤aV(x)+bV(y)−αab‖x−y‖²/2 for every x,y and a,b≥0 with a+b=1. The upper Hessian bound is unnecessary for this implication. ASTIS proves the supporting implication more generally on any real normed space for any real α, retaining genuine everywhere C² regularity.</p><ul><li>The primary setting is V∈C²(R^d), 0&lt;α≤β, and αI≼∇²V(x)≼βI for every x.</li><li>The selected supporting implication only uses genuine C² regularity and D²V(x)[v,v]≥α‖v‖² for every x,v. In Euclidean coordinates this is the quadratic-form meaning of the lower matrix inequality.</li><li>All lines lie in the full state space. The target chord weights are nonnegative real numbers summing to one.</li><li>The normed-space/arbitrary-real-modulus generalization is confined to the analytic implication; normalization consumers separately require positive modulus, finite dimension and canonical volume.</li></ul><h3>primary Hessian setting</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[V\in C^2(\mathbb R^d),\quad0&lt;\alpha\le\beta,\quad\alpha I\preceq\nabla^2V(x)\preceq\beta I\quad(\forall x).\]</div><h3>selected lower-curvature consequence</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[D^2V(x)[v,v]\ge\alpha\|v\|^2\quad\Longrightarrow\quad V(ax+by)\le aV(x)+bV(y)-\frac\alpha2ab\|x-y\|^2,\quad a,b\ge0,\ a+b=1.\]</div><h2>Read the formalized proofs</h2><p>Each statement and proof below has its own closed Lean disclosure. Samplinglib parents, Mathlib calls and external mathematical sources are distinguished in each proof.</p><article class="proof-reader" data-authored-declaration="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.HessianStrongConvexity.strongConvexOn_univ_of_fderiv2_lower"><div class="eyebrow">Samplinglib mathematical lesson</div><h2>From a genuine Hessian lower bound to strong convexity</h2><p><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.HessianStrongConvexity.strongConvexOn_univ_of_fderiv2_lower</code> · theorem · <a href="../../../lessons/index.html">Teaching coverage</a></p><section class="reader-first-pass" aria-label="First reading"><div class="reader-first-pass-label">Read this first</div><h2>Mathematical statement</h2><p class="reader-claim">Let E be a real normed space, let V:E→R be twice continuously Fréchet differentiable everywhere, and let α be any real number. Suppose D²V(x)[v,v]≥α‖v‖² for every x,v∈E. Then V is α-strongly convex on the whole space in the chord convention below. No finite dimension, completeness, inner product, positive α, upper Hessian bound, measure or normalizer is an additional hypothesis.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation reader-main-equation">\[D^2V(x)[v,v]\ge\alpha\|v\|^2\quad(\forall x,v\in E)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad V(ax+by)\le aV(x)+bV(y)-\frac\alpha2ab\|x-y\|^2\quad(a,b\ge0,\ a+b=1).\]</div><h3>Proof roadmap</h3><ol class="proof-roadmap"><li><span>1</span><strong>Restrict to a line and compensate its curvature</strong><p>Fix x,y∈E and set v=y−x and c=α‖v‖². Restrict V to the line ℓ(t)=x+tv, then subtract the scalar quadratic ct²/2. The norm of v is a fixed number: the proof never differentiates the norm on E, which is why no inner product or smooth norm is needed.</p></li><li><span>2</span><strong>Calculate two genuine scalar derivatives</strong><p>The chain rule differentiates V along the constant direction v. Applying the chain rule to DV and then evaluating at the fixed vector v gives the second derivative. The assumed diagonal Hessian inequality therefore makes the compensated second derivative nonnegative.</p></li><li><span>3</span><strong>Use one-dimensional convexity</strong><p>A scalar function with the displayed genuine first and second derivatives and nonnegative second derivative is convex. Continuity of q follows from its first derivative witness. Applying its chord inequality at 0 and 1 with weights a,b≥0 and a+b=1 controls q(b).</p></li><li><span>4</span><strong>Recover the exact strong-convexity constant</strong><p>Since x+b(y−x)=ax+by and ‖y−x‖=‖x−y‖, rearrange the scalar chord inequality and use b(1−b)=ab. This is algebra, not multiplication of an inequality by α, so it is valid even when α is zero or negative.</p></li></ol></section><details class="reader-ledger assumption-ledger"><summary>Full assumptions and notation</summary><h3>All objects and hypotheses</h3><ul><li>ContDiff ℝ 2 V is a genuine C² witness. D²V(x)[v,v] means (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ V) x v) v; the regularity hypothesis ensures these totalized operators represent actual derivatives.</li><li>The Hessian lower bound holds at every point and in every direction, not almost everywhere or only along algorithmic iterates.</li><li>The papers use Euclidean spaces and 0&lt;α≤β with an upper Hessian bound βI. This supporting implication only needs their lower bound, and remains valid on arbitrary real normed spaces for any real α.</li><li>The domain is the whole vector space, so every affine line used in the proof is admissible. No boundary extension or differentiability-within-set convention is hidden.</li></ul><p class="muted"></p></details><h2>Mathematical proof</h2><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>1. Restrict to a line and compensate its curvature</h4><p>Fix x,y∈E and set v=y−x and c=α‖v‖². Restrict V to the line ℓ(t)=x+tv, then subtract the scalar quadratic ct²/2. The norm of v is a fixed number: the proof never differentiates the norm on E, which is why no inner product or smooth norm is needed.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\ell(t)=x+tv,\qquad c=\alpha\|v\|^2,\qquad q(t)=V(\ell(t))-\frac c2t^2.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>The proof defines v, c and q locally after opening the StrongConvexOn chord goal. hV.differentiable gives differentiability of V; hV.fderiv_right at order one gives differentiability of its actual first derivative. hline is the derivative witness for the affine line.</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>2. Calculate two genuine scalar derivatives</h4><p>The chain rule differentiates V along the constant direction v. Applying the chain rule to DV and then evaluating at the fixed vector v gives the second derivative. The assumed diagonal Hessian inequality therefore makes the compensated second derivative nonnegative.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[q&#x27;(t)=DV(\ell(t))[v]-ct,\qquad q&#x27;&#x27;(t)=D^2V(\ell(t))[v,v]-c\ge0.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>hq&#x27; and hq&#x27;&#x27; are HasDerivAt proofs, obtained from HasFDerivAt.comp_hasDerivAt, HasDerivAt.clm_apply and the scalar polynomial rules. Explicit reflexivity conversions reconcile definitionally equal scalar instances; they add no mathematical premise. hH supplies the final nonnegativity.</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>3. Use one-dimensional convexity</h4><p>A scalar function with the displayed genuine first and second derivatives and nonnegative second derivative is convex. Continuity of q follows from its first derivative witness. Applying its chord inequality at 0 and 1 with weights a,b≥0 and a+b=1 controls q(b).</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[q(b)\le aq(0)+bq(1)=aV(x)+b\left(V(y)-\frac c2\right).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>convexOn_of_hasDerivWithinAt2_nonneg is applied on Set.univ. Each whole-line HasDerivAt witness supplies the required HasDerivWithinAt witness. hconv.2 then yields hchord at the endpoints 0 and 1.</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>4. Recover the exact strong-convexity constant</h4><p>Since x+b(y−x)=ax+by and ‖y−x‖=‖x−y‖, rearrange the scalar chord inequality and use b(1−b)=ab. This is algebra, not multiplication of an inequality by α, so it is valid even when α is zero or negative.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[V(ax+by)\le aV(x)+bV(y)-\frac c2(b-b^2)=aV(x)+bV(y)-\frac\alpha2ab\|x-y\|^2.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>hxy and hend identify the line points with the desired chord and endpoint; norm_sub_rev identifies the two displacement norms. Substituting a=1−b and polynomial arithmetic gives the defining StrongConvexOn inequality with exactly α/2.</p></details></div><section class="lean-verification"><h2>Lean verification</h2><p>The exact proposition and proof begin folded so the mathematical argument remains the primary reading path.</p><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-statement" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.HessianStrongConvexity.strongConvexOn_univ_of_fderiv2_lower"><summary>Lean statement · strongConvexOn_univ_of_fderiv2_lower</summary><p>The public theorem quantifies over any real normed E, V:E→ℝ and α:ℝ. Its two hypotheses are ContDiff ℝ 2 V and the everywhere, every-direction nested-fderiv diagonal lower bound. The conclusion is Mathlib StrongConvexOn Set.univ α V. There is no assumed gradient or Hessian field with a separate unproved identification.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem strongConvexOn_univ_of_fderiv2_lower
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E]
    {V : E → ℝ} {α : ℝ}
    (hV : ContDiff ℝ 2 V)
    (hH : ∀ x v : E, α * ‖v‖ ^ 2 ≤ (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ V) x v) v) :
    StrongConvexOn (Set.univ : Set E) α V</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-hessianstrongconvexity.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-proof" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.HessianStrongConvexity.strongConvexOn_univ_of_fderiv2_lower"><summary>Lean proof · strongConvexOn_univ_of_fderiv2_lower</summary><p>This declaration is a new mathematical adapter built from Mathlib calculus and one-dimensional convexity. It calls no ASTIS production theorem. In the focused tests, its positive-α finite-dimensional Borel inner-product specialization feeds the existing ASTIS StrongConvexGibbsIntegrability theorem. The tests derive Integrable and a strictly positive canonical-volume Gibbs integral, then an actual IsProbabilityMeasure for volume.tilted(-V), which supplies the probability input of the existing quadratic_tilt_tilt RGO consumer. The tests assume neither StrongConvexOn nor integrability. Separate tests cover α=0 and zero dimension.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem strongConvexOn_univ_of_fderiv2_lower
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E]
    {V : E → ℝ} {α : ℝ}
    (hV : ContDiff ℝ 2 V)
    (hH : ∀ x v : E, α * ‖v‖ ^ 2 ≤ (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ V) x v) v) :
    StrongConvexOn (Set.univ : Set E) α V := by
  have hVd : Differentiable ℝ V := hV.differentiable (by norm_num)
  have hVdd : Differentiable ℝ (fderiv ℝ V) :=
    (hV.fderiv_right (m := 1) (by norm_num)).differentiable_one
  refine ⟨convex_univ, ?_⟩
  intro x _ y _ a b ha hb hab
  let v : E := y - x
  let c : ℝ := α * ‖v‖ ^ 2
  let q : ℝ → ℝ := fun t =&gt; V (x + t • v) - c / 2 * t ^ 2
  let q&#x27; : ℝ → ℝ := fun t =&gt; fderiv ℝ V (x + t • v) v - c * t
  let q&#x27;&#x27; : ℝ → ℝ := fun t =&gt;
    (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ V) (x + t • v) v) v - c
  have hline (t : ℝ) : HasDerivAt (fun s : ℝ =&gt; x + s • v) v t := by
    simpa using ((hasDerivAt_id t).smul_const v).const_add x
  have hq&#x27; (t : ℝ) : HasDerivAt q (q&#x27; t) t := by
    have hquadratic : HasDerivAt (fun s : ℝ =&gt; c / 2 * s ^ 2) (c * t) t := by
      convert ((hasDerivAt_id t).pow 2).const_mul (c / 2) using 1 &lt;;&gt;
        first | rfl | (norm_num [id_eq]; ring)
    convert ((hVd _).hasFDerivAt.comp_hasDerivAt t (hline t)).sub hquadratic
      using 1 &lt;;&gt; rfl
  have hq&#x27;&#x27; (t : ℝ) : HasDerivAt q&#x27; (q&#x27;&#x27; t) t := by
    have hfirst := ((hVdd _).hasFDerivAt.comp_hasDerivAt t (hline t)).clm_apply
      (hasDerivAt_const t v)
    convert hfirst.sub ((hasDerivAt_id t).const_mul c) using 1 &lt;;&gt;
      first | rfl | simp [q&#x27;&#x27;, ContinuousLinearMap.map_zero]
  have hconv : ConvexOn ℝ Set.univ q :=
    convexOn_of_hasDerivWithinAt2_nonneg convex_univ
      (fun t _ =&gt; (hq&#x27; t).continuousAt.continuousWithinAt)
      (fun t _ =&gt; (hq&#x27; t).hasDerivWithinAt)
      (fun t _ =&gt; (hq&#x27;&#x27; t).hasDerivWithinAt)
      (fun t _ =&gt; sub_nonneg.mpr (hH (x + t • v) v))
  have hchord := hconv.2 (x := 0) (Set.mem_univ _) (y := 1) (Set.mem_univ _)
    ha hb hab
  have hxy : x + b • v = a • x + b • y := by
    dsimp only [v]
    rw [show a = 1 - b by linarith]
    module
  have hend : x + v = y := by simp [v]
  simp only [q, smul_eq_mul, mul_zero, mul_one, zero_add, zero_smul, add_zero,
    one_pow, one_smul] at hchord
  norm_num only [zero_pow, mul_zero, sub_zero] at hchord
  rw [hxy, hend] at hchord
  have hnorm : ‖v‖ ^ 2 = ‖x - y‖ ^ 2 := by
    dsimp only [v]
    rw [norm_sub_rev]
  change V (a • x + b • y) ≤ a * V x + b * V y - a * b * (α / 2 * ‖x - y‖ ^ 2)
  dsimp only [c] at hchord
  rw [hnorm] at hchord
  rw [show a = 1 - b by linarith] at hchord ⊢
  nlinarith

end AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.HessianStrongConvexity</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-hessianstrongconvexity.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details></section><section data-source-comparison="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.HessianStrongConvexity.strongConvexOn_univ_of_fderiv2_lower"><h3>Source assumptions versus formal assumptions</h3><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Actual Lean</th><th>Difference kind</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>V is everywhere C²</td><td>ContDiff ℝ 2 V</td><td>same</td><td>This supplies actual first and second Fréchet derivatives, rather than relying on totalized derivative notation alone.</td></tr><tr><td>αI≼∇²V(x) pointwise</td><td>∀ x v, α*‖v‖² ≤ (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ V) x v) v</td><td>same</td><td>For a C² Euclidean potential this is the defining quadratic-form meaning of the Hessian lower bound. No separate field is assumed to be the Hessian.</td></tr><tr><td>Euclidean finite-dimensional state space</td><td>Any real normed space, including zero dimension</td><td>generalization</td><td>The proof differentiates only a scalar line and its scalar quadratic compensation, not the squared norm on the ambient space. No inner product, completeness or finite dimension is used.</td></tr><tr><td>0&lt;α≤β and the additional upper Hessian bound βI</td><td>Arbitrary α:ℝ; no β or upper Hessian bound</td><td>generalization</td><td>Only the lower bound controls the compensated second derivative. The concluding algebra works for any α. This does not remove positivity or smoothness conditions from either source sampling theorem.</td></tr><tr><td>Strong convexity in the C² Hessian convention</td><td>StrongConvexOn Set.univ α V, with chord correction αab‖x−y‖²/2</td><td>source-implicit</td><td>The selected analytic expansion proves the conversion to the exact chord normalization. It does not assume StrongConvexOn or change α to a weaker constant.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>One analytic prerequisite shared by both papers. No upper-Hessian theorem, entire Gibbs/RGO initialization, invariance, discrepancy or query-cost guarantee is proved by this declaration.</p><p><strong>Encoder–denoiser:</strong> accepted · equivalent-after-elaboration</p><h4>Detected semantic differences</h4><ul><li><strong>domains</strong>: The analytic theorem is explicitly generalized from the source&#x27;s Euclidean space to arbitrary real normed spaces, including zero dimension and spaces not assumed complete. — The generalization is stated in the extraction and lesson and justified by restriction to real affine lines. No derivative of the ambient norm, volume construction or inner-product identity is required.</li><li><strong>assumptions</strong>: The source&#x27;s 0&lt;α≤β and upper Hessian bound are reduced to the lower derivative bound with arbitrary real α for this supporting implication only. — The proof needs only q&#x27;&#x27;≥0 after subtracting c=α‖v‖². The α=0 and zero-dimensional arbitrary-α tests compile. Negative α is allowed in the chord convention but does not establish ordinary convexity or finite Gibbs normalization; source consumers retain their own positivity requirements.</li><li><strong>scopes</strong>: The implication is an attributed ASTIS mathematical expansion of the source Hessian assumptions, not an additional theorem claimed to appear in either paper. — Both primary Introduction (1.1) statements supply the C² Hessian-sandwich setting. The extraction, attribution and role prerequisite transparently identify the new supporting hessian-to-chord proof and no larger source conclusion.</li><li><strong>conclusion</strong>: Gibbs integrability, positive normalization, probability and RGO closure are verified downstream test consumers rather than additional production conclusions of this declaration. — Those tests add positive α and finite-dimensional Borel inner-product structure, then use separate compiled ASTIS theorems. No such measure-theoretic hypothesis or conclusion occurs in the Hessian adapter itself, and no sampler or process result is inferred.</li></ul><p>A generalization is not a source correction. Proposed missing conditions require separate independent repair review. No proposed repair silently changes the original theorem.</p></section><details class="reader-ledger boundary-ledger"><summary>Scope and omitted-condition boundaries</summary><ul><li>This is the supporting analytic meaning of the lower-Hessian part of the papers&#x27; C² potential assumptions, not either complete sampling theorem.</li><li>The production theorem does not prove an upper-Hessian smoothness bound, a condition-number update, Gibbs integrability, a minimizer, an invariant kernel, output error or query complexity. Its tests exercise normalization and RGO consumers under their own required positive-modulus and finite-dimensional measure hypotheses.</li><li>For a C² Euclidean potential, the source matrix inequality αI≼∇²V(x) means exactly the displayed diagonal quadratic-form lower bound. This declaration uses the canonical second Fréchet derivative, not an arbitrary field labelled Hessian, and does not introduce a coordinate-matrix representation API.</li><li>The extension to any real normed space and arbitrary α concerns this analytic implication only. It is not an infinite-dimensional probability normalization or nonpositive-curvature sampling result.</li></ul></details><details class="proof-reader-provenance reader-ledger"><summary>Sources, dependencies, and reuse</summary><h2>Source and reuse</h2><h3>Samplinglib parents called</h3><ul></ul><h3>Mathlib API called (external library)</h3><ul><li>ContDiff.differentiable</li><li>ContDiff.fderiv_right</li><li>ContDiff.differentiable_one</li><li>HasFDerivAt.comp_hasDerivAt</li><li>HasDerivAt.clm_apply</li><li>HasDerivAt.pow</li><li>HasDerivAt.const_mul</li><li>HasDerivAt.sub</li><li>convexOn_of_hasDerivWithinAt2_nonneg</li><li>norm_sub_rev</li></ul><h3>Mathematical sources</h3><ul><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1">Chen, Chewi, Lu and Zhang, Smoothed Picard Hamiltonian Monte Carlo v1, Introduction (1.1)</a> — The C² Euclidean potential and pointwise Hessian sandwich supply the source setting. The line-restriction proof is ASTIS&#x27;s expanded supporting argument, not quoted source prose or full sampler formalization.</li><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06905v1">Chen, Chewi, Lu and Zhang, Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler v1, Introduction (1.1)</a> — The same C² lower-Hessian assumption supplies a second consumer of this canonical shared analytic adapter.</li></ul><p>Samplinglib prose is not a quotation or a source-equivalence certificate. Definitions and aliases are explained as constructions, not counted as new mathematical proofs.</p></details></article><h3>Which proof obligations and prerequisites are covered?</h3><ul><li><span class="status status-green">Independently verified prerequisite</span> Derive the exact α-strong-convexity chord inequality from the genuine C² lower Hessian bound</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Combine source-potential specializations with the separate Gibbs, oracle and sampling-theorem obligations</li></ul></section><section id="sphmc-markov-tv-contraction" data-publication-item="sphmc-markov-tv-contraction"><h2>A common Markov kernel cannot amplify total-variation error</h2><p>Fan Chen, Sinho Chewi, Jianfeng Lu and Matthew S. Zhang. ASTIS expands the selected standard data-processing step; no author endorsement or full theorem formalization is implied.</p><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S7.SS2">arXiv:2609.06906v1 · Section 7.2, proof of Theorem 1.3: data-processing display after constructing the actual and proxy input laws</a> · faithful paraphrase</p><h3>Complete source statement (Samplinglib restatement)</h3><p>SPHMC v1 Section 7.2, proof of Theorem 1.3, passes the actual/proxy input TV error through the same proximal BPS Markov kernel. ASTIS expands only this data-processing step: on any two measurable spaces, if probability input laws μ and ν satisfy |μ(S)−ν(S)|≤δ for every measurable input event S, then for every Markov kernel K and every measurable output event T, |(μK)(T)−(νK)(T)|≤δ. In the paper the selected inputs are the implementable law and its comparison law, δ=ε/2, and K is K_{ε/2,1}. The generic measurable-space formulation and arbitrary real δ are an explicit generalization; the premise on the empty event implies δ≥0. This is an attributed faithful paraphrase with ASTIS&#x27;s standard proof expansion, not copied prose. The construction of the paper&#x27;s actual kernel, proxy warmness, its separate mixing error and actual-input expected query costs are not supplied by this declaration.</p><ul><li>K(x,·) is a probability measure for every x, and x↦K(x,T) is measurable for each measurable T. IsMarkovKernel records the first property and Kernel records the second; merely specifying transition probabilities without measurable dependence would not suffice.</li><li>Both input measures have total mass one. Every event probability is finite, so Measure.real is an ordinary real probability, not a misleading conversion of infinity.</li><li>The discrepancy premise is uniform over all measurable input events; a bound on one event, or only pointwise convergence of densities, is not the same hypothesis.</li><li>The same K is applied to both inputs. Comparing distinct kernels requires an additional approximation-error estimate.</li></ul><h3>selected data-processing step</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\operatorname{TV}(\widehat\pi_{\varepsilon/2}K_{\varepsilon/2,1},\widehat\pi^{\dagger}_{\varepsilon/2}K_{\varepsilon/2,1})\le\varepsilon/2.\]</div><h3>generic eventwise formulation</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\sup_{T\in\mathcal B}|(\mu K)(T)-(\nu K)(T)|\le\delta,\qquad (\rho K)(T)=\int_A K(x,T)\,\rho(dx).\]</div><h2>Read the formalized proofs</h2><p>Each statement and proof below has its own closed Lean disclosure. Samplinglib parents, Mathlib calls and external mathematical sources are distinguished in each proof.</p><article class="proof-reader" data-authored-declaration="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.KernelTotalVariation.abs_real_comp_sub_le"><div class="eyebrow">Samplinglib mathematical lesson</div><h2>A common Markov kernel cannot amplify total-variation error</h2><p><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.KernelTotalVariation.abs_real_comp_sub_le</code> · theorem · <a href="../../../lessons/index.html">Teaching coverage</a></p><section class="reader-first-pass" aria-label="First reading"><div class="reader-first-pass-label">Read this first</div><h2>Mathematical statement</h2><p class="reader-claim">Let A and B be measurable spaces, μ and ν probability measures on A, and K a Markov kernel from A to B. Let δ be real. If |μ(S)−ν(S)|≤δ for every measurable S⊆A, then |(μK)(T)−(νK)(T)|≤δ for every measurable T⊆B. No density, topology or coupling is required. The input assumption on the empty event already implies δ≥0.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation reader-main-equation">\[\sup_{T\in\mathcal B}|(\mu K)(T)-(\nu K)(T)|\le\delta,\qquad (\rho K)(T)=\int_A K(x,T)\,\rho(dx).\]</div><h3>Proof roadmap</h3><ol class="proof-roadmap"><li><span>1</span><strong>Replace the output event by a bounded observable</strong><p>Fix a measurable output event T and put f(x)=K(x,T). Kernel measurability makes f measurable and each transition law being a probability gives 0≤f≤1. Since each input measure is finite, f is strongly measurable and integrable for both inputs. Evaluating the composed law is therefore a genuine integral.</p></li><li><span>2</span><strong>Express a bounded observable by its level events</strong><p>For each u, the event {x:u≤f(x)} is measurable. Integrate these event probabilities over (0,1]. This layercake identity follows because for each fixed x the set of levels u in (0,1] with u≤f(x) has length f(x). It remains valid at f=0 and f=1 and for atoms; no continuous distribution is assumed.</p></li><li><span>3</span><strong>Justify subtraction of the two level integrals</strong><p>As u increases, each level set shrinks, hence its measure is an antitone measurable function of u. These real probabilities lie in [0,1]. The interval has finite measure, so both level functions are integrable there. This explicit witness licenses subtracting their integrals.</p></li><li><span>4</span><strong>Apply the event bound and preserve the exact constant</strong><p>Apply the input hypothesis to every measurable level event. The absolute integrand is bounded by δ. The norm-of-integral estimate multiplies this bound by the measure of (0,1], which is exactly one. Thus no factor two is lost; this is the event-supremum convention for probability total variation.</p></li></ol></section><details class="reader-ledger assumption-ledger"><summary>Full assumptions and notation</summary><h3>All objects and hypotheses</h3><ul><li>K(x,·) is a probability measure for every x, and x↦K(x,T) is measurable for each measurable T. IsMarkovKernel records the first property and Kernel records the second; merely specifying transition probabilities without measurable dependence would not suffice.</li><li>Both input measures have total mass one. Every event probability is finite, so Measure.real is an ordinary real probability, not a misleading conversion of infinity.</li><li>The discrepancy premise is uniform over all measurable input events; a bound on one event, or only pointwise convergence of densities, is not the same hypothesis.</li><li>The same K is applied to both inputs. Comparing distinct kernels requires an additional approximation-error estimate.</li></ul><p class="muted"></p></details><h2>Mathematical proof</h2><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>1. Replace the output event by a bounded observable</h4><p>Fix a measurable output event T and put f(x)=K(x,T). Kernel measurability makes f measurable and each transition law being a probability gives 0≤f≤1. Since each input measure is finite, f is strongly measurable and integrable for both inputs. Evaluating the composed law is therefore a genuine integral.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[0\le f(x)\le1,\qquad(\rho K)(T)=\int_A f\,d\rho,\quad\rho\in\{\mu,\nu\}.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>The local hf, hf0 and hf1 establish measurability and the bounds. hfi uses integrable_const and Integrable.mono&#x27;. happly unfolds Measure.bind, applies Measure.bind_apply, and converts the nonnegative integral with integral_toReal; finite event masses discharge the conversion condition.</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>2. Express a bounded observable by its level events</h4><p>For each u, the event {x:u≤f(x)} is measurable. Integrate these event probabilities over (0,1]. This layercake identity follows because for each fixed x the set of levels u in (0,1] with u≤f(x) has length f(x). It remains valid at f=0 and f=1 and for atoms; no continuous distribution is assumed.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[f(x)=\int_{(0,1]}\mathbf1_{\{u\le f(x)\}}\,du,\qquad\int_A f\,d\rho=\int_{(0,1]}\rho\{x:u\le f(x)\}\,du.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>hlayer applies Integrable.integral_eq_integral_Ioc_meas_le using hfi and the everywhere bounds promoted to almost-everywhere bounds. This reuses Mathlib&#x27;s layercake theorem rather than reproving Tonelli.</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>3. Justify subtraction of the two level integrals</h4><p>As u increases, each level set shrinks, hence its measure is an antitone measurable function of u. These real probabilities lie in [0,1]. The interval has finite measure, so both level functions are integrable there. This explicit witness licenses subtracting their integrals.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[0\le\rho\{f\ge u\}\le1,\qquad \int f\,d\mu-\int f\,d\nu=\int_{(0,1]}[\mu\{f\ge u\}-\nu\{f\ge u\}]\,du.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>htail uses Antitone.measurable, finite-measure bounded integrability and measureReal_mono. integral_sub receives both htail witnesses. A totalized integral subtraction without those witnesses would not justify the mathematical equality.</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>4. Apply the event bound and preserve the exact constant</h4><p>Apply the input hypothesis to every measurable level event. The absolute integrand is bounded by δ. The norm-of-integral estimate multiplies this bound by the measure of (0,1], which is exactly one. Thus no factor two is lost; this is the event-supremum convention for probability total variation.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[|(\mu K)(T)-(\nu K)(T)|\le\int_{(0,1]}|\mu\{f\ge u\}-\nu\{f\ge u\}|\,du\le\delta\,|(0,1]|=\delta.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>measurableSet_le measurable_const hf supplies each measurable event. norm_integral_le_of_norm_le_const and the interval-volume simplification close the bound; no integral-contraction hypothesis is smuggled into the inputs.</p></details></div><section class="lean-verification"><h2>Lean verification</h2><p>The exact proposition and proof begin folded so the mathematical argument remains the primary reading path.</p><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-statement" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.KernelTotalVariation.abs_real_comp_sub_le"><summary>Lean statement · abs_real_comp_sub_le</summary><p>Measure.real turns finite event mass into a real number. Lean writes K ∘ₘ μ for the input law μ passed through K (often written μK on paper). The theorem quantifies over arbitrary measurable spaces and uses actual Kernel and IsMarkovKernel data. There is no separate δ≥0 premise because the event bound already forces it.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem abs_real_comp_sub_le
    {A B : Type*} [MeasurableSpace A] [MeasurableSpace B]
    (μ ν : Measure A) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ] [IsProbabilityMeasure ν]
    (K : Kernel A B) [IsMarkovKernel K]
    {δ : ℝ}
    (hδ : ∀ s, MeasurableSet s → |μ.real s - ν.real s| ≤ δ) :
    ∀ t, MeasurableSet t →
      |(K ∘ₘ μ).real t - (K ∘ₘ ν).real t| ≤ δ</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-probability-kerneltotalvariation.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-proof" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.KernelTotalVariation.abs_real_comp_sub_le"><summary>Lean proof · abs_real_comp_sub_le</summary><p>The only production declaration is this contraction theorem; hf, hfi, happly, htail and hlayer are local proof terms. The focused tests exercise the declaration at zero discrepancy and compose it with a separately assumed proxy-output estimate using the triangle inequality. They also check that the empty-event premise forces nonnegative δ. No wrapper is counted as a new leaf.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem abs_real_comp_sub_le
    {A B : Type*} [MeasurableSpace A] [MeasurableSpace B]
    (μ ν : Measure A) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ] [IsProbabilityMeasure ν]
    (K : Kernel A B) [IsMarkovKernel K]
    {δ : ℝ}
    (hδ : ∀ s, MeasurableSet s → |μ.real s - ν.real s| ≤ δ) :
    ∀ t, MeasurableSet t →
      |(K ∘ₘ μ).real t - (K ∘ₘ ν).real t| ≤ δ := by
  intro t ht
  let f : A → ℝ := fun x =&gt; (K x).real t
  have hf : Measurable f := (K.measurable_coe ht).ennreal_toReal
  have hf0 (x : A) : 0 ≤ f x := measureReal_nonneg
  have hf1 (x : A) : f x ≤ 1 := measureReal_le_one
  have hfi (ρ : Measure A) [IsProbabilityMeasure ρ] :
      Integrable f ρ := by
    apply (integrable_const (1 : ℝ)).mono&#x27; hf.aestronglyMeasurable
    exact Filter.Eventually.of_forall fun x =&gt; by
      simpa only [Real.norm_eq_abs, abs_of_nonneg (hf0 x)] using hf1 x
  have happly (ρ : Measure A) [IsProbabilityMeasure ρ] :
      (K ∘ₘ ρ).real t = ∫ x, f x ∂ρ := by
    change (Measure.bind ρ K t).toReal = ∫ x, (K x t).toReal ∂ρ
    rw [Measure.bind_apply ht K.aemeasurable]
    exact (integral_toReal (K.measurable_coe ht).aemeasurable
      (Filter.Eventually.of_forall fun x =&gt; measure_lt_top (K x) t)).symm
  have htail (ρ : Measure A) [IsProbabilityMeasure ρ] :
      IntegrableOn (fun u : ℝ =&gt; ρ.real {x | u ≤ f x}) (Ioc 0 1) := by
    apply Measure.integrableOn_of_bounded
      (M := ρ.real univ) measure_Ioc_lt_top.ne
    · apply
        (Measurable.ennreal_toReal (Antitone.measurable ?_)).aestronglyMeasurable
      exact fun _ _ huv =&gt; measure_mono (fun _ hx =&gt; huv.trans hx)
    · exact Filter.Eventually.of_forall fun u =&gt; by
        simp only [Real.norm_eq_abs, abs_of_nonneg measureReal_nonneg]
        exact measureReal_mono (subset_univ _)
  have hlayer (ρ : Measure A) [IsProbabilityMeasure ρ] :
      (∫ x, f x ∂ρ) =
        ∫ u in Ioc (0 : ℝ) 1, ρ.real {x | u ≤ f x} :=
    (hfi ρ).integral_eq_integral_Ioc_meas_le
      (Filter.Eventually.of_forall hf0)
      (Filter.Eventually.of_forall hf1)
  rw [happly μ, happly ν, hlayer μ, hlayer ν,
    ← integral_sub (htail μ) (htail ν)]
  simpa [Real.norm_eq_abs, measureReal_def] using
    (norm_integral_le_of_norm_le_const
      (μ := volume.restrict (Ioc (0 : ℝ) 1))
      (f := fun u =&gt; μ.real {x | u ≤ f x} - ν.real {x | u ≤ f x})
      (C := δ)
      (Filter.Eventually.of_forall fun u =&gt; by
        simpa only [Real.norm_eq_abs] using
          hδ {x | u ≤ f x} (measurableSet_le measurable_const hf)))

end AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.KernelTotalVariation</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-probability-kerneltotalvariation.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details></section><section data-source-comparison="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.KernelTotalVariation.abs_real_comp_sub_le"><h3>Source assumptions versus formal assumptions</h3><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Actual Lean</th><th>Difference kind</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Probability input laws and the proximal BPS Markov kernel</td><td>Probability μ,ν on A and a common Kernel A B with IsMarkovKernel</td><td>generalization</td><td>Arbitrary measurable spaces and any Markov kernel suffice for the selected standard step; constructing the source-specific kernel remains separate.</td></tr><tr><td>Input TV bound ε/2</td><td>Uniform event bound with δ:ℝ</td><td>generalization</td><td>Set δ=ε/2. The premise on the empty event supplies δ≥0, so omitting a separate sign assumption does not change the source specialization.</td></tr><tr><td>By data processing</td><td>Measurability, bounded input integrability, integrable layer functions and kernel evaluation proved in the body</td><td>source-implicit</td><td>These are the mathematical justifications of the displayed step, derived from actual kernel/probability data, not added input restrictions.</td></tr><tr><td>Probability total variation</td><td>Uniform absolute real-event difference</td><td>same</td><td>This is the event-supremum convention, equivalent to half the L1 density difference when densities exist; densities are not assumed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>This proves the standard data-processing edge used in SPHMC Section 7.2, not construction of the proximal BPS kernel or the implementable/proxy laws. A separate estimate TV(νK,π)≤ε gives TV(μK,π)≤δ+ε by triangle. The focused consumer test makes that estimate explicit; it does not establish mixing. TV proximity alone does not control an unbounded expected query count. Actual-input cost, event-rate bounds and algorithm termination remain independent open obligations. The MCMC E6 perturbed-kernel route is an intended additional consumer for its TV-specialized stability step, not an already compiled general perturbation theorem.</p><p><strong>Encoder–denoiser:</strong> accepted · equivalent-after-elaboration</p><h4>Detected semantic differences</h4><ul><li><strong>domains</strong>: The generic two-measurable-space formulation extends the concrete source application. — The source restatement explicitly discloses this extension, and the supplied proof uses only measurable events and finite probability masses.</li><li><strong>constant_dependencies</strong>: Arbitrary real δ replaces the particular ε/2 allowance. — The empty-event premise rules out negative δ; no extra sign hypothesis or loss of constant is required. This does not relax the parameter requirements of the actual sampling algorithm.</li></ul><p>A generalization is not a source correction. Proposed missing conditions require separate independent repair review. No proposed repair silently changes the original theorem.</p></section><details class="reader-ledger boundary-ledger"><summary>Scope and omitted-condition boundaries</summary><ul><li>This proves the standard data-processing edge used in SPHMC Section 7.2, not construction of the proximal BPS kernel or the implementable/proxy laws.</li><li>A separate estimate TV(νK,π)≤ε gives TV(μK,π)≤δ+ε by triangle. The focused consumer test makes that estimate explicit; it does not establish mixing.</li><li>TV proximity alone does not control an unbounded expected query count. Actual-input cost, event-rate bounds and algorithm termination remain independent open obligations.</li><li>The MCMC E6 perturbed-kernel route is an intended additional consumer for its TV-specialized stability step, not an already compiled general perturbation theorem.</li></ul></details><details class="proof-reader-provenance reader-ledger"><summary>Sources, dependencies, and reuse</summary><h2>Source and reuse</h2><h3>Samplinglib parents called</h3><ul></ul><h3>Mathlib API called (external library)</h3><ul><li>Kernel.measurable_coe</li><li>Measure.bind_apply</li><li>integral_toReal</li><li>Integrable.integral_eq_integral_Ioc_meas_le</li><li>Antitone.measurable</li><li>Measure.integrableOn_of_bounded</li><li>integral_sub</li><li>norm_integral_le_of_norm_le_const</li></ul><h3>Mathematical sources</h3><ul><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S7.SS2">Chen, Chewi, Lu and Zhang, SPHMC v1, Section 7.2, proof of Theorem 1.3</a> — Only the data-processing step for actual/proxy inputs. This four-step proof is ASTIS&#x27;s standard mathematical expansion, not quoted paper prose.</li><li><a href="https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/db584cd6d46c92f209a44c0f1c829460d327499d/Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Measure/LevyProkhorovMetric.lean">Kalle Kytölä and Mathlib contributors, Apache-2.0</a> — The local bounded tail-integrability pattern is adapted from this module; public Layercake and kernel APIs do the mathematical work. No external code is marked ASTIS-owned merely by citation.</li></ul><p>Samplinglib prose is not a quotation or a source-equivalence certificate. Definitions and aliases are explained as constructions, not counted as new mathematical proofs.</p></details></article><h3>Which proof obligations and prerequisites are covered?</h3><ul><li><span class="status status-orange">Formalized proof edge; source adapter/review separate</span> Transfer the input eventwise discrepancy through the same actual Markov kernel with factor one</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Construct the source algorithm kernel and actual/proxy laws and establish the separate proxy mixing bound</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Establish the source expected query cost under the actual input law, not by unbounded-cost TV transfer</li></ul></section><section id="sphmc-quadratic-regularization-curvature" data-publication-item="sphmc-quadratic-regularization-curvature"><h2>Curvature and actual gradient smoothness of the RGO potential</h2><p>Fan Chen, Sinho Chewi, Jianfeng Lu and Matthew S. Zhang state the SPHMC RGO-potential curvature/smoothness clause and the related PBPS potential and Hessian sandwich in the cited v1 papers. This is ASTIS&#x27;s attributed paraphrase and expanded analytic proof, not copied prose, a source correction or either complete sampling theorem.</p><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S6.SS2.SSS2">arXiv:2609.06906v1; related potential in arXiv:2609.06905v1 · Section 6.2.2, Lemma 6.4, strong-convexity/smoothness clause; PBPS Proposition 2.1(i), (2.9)-(2.10), as prerequisite/consequence support only</a> · faithful paraphrase</p><h3>Complete source statement (Samplinglib restatement)</h3><p>In SPHMC Section 6.2.2 let U∈C²(R^d) satisfy κ⁻¹I≼∇²U(x)≼I everywhere. For A∈(0,∞] and u∈R^d put U_A,u(x)=U(x)+‖x−u‖²/(2A). Lemma 6.4&#x27;s selected clause states that U_A,u is α_A-strongly convex and β_A-smooth, where α_A=κ⁻¹+A⁻¹ and β_A=1+A⁻¹; smoothness here means actual gradient Lipschitz continuity. Zero precision A⁻¹=0 retains A=∞. ASTIS proves this clause for general nonnegative m,L,r on a complete real inner-product space, with U+r‖x−u‖²/2 and constants m+r,L+r. PBPS (2.9)-(2.10) uses the same quadratic potential with constants α,β,η⁻¹ and states its Hessian sandwich; the present public two-conclusion theorem is prerequisite/consequence support for that use, not a formalization of the entire PBPS statement.</p><ul><li>SPHMC&#x27;s potential is genuinely C² on Euclidean space and satisfies its pointwise lower and upper Hessian bounds with the source condition parameter κ≥1.</li><li>The regularization centre is arbitrary and A is positive or infinite. Precision r=A⁻¹ is nonnegative; r=0 represents infinite variance, not a legal zero finite variance.</li><li>The generalized Lean hypothesis is m‖v‖²≤D²U(x)[v,v]≤L‖v‖² for every x,v, with m,L,r nonnegative, together with ContDiff ℝ 2 U.</li><li>The deterministic theorem is on the whole complete real inner-product space. It uses neither an assumed derivative field nor any measure or normalization premise.</li></ul><h3>source regularization</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[U_{A,u}(x)=U(x)+\frac{\|x-u\|^2}{2A},\qquad\alpha_A=\kappa^{-1}+A^{-1},\qquad\beta_A=1+A^{-1}.\]</div><h3>exact curvature and smoothness</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[U_{A,u}(ax+by)\le aU_{A,u}(x)+bU_{A,u}(y)-\frac{\alpha_A}{2}ab\|x-y\|^2,\quad\|\nabla U_{A,u}(x)-\nabla U_{A,u}(y)\|\le\beta_A\|x-y\|,\quad a,b\ge0,\ a+b=1.\]</div><h2>Read the formalized proofs</h2><p>Each statement and proof below has its own closed Lean disclosure. Samplinglib parents, Mathlib calls and external mathematical sources are distinguished in each proof.</p><article class="proof-reader" data-authored-declaration="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.QuadraticRegularization.strongConvexOn_and_lipschitzWith_gradient_add_quadratic"><div class="eyebrow">Samplinglib mathematical lesson</div><h2>Quadratic regularization shifts actual curvature and gradient smoothness</h2><p><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.QuadraticRegularization.strongConvexOn_and_lipschitzWith_gradient_add_quadratic</code> · theorem · <a href="../../../lessons/index.html">Teaching coverage</a></p><section class="reader-first-pass" aria-label="First reading"><div class="reader-first-pass-label">Read this first</div><h2>Mathematical statement</h2><p class="reader-claim">Let E be a complete real inner-product space and U:E→R be everywhere twice continuously Fréchet differentiable. Let m,L,r be nonnegative real constants. Suppose m‖v‖²≤D²U(x)[v,v]≤L‖v‖² for every x,v∈E. For every centre u∈E, the actual potential W(x)=U(x)+r‖x−u‖²/2 is (m+r)-strongly convex on E and its actual gradient is (L+r)-Lipschitz on E. The constants are exact, including r=0. No gradient field, Hessian field or operator-norm bound is supplied as an assumption.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation reader-main-equation">\[W(x)=U(x)+\frac r2\|x-u\|^2,\qquad W(ax+by)\le aW(x)+bW(y)-\frac{m+r}{2}ab\|x-y\|^2,\qquad\|\nabla W(x)-\nabla W(y)\|\le(L+r)\|x-y\|,\quad a,b\ge0,\ a+b=1.\]</div><h3>Proof roadmap</h3><ol class="proof-roadmap"><li><span>1</span><strong>Differentiate the actual quadratic potential</strong><p>The squared norm is smooth on a real inner-product space. Differentiate the shifted quadratic twice: its first derivative evaluates r⟨x−u,v⟩, and its second derivative evaluates r⟨v,w⟩. Thus W is genuinely C² and its diagonal Hessian is the diagonal Hessian of U plus r‖v‖². The original sandwich shifts by exactly r on both sides.</p></li><li><span>2</span><strong>Turn the shifted lower bound into the exact chord inequality</strong><p>Apply the shared Hessian-to-strong-convexity theorem to W and the shifted lower bound. Its scalar-line argument yields the chord correction (m+r)ab‖x−y‖²/2 without losing a factor. This is the first public conclusion.</p></li><li><span>3</span><strong>Identify the genuine derivative of the gradient and its symmetry</strong><p>Completeness supplies inverse Riesz representation. Apply it to the first derivative of W to obtain Mathlib&#x27;s actual gradient, then differentiate this fixed continuous linear transformation. The resulting operator Tₓ=D(∇W)(x) represents the genuine Hessian: ⟨Tₓv,w⟩=D²W(x)[v,w]. The C² symmetry theorem makes Tₓ symmetric; no matrix representation or finite-dimensional eigenbasis is introduced.</p></li><li><span>4</span><strong>Bound the operator norm from the diagonal sandwich</strong><p>For a symmetric operator the operator norm equals the supremum of the absolute Rayleigh quotient. The shifted lower bound and m+r≥0 make the Hessian diagonal nonnegative. For v≠0 the shifted upper bound therefore gives quotient at most L+r. At v=0 the totalized quotient is zero, so this case also satisfies the bound and does not require E to contain a nonzero vector.</p></li><li><span>5</span><strong>Use the whole-space mean-value bound</strong><p>The actual gradient is differentiable everywhere and its derivative has norm at most L+r at every point. The whole-space mean-value theorem gives global (L+r)-Lipschitz continuity, which is the precise smoothness sense returned here.</p></li></ol></section><details class="reader-ledger assumption-ledger"><summary>Full assumptions and notation</summary><h3>All objects and hypotheses</h3><ul><li>E is any complete real inner-product space, including zero dimension; no finite-dimensional or nontrivial-space assumption occurs in the theorem.</li><li>ContDiff ℝ 2 U witnesses genuine C² regularity. D²U(x)[v,v] is (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ U) x v) v. Totalized derivative notation alone would not establish that these are actual derivatives.</li><li>The lower and upper inequalities hold at every point and in every direction on the whole space. The nonnegative types m,L,r:ℝ≥0 encode their signs. In particular, nonnegative lower curvature is used to control the absolute Rayleigh quotient.</li><li>In SPHMC Lemma 6.4 use m=κ⁻¹, L=1 and r=A⁻¹. A=∞ is represented by r=0, not by declaring a zero finite variance admissible. PBPS uses the same regularized potential with m=α, L=β and r=η⁻¹, under its source-positive parameters.</li><li>No measure, normalizer, measurability or integrability is needed for the production conclusion. Focused probability consumers separately add finite dimension, a Borel measurable structure, canonical volume and positive source curvature.</li></ul><p class="muted"></p></details><h2>Mathematical proof</h2><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>1. Differentiate the actual quadratic potential</h4><p>The squared norm is smooth on a real inner-product space. Differentiate the shifted quadratic twice: its first derivative evaluates r⟨x−u,v⟩, and its second derivative evaluates r⟨v,w⟩. Thus W is genuinely C² and its diagonal Hessian is the diagonal Hessian of U plus r‖v‖². The original sandwich shifts by exactly r on both sides.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[DW(x)[v]=DU(x)[v]+r\langle x-u,v\rangle,\qquad D^2W(x)[v,w]=D^2U(x)[v,w]+r\langle v,w\rangle,\qquad(m+r)\|v\|^2\le D^2W(x)[v,v]\le(L+r)\|v\|^2.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>ContDiff.norm_sq proves hW. HasFDerivAt.norm_sq and the sum/chain rules give hq, hWfd and hWdd; hbounds evaluates the derivative on the diagonal. The local J packages v↦innerSL ℝ v as a continuous real-linear map, proving additivity and scalar compatibility because the generic innerSL API is conjugate-linear. This is an implementation adapter, not an assumed Hessian.</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>2. Turn the shifted lower bound into the exact chord inequality</h4><p>Apply the shared Hessian-to-strong-convexity theorem to W and the shifted lower bound. Its scalar-line argument yields the chord correction (m+r)ab‖x−y‖²/2 without losing a factor. This is the first public conclusion.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[D^2W(x)[v,v]\ge(m+r)\|v\|^2\quad\Longrightarrow\quad W(ax+by)\le aW(x)+bW(y)-\frac{m+r}{2}ab\|x-y\|^2.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>HessianStrongConvexity.strongConvexOn_univ_of_fderiv2_lower receives hW and the lower half of hbounds, returning StrongConvexOn Set.univ with precisely the coerced NNReal sum m+r.</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>3. Identify the genuine derivative of the gradient and its symmetry</h4><p>Completeness supplies inverse Riesz representation. Apply it to the first derivative of W to obtain Mathlib&#x27;s actual gradient, then differentiate this fixed continuous linear transformation. The resulting operator Tₓ=D(∇W)(x) represents the genuine Hessian: ⟨Tₓv,w⟩=D²W(x)[v,w]. The C² symmetry theorem makes Tₓ symmetric; no matrix representation or finite-dimensional eigenbasis is introduced.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[T_x=D(\nabla W)(x),\qquad\langle T_xv,w\rangle=D^2W(x)[v,w]=D^2W(x)[w,v]=\langle v,T_xw\rangle.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>R packages (InnerProductSpace.toDual ℝ E).symm as a continuous real-linear map. T is R.comp applied to fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ W) x. hTd is a HasFDerivAt witness for gradient W, hinner uses InnerProductSpace.toDual_symm_apply, and hsym uses ContDiffAt.isSymmSndFDerivAt.</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>4. Bound the operator norm from the diagonal sandwich</h4><p>For a symmetric operator the operator norm equals the supremum of the absolute Rayleigh quotient. The shifted lower bound and m+r≥0 make the Hessian diagonal nonnegative. For v≠0 the shifted upper bound therefore gives quotient at most L+r. At v=0 the totalized quotient is zero, so this case also satisfies the bound and does not require E to contain a nonzero vector.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\|T_x\|=\sup_v\left|\frac{\langle T_xv,v\rangle}{\|v\|^2}\right|\le L+r.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>ContinuousLinearMap.norm_eq_iSup_rayleighQuotient and ciSup_le reduce hnorm to each quotient. hbounds supplies nonnegativity and the upper estimate. The proof handles v=0 explicitly; otherwise norm_pos_iff and div_le_iff₀ justify division.</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>5. Use the whole-space mean-value bound</h4><p>The actual gradient is differentiable everywhere and its derivative has norm at most L+r at every point. The whole-space mean-value theorem gives global (L+r)-Lipschitz continuity, which is the precise smoothness sense returned here.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\|D(\nabla W)(x)\|\le L+r\quad(\forall x)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\|\nabla W(x)-\nabla W(y)\|\le(L+r)\|x-y\|\quad(\forall x,y).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>lipschitzWith_of_nnnorm_fderiv_le uses differentiability from hTd and rewrites the derivative by (hTd x).fderiv before applying hnorm. The public LipschitzWith conclusion is about gradient W itself, not a separately declared gradient or Hessian norm.</p></details></div><section class="lean-verification"><h2>Lean verification</h2><p>The exact proposition and proof begin folded so the mathematical argument remains the primary reading path.</p><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-statement" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.QuadraticRegularization.strongConvexOn_and_lipschitzWith_gradient_add_quadratic"><summary>Lean statement · strongConvexOn_and_lipschitzWith_gradient_add_quadratic</summary><p>The theorem quantifies over E with NormedAddCommGroup, InnerProductSpace ℝ and CompleteSpace; U:E→ℝ; m,L,r:ℝ≥0; and u:E. Its only mathematical input hypotheses are ContDiff ℝ 2 U and the everywhere/every-direction nested-fderiv sandwich. Its let-bound W is the actual quadratic regularization. The two conclusions are StrongConvexOn Set.univ (↑(m+r)) W and LipschitzWith (L+r) (gradient W).</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem strongConvexOn_and_lipschitzWith_gradient_add_quadratic
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E] [CompleteSpace E]
    {U : E → ℝ} {m L r : ℝ≥0}
    (hU : ContDiff ℝ 2 U)
    (hH : ∀ x v : E,
      (m : ℝ) * ‖v‖ ^ 2 ≤ (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ U) x v) v ∧
      (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ U) x v) v ≤ (L : ℝ) * ‖v‖ ^ 2)
    (u : E) :
    let W := fun x =&gt; U x + (r : ℝ) / 2 * ‖x - u‖ ^ 2
    StrongConvexOn Set.univ ((m + r : ℝ≥0) : ℝ) W ∧
      LipschitzWith (L + r) (gradient W)</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-quadraticregularization.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-proof" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.QuadraticRegularization.strongConvexOn_and_lipschitzWith_gradient_add_quadratic"><summary>Lean proof · strongConvexOn_and_lipschitzWith_gradient_add_quadratic</summary><p>One shared production theorem contains the quadratic-shift, Riesz and norm estimates as local proof terms, not public wrapper siblings. The focused tests instantiate the source constants κ⁻¹, 1, A⁻¹; separately retain r=0 and zero dimension. Under source-positive curvature and the finite-dimensional Borel canonical-volume assumptions, they use the derived strong convexity with StrongConvexGibbsIntegrability to return integrability, strictly positive Gibbs normalizer and actual probability. That derived probability feeds quadratic_tilt_tilt, with positive A⁻¹ used for the additional normalized Gaussian reweighting. Neither integrability nor probability is assumed.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem strongConvexOn_and_lipschitzWith_gradient_add_quadratic
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E] [CompleteSpace E]
    {U : E → ℝ} {m L r : ℝ≥0}
    (hU : ContDiff ℝ 2 U)
    (hH : ∀ x v : E,
      (m : ℝ) * ‖v‖ ^ 2 ≤ (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ U) x v) v ∧
      (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ U) x v) v ≤ (L : ℝ) * ‖v‖ ^ 2)
    (u : E) :
    let W := fun x =&gt; U x + (r : ℝ) / 2 * ‖x - u‖ ^ 2
    StrongConvexOn Set.univ ((m + r : ℝ≥0) : ℝ) W ∧
      LipschitzWith (L + r) (gradient W) := by
  let W := fun x =&gt; U x + (r : ℝ) / 2 * ‖x - u‖ ^ 2
  have hUd : Differentiable ℝ U := hU.differentiable (by norm_num)
  have hUdd : Differentiable ℝ (fderiv ℝ U) :=
    (hU.fderiv_right (m := 1) (by norm_num)).differentiable_one
  have hn : ContDiff ℝ 2 (fun x : E =&gt; ‖x - u‖ ^ 2) :=
    (contDiff_id.sub contDiff_const).norm_sq (𝕜 := ℝ)
  have hW : ContDiff ℝ 2 W := hU.add (contDiff_const.mul hn)
  have hq (x : E) : HasFDerivAt (fun z =&gt; (r : ℝ) / 2 * ‖z - u‖ ^ 2)
      ((r : ℝ) • innerSL ℝ (x - u)) x := by
    convert (((hasFDerivAt_id x).sub_const u).norm_sq).const_mul ((r : ℝ) / 2)
      using 1 &lt;;&gt; first | rfl | (ext v; simp; ring)
  have hWfd (x : E) : fderiv ℝ W x =
      fderiv ℝ U x + (r : ℝ) • innerSL ℝ (x - u) :=
    ((hUd x).hasFDerivAt.add (hq x)).fderiv
  let J : E →L[ℝ] (E →L[ℝ] ℝ) :=
    { toFun := fun v =&gt; innerSL ℝ v
      map_add&#x27; := by intros; ext; simp
      map_smul&#x27; := by intros; ext; simp
      cont := (innerSL ℝ (E := E)).continuous }
  have hWdd (x : E) : HasFDerivAt (fderiv ℝ W)
      (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ U) x + (r : ℝ) • J) x := by
    rw [show fderiv ℝ W = (fun z =&gt; fderiv ℝ U z +
      (r : ℝ) • innerSL ℝ (z - u)) from funext hWfd]
    convert (hUdd x).hasFDerivAt.add
      ((J.hasFDerivAt.comp x ((hasFDerivAt_id x).sub_const u)).const_smul
        (r : ℝ)) using 1 &lt;;&gt; rfl
  have hbounds (x v : E) :
      ((m + r : ℝ≥0) : ℝ) * ‖v‖ ^ 2 ≤ (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ W) x v) v ∧
      (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ W) x v) v ≤ ((L + r : ℝ≥0) : ℝ) * ‖v‖ ^ 2 := by
    rw [(hWdd x).fderiv]
    change ((m : ℝ) + r) * ‖v‖ ^ 2 ≤
        (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ U) x v) v + (r : ℝ) * inner ℝ v v ∧
      (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ U) x v) v + (r : ℝ) * inner ℝ v v ≤
        ((L : ℝ) + r) * ‖v‖ ^ 2
    rw [real_inner_self_eq_norm_sq]
    constructor &lt;;&gt; nlinarith [(hH x v).1, (hH x v).2]
  refine ⟨HessianStrongConvexity.strongConvexOn_univ_of_fderiv2_lower hW
    (fun x v =&gt; (hbounds x v).1), ?_⟩
  let R : (E →L[ℝ] ℝ) →L[ℝ] E :=
    { toFun := (toDual ℝ E).symm
      map_add&#x27; := (toDual ℝ E).symm.map_add
      map_smul&#x27; := by intros; simp
      cont := (toDual ℝ E).symm.continuous }
  let T (x : E) : E →L[ℝ] E := R.comp (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ W) x)
  have hTd (x : E) : HasFDerivAt (gradient W) (T x) x := by
    exact R.hasFDerivAt.comp x
      (((hW.fderiv_right (m := 1) (by norm_num)).differentiable_one x).hasFDerivAt)
  have hinner (x v w : E) : inner ℝ (T x v) w =
      (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ W) x v) w := by
    exact toDual_symm_apply
  have hsym (x : E) : (T x).IsSymmetric := by
    intro v w
    change inner ℝ (T x v) w = inner ℝ v (T x w)
    calc
      _ = (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ W) x v) w := hinner x v w
      _ = (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ W) x w) v :=
        hW.contDiffAt.isSymmSndFDerivAt (by norm_num) v w
      _ = inner ℝ (T x w) v := (hinner x w v).symm
      _ = inner ℝ v (T x w) := real_inner_comm _ _
  have hnorm (x : E) : ‖T x‖ ≤ ((L + r : ℝ≥0) : ℝ) := by
    rw [(T x).norm_eq_iSup_rayleighQuotient (hsym x)]
    apply ciSup_le
    intro v
    change |inner ℝ (T x v) v / ‖v‖ ^ 2| ≤ ((L + r : ℝ≥0) : ℝ)
    rw [hinner]
    have hnonneg : 0 ≤ (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ W) x v) v :=
      (mul_nonneg (NNReal.coe_nonneg _) (sq_nonneg _)).trans (hbounds x v).1
    rw [abs_of_nonneg (div_nonneg hnonneg (sq_nonneg _))]
    by_cases hv : v = 0
    · simp [hv]
      positivity
    · exact (div_le_iff₀ (sq_pos_of_pos (norm_pos_iff.mpr hv))).2 (hbounds x v).2
  apply lipschitzWith_of_nnnorm_fderiv_le (fun x =&gt; (hTd x).differentiableAt)
  intro x
  rw [(hTd x).fderiv]
  exact hnorm x

end AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.QuadraticRegularization</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-quadraticregularization.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details></section><section data-source-comparison="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.QuadraticRegularization.strongConvexOn_and_lipschitzWith_gradient_add_quadratic"><h3>Source assumptions versus formal assumptions</h3><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Actual Lean</th><th>Difference kind</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>U∈C²(R^d)</td><td>ContDiff ℝ 2 U</td><td>same</td><td>The proof obtains actual first and second Fréchet derivatives from genuine C² regularity; merely totalized nested fderiv notation would be insufficient.</td></tr><tr><td>κ⁻¹I≼∇²U(x)≼I at every x</td><td>∀ x v, ↑m*‖v‖²≤(fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ U) x v) v ∧ (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ U) x v) v≤↑L*‖v‖²</td><td>generalization</td><td>The quadratic-form Hessian meaning is retained exactly. m=κ⁻¹,L=1 recovers SPHMC; any nonnegative m,L are allowed. No unrelated field or operator-norm bound is assumed.</td></tr><tr><td>Euclidean finite-dimensional state space</td><td>Complete real inner-product space, including zero dimension</td><td>generalization</td><td>Riesz representation and the symmetric-operator Rayleigh norm formula work on complete real inner-product spaces; no finite-dimensional spectral theorem is used. Euclidean spaces satisfy completeness automatically. This does not generalize probability normalization to infinite dimension.</td></tr><tr><td>A∈(0,∞], quadratic coefficient 1/(2A)</td><td>r:ℝ≥0, quadratic coefficient ↑r/2</td><td>same</td><td>Set r=A⁻¹ for finite positive A and r=0 for A=∞. Zero precision is explicitly tested and must not be confused with a zero finite variance.</td></tr><tr><td>α_A-strong convexity and β_A-smoothness, α_A=κ⁻¹+A⁻¹, β_A=1+A⁻¹</td><td>StrongConvexOn Set.univ (↑(m+r)) W ∧ LipschitzWith (L+r) (gradient W)</td><td>same</td><td>The exact source specialization retains the chord correction α_A/2 and actual gradient Lipschitz constant β_A. Smoothness is derived, not replaced by a declared Hessian norm bound.</td></tr><tr><td>Positive lower source curvature</td><td>Nonnegative m and r are sufficient for this deterministic theorem</td><td>generalization</td><td>The lower sandwich is needed for nonnegative Rayleigh quotients. Strict positivity is not needed for strong convexity with possibly zero modulus or for gradient Lipschitz continuity; focused Gibbs tests restore source-positive curvature and finite-dimensional canonical-volume hypotheses.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Exactly the SPHMC Lemma 6.4 strong-convexity/smoothness clause, generalized as recorded. PBPS is a related prerequisite/consequence consumer, not a second direct source-proof binding. Neither normalized-law closure, condition-number recurrence, conditional-law identification, Poincaré, covariance, sampler guarantees nor costs are proved by this declaration.</p><p><strong>Encoder–denoiser:</strong> accepted · equivalent-after-elaboration</p><h4>Detected semantic differences</h4><ul><li><strong>domains</strong>: Complete real inner-product spaces, including infinite dimension and the zero space, generalize the source Euclidean domain. — The selected extraction and full lesson disclose this extension. The actual Riesz/Rayleigh/mean-value proof uses no finite-dimensional spectral basis or nonzero-vector existence.</li><li><strong>assumptions</strong>: The deterministic theorem allows nonnegative m,L,r, including degenerate zero curvature, beyond the papers&#x27; positive source-curvature regime. — Signs are explicit in NNReal. The absolute Rayleigh bound uses nonnegative lower curvature; m+r=0 yields convexity, not strict strong convexity or a Gibbs normalization claim. Positive source specializations remain valid.</li><li><strong>scopes</strong>: Direct SPHMC curvature/smoothness credit and PBPS analytic support are intentionally different; neither extends to the remainder of the source results. — Public output is the two-conclusion theorem, not local hbounds or PBPS (2.10) as a public declaration. Normalized Gibbs/RGO tests are derived consumers, not additional production conclusions or a proof of the full SPHMC lemma.</li></ul><p>A generalization is not a source correction. Proposed missing conditions require separate independent repair review. No proposed repair silently changes the original theorem.</p></section><details class="reader-ledger boundary-ledger"><summary>Scope and omitted-condition boundaries</summary><ul><li>Direct source coverage is SPHMC Lemma 6.4&#x27;s strong-convexity and smoothness clause for the actual regularized potential. Its normalized-oracle closure and condition-number recurrence (6.1) are not proved by this declaration.</li><li>PBPS (2.9) supplies a second potential consumer and (2.10) supplies its Hessian sandwich. This theorem gives an analytic consequence useful downstream, including smoothness for that potential; it does not return the Hessian sandwich as a public conclusion and is not direct formalization of all of (2.10) or Proposition 2.1.</li><li>Conditional-law identification, Poincaré or covariance bounds, invariant kernels, sampler accuracy, condition-number algebra and query costs are not proved by this declaration.</li><li>The complete-inner-product-space and nonnegative-parameter extensions concern only deterministic curvature/smoothness. They do not establish infinite-dimensional Gibbs normalization or remove positive-curvature conditions from the source sampling results.</li></ul></details><details class="proof-reader-provenance reader-ledger"><summary>Sources, dependencies, and reuse</summary><h2>Source and reuse</h2><h3>Samplinglib parents called</h3><ul><li><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-hessianstrongconvexity.html#decl-autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-hessianstrongconvexity-stron-258a330876"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.HessianStrongConvexity.strongConvexOn_univ_of_fderiv2_lower</code></a></li></ul><h3>Mathlib API called (external library)</h3><ul><li>ContDiff.norm_sq</li><li>ContDiff.differentiable</li><li>ContDiff.fderiv_right</li><li>ContDiff.differentiable_one</li><li>HasFDerivAt.norm_sq</li><li>HasFDerivAt.comp</li><li>HasFDerivAt.const_mul</li><li>HasFDerivAt.const_smul</li><li>HasFDerivAt.add</li><li>HasFDerivAt.fderiv</li><li>InnerProductSpace.toDual_symm_apply</li><li>ContDiffAt.isSymmSndFDerivAt</li><li>ContinuousLinearMap.norm_eq_iSup_rayleighQuotient</li><li>lipschitzWith_of_nnnorm_fderiv_le</li></ul><h3>Mathematical sources</h3><ul><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S6.SS2.SSS2">Chen, Chewi, Lu and Zhang, Smoothed Picard Hamiltonian Monte Carlo v1, Section 6.2.2, Lemma 6.4</a> — Direct curvature/smoothness clause for U_A,u with constants κ⁻¹+A⁻¹ and 1+A⁻¹, including A=∞ through zero precision. The five-step proof is ASTIS&#x27;s mathematical expansion, not quoted prose or the full lemma.</li><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06905v1">Chen, Chewi, Lu and Zhang, Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler v1, Proposition 2.1(i), (2.9)-(2.10), and Section 4.1</a> — Prerequisite/consequence support: the same actual regularized potential has shifted curvature and actual gradient Lipschitz continuity. The public theorem does not return (2.10)&#x27;s Hessian sandwich or identify the conditional law, Poincaré or covariance bounds.</li></ul><p>Samplinglib prose is not a quotation or a source-equivalence certificate. Definitions and aliases are explained as constructions, not counted as new mathematical proofs.</p></details></article><h3>Which proof obligations and prerequisites are covered?</h3><ul><li><span class="status status-orange">Formalized proof edge; source adapter/review separate</span> Derive the exact shifted strong-convexity chord inequality for the actual RGO potential</li><li><span class="status status-orange">Formalized proof edge; source adapter/review separate</span> Derive actual global gradient Lipschitz continuity with the exact shifted smoothness constant</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Keep the separate normalized-oracle closure and source condition-number recurrence obligations distinct from the curvature clause</li></ul></section><section id="smoothed-gibbs-potential" data-publication-item="smoothed-gibbs-potential"><h2>Actual source smoothed Gibbs potential and preserved partition</h2><p>ASTIS original mathematical restatement of the source smoothing-definition/normalization prerequisite, not a reproduced numbered theorem or full Lemma4.1.</p><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S3.SS1">arXiv:2609.06906v1 · arXiv:2609.06906v1 equation1.1 (HTML135-137), Section3.1 unnormalized smoothing definition (HTML237), Section4.1 setup (HTML403-412)</a> · faithful paraphrase</p><h3>Complete source statement (Samplinglib restatement)</h3><p>The original Gibbs mass Z_V is strictly positive and mu is a probability measure. The actual Gaussian add-noise law equals volume.withDensity(ofReal(exp(-V_eta)/Z_V)). The source unnormalized weight A_eta is integrable with integral exactly Z_V, so this same law is volume.tilted(-V_eta). A_eta is positive everywhere, V_eta is globally C2, and at every y the normalized negative-log density U_eta(y)=V_eta(y)+log Z_V.</p><ul><li>E is a finite-dimensional real inner-product space equipped with its Borel measurable space, including dimension zero. V:E-&gt;R is globally C2. alpha&gt;0, eta&gt;0, and for every x,v, alpha norm(v)^2&lt;=D2V(x)[v,v]. The source upper Hessian bound and eta upper cap are not required for this normalization/regularity adapter.</li><li>Z_V=integral exp(-V(x))dx, mu=volume.tilted(-V), C_eta=(sqrt(2*pi*eta))^(-dim E), A_eta(y)=C_eta integral exp(-V(x)-norm(y-x)^2/(2eta))dx, and the SOURCE potential is V_eta(y)=-log A_eta(y). These are actual functions and measures, not supplied normalized Gibbs certificates.</li><li>Original Gibbs integrability, positive Z_V, mu probability, actual Gaussian add-noise density, integrable A_eta and preserved partition are derived. The normalized probability-density potential is U_eta=-log(C_eta integral exp(-norm(y-x)^2/(2eta))dmu(x)); the exact identity U_eta=V_eta+log Z_V is a conclusion. No moments, minimizer, input law, conditional representative, smoothed regularity or smoothed Hessian certificate is passed.</li></ul><h3>Actual source potential, target law and normalization</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[A_\eta(y)=C_\eta\!\int e^{-V(x)-\|y-x\|^2/(2\eta)}dx,\quad V_\eta=-\log A_\eta,\quad \int A_\eta\,dy=Z_V&gt;0,\quad \pi_\eta=\frac{e^{-V_\eta}}{Z_V}dy,\quad V_\eta\in C^2,\quad U_\eta=V_\eta+\log Z_V.\]</div><h2>Read the formalized proofs</h2><p>Each statement and proof below has its own closed Lean disclosure. Samplinglib parents, Mathlib calls and external mathematical sources are distinguished in each proof.</p><article class="proof-reader" data-authored-declaration="AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.SmoothedGibbsPotential.smoothed_gibbs_potential"><div class="eyebrow">Samplinglib mathematical lesson</div><h2>Actual source smoothed Gibbs potential and preserved partition</h2><p><code>AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.SmoothedGibbsPotential.smoothed_gibbs_potential</code> · theorem · <a href="../../../lessons/index.html">Teaching coverage</a></p><section class="reader-first-pass" aria-label="First reading"><div class="reader-first-pass-label">Read this first</div><h2>Mathematical statement</h2><p class="reader-claim">The original Gibbs mass Z_V is strictly positive and mu is a probability measure. The actual Gaussian add-noise law equals volume.withDensity(ofReal(exp(-V_eta)/Z_V)). The source unnormalized weight A_eta is integrable with integral exactly Z_V, so this same law is volume.tilted(-V_eta). A_eta is positive everywhere, V_eta is globally C2, and at every y the normalized negative-log density U_eta(y)=V_eta(y)+log Z_V.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation reader-main-equation">\[A_\eta(y)=C_\eta\!\int e^{-V(x)-\|y-x\|^2/(2\eta)}dx,\quad V_\eta=-\log A_\eta,\quad \int A_\eta\,dy=Z_V&gt;0,\quad \pi_\eta=\frac{e^{-V_\eta}}{Z_V}dy,\quad V_\eta\in C^2,\quad U_\eta=V_\eta+\log Z_V.\]</div><h3>Proof roadmap</h3><ol class="proof-roadmap"><li><span>1</span><strong>Derive the original Gibbs probability</strong><p>The genuine lower Hessian produces strong convexity of V; the existing Gibbs-integrability producer yields integrable exp(-V) without a minimizer premise. The positive exponential integral gives Z_V&gt;0, and the exact exponential tilt is therefore a probability measure.</p></li><li><span>2</span><strong>Use the actual Gaussian density and regularity producer</strong><p>Apply the independently admitted shared arbitrary-probability Gaussian result to this derived mu. It identifies the true pushforward law, gives positive density and C2 of the normalizer and normalized negative-log density. These facts concern the actual two independent variables x and standard Gaussian g.</p></li><li><span>3</span><strong>Connect the source unnormalized convolution exactly</strong><p>The tilted Bochner-integral identity inserts the actual original Gibbs density. Exponential additivity and moving the fixed Z_V outside the integral yield C_eta Z_mu=A_eta/Z_V at every observation, with the exact Gaussian prefactor retained. Positivity of this density and Z_V gives A_eta&gt;0 everywhere.</p></li><li><span>4</span><strong>Derive integrability and partition preservation from probability</strong><p>The true smoothed pushforward is a probability measure. Evaluating its withDensity identification on the whole space gives lintegral ofReal(A_eta/Z_V)=1. Its continuous nonnegative density and this finite mass imply actual Bochner integrability, then ordinary integral(A_eta/Z_V)=1. Multiplication by positive Z_V gives integrability of A_eta and integral A_eta=Z_V.</p></li><li><span>5</span><strong>Identify the actual source Gibbs law</strong><p>Everywhere positivity gives exp(-V_eta)=A_eta exactly. Insert the derived integral A_eta=Z_V into the definition of exponential tilt: the actual Gaussian-smoothed law is precisely volume.tilted(-V_eta), with no supplied Gibbs or normalizer witness.</p></li><li><span>6</span><strong>Preserve the source potential convention and derive C2</strong><p>Real log_div at positive A_eta and Z_V gives the exact additive constant U_eta=V_eta+log Z_V. The shared theorem already proved C2 of U_eta; subtraction of the fixed log Z_V therefore proves C2 of the actual source V_eta. The potentials are not asserted literally equal. Neither this constant identity nor C2 proves the source score/covariance or Hessian inequalities.</p></li></ol></section><details class="reader-ledger assumption-ledger"><summary>Full assumptions and notation</summary><h3>All objects and hypotheses</h3><ul><li>E is a finite-dimensional real inner-product space equipped with its Borel measurable space, including dimension zero. V:E-&gt;R is globally C2. alpha&gt;0, eta&gt;0, and for every x,v, alpha norm(v)^2&lt;=D2V(x)[v,v]. The source upper Hessian bound and eta upper cap are not required for this normalization/regularity adapter.</li><li>Z_V=integral exp(-V(x))dx, mu=volume.tilted(-V), C_eta=(sqrt(2*pi*eta))^(-dim E), A_eta(y)=C_eta integral exp(-V(x)-norm(y-x)^2/(2eta))dx, and the SOURCE potential is V_eta(y)=-log A_eta(y). These are actual functions and measures, not supplied normalized Gibbs certificates.</li><li>Original Gibbs integrability, positive Z_V, mu probability, actual Gaussian add-noise density, integrable A_eta and preserved partition are derived. The normalized probability-density potential is U_eta=-log(C_eta integral exp(-norm(y-x)^2/(2eta))dmu(x)); the exact identity U_eta=V_eta+log Z_V is a conclusion. No moments, minimizer, input law, conditional representative, smoothed regularity or smoothed Hessian certificate is passed.</li></ul><p class="muted"></p></details><h2>Mathematical proof</h2><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>1. Derive the original Gibbs probability</h4><p>The genuine lower Hessian produces strong convexity of V; the existing Gibbs-integrability producer yields integrable exp(-V) without a minimizer premise. The positive exponential integral gives Z_V&gt;0, and the exact exponential tilt is therefore a probability measure.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[D^2V\succeq\alpha I,\ \alpha&gt;0\ \Longrightarrow\ e^{-V}\in L^1(dx),\quad Z_V&gt;0,\quad \mu(dx)=Z_V^{-1}e^{-V(x)}dx.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>HessianStrongConvexity.strongConvexOn_univ_of_fderiv2_lower; StrongConvexGibbsIntegrability.integrable_exp_neg_of_strongConvexOn; integral_exp_pos; isProbabilityMeasure_tilted</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>2. Use the actual Gaussian density and regularity producer</h4><p>Apply the independently admitted shared arbitrary-probability Gaussian result to this derived mu. It identifies the true pushforward law, gives positive density and C2 of the normalizer and normalized negative-log density. These facts concern the actual two independent variables x and standard Gaussian g.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\pi_\eta=(x+\sqrt\eta\,g)_\#(\mu\otimes\gamma)=C_\eta Z_\mu\,dy,\quad Z_\mu(y)=\int e^{-\|y-x\|^2/(2\eta)}\mu(dx).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>GaussianConvolutionRegularity.gaussian_convolution_potential_c2; hc</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>3. Connect the source unnormalized convolution exactly</h4><p>The tilted Bochner-integral identity inserts the actual original Gibbs density. Exponential additivity and moving the fixed Z_V outside the integral yield C_eta Z_mu=A_eta/Z_V at every observation, with the exact Gaussian prefactor retained. Positivity of this density and Z_V gives A_eta&gt;0 everywhere.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[C_\eta Z_\mu(y)=\frac{C_\eta}{Z_V}\int e^{-V(x)-\|y-x\|^2/(2\eta)}dx=\frac{A_\eta(y)}{Z_V}&gt;0.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>integral_tilted; Real.exp_add; integral_div; he; hA; hlaw</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>4. Derive integrability and partition preservation from probability</h4><p>The true smoothed pushforward is a probability measure. Evaluating its withDensity identification on the whole space gives lintegral ofReal(A_eta/Z_V)=1. Its continuous nonnegative density and this finite mass imply actual Bochner integrability, then ordinary integral(A_eta/Z_V)=1. Multiplication by positive Z_V gives integrability of A_eta and integral A_eta=Z_V.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\int^{\!-}\!\operatorname{ofReal}(A_\eta/Z_V)\,dy=1\ \Longrightarrow\ A_\eta\in L^1(dy),\quad\int A_\eta\,dy=Z_V.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>Measure.isProbabilityMeasure_map; withDensity_apply; lintegral_ofReal_ne_top_iff_integrable; ofReal_integral_eq_lintegral_ofReal; hiD; hIntD; hiA; hIntA</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>5. Identify the actual source Gibbs law</h4><p>Everywhere positivity gives exp(-V_eta)=A_eta exactly. Insert the derived integral A_eta=Z_V into the definition of exponential tilt: the actual Gaussian-smoothed law is precisely volume.tilted(-V_eta), with no supplied Gibbs or normalizer witness.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[e^{-V_\eta(y)}=A_\eta(y),\quad \pi_\eta(dy)=\frac{e^{-V_\eta(y)}}{\int e^{-V_\eta}}dy=\operatorname{tilt}_{dy}(-V_\eta).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>Measure.tilted; Real.exp_log; hIntA; hTilt</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>6. Preserve the source potential convention and derive C2</h4><p>Real log_div at positive A_eta and Z_V gives the exact additive constant U_eta=V_eta+log Z_V. The shared theorem already proved C2 of U_eta; subtraction of the fixed log Z_V therefore proves C2 of the actual source V_eta. The potentials are not asserted literally equal. Neither this constant identity nor C2 proves the source score/covariance or Hessian inequalities.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[U_\eta=-\log(A_\eta/Z_V)=V_\eta+\log Z_V,\quad V_\eta=U_\eta-\log Z_V\in C^2.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>Real.log_div; hv; ContDiff.sub; contDiff_const</p></details></div><section class="lean-verification"><h2>Lean verification</h2><p>The exact proposition and proof begin folded so the mathematical argument remains the primary reading path.</p><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-statement" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.SmoothedGibbsPotential.smoothed_gibbs_potential"><summary>Lean statement · smoothed_gibbs_potential</summary><p>Actual source unnormalized Gaussian convolution positive/integrable, preserved original Gibbs partition, actual smoothed Gibbs law, C2 potential and exact normalized constant shift.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem smoothed_gibbs_potential {V : E → ℝ} {α η : ℝ} (hα : 0 &lt; α) (hV : ContDiff ℝ 2 V)
    (hH : ∀ x v : E, α*‖v‖^2 ≤ fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ V) x v v) (hη : 0 &lt; η) :
    let ZV := ∫ x, Real.exp (-V x) ∂(volume : Measure E)
    let μ := (volume : Measure E).tilted (fun x =&gt; -V x)
    let C := ((Real.sqrt (2*Real.pi*η))⁻¹)^Module.finrank ℝ E
    let A := fun y : E =&gt; C*∫ x, Real.exp (-V x-‖y-x‖^2/(2*η)) ∂(volume : Measure E)
    0 &lt; ZV ∧ IsProbabilityMeasure μ ∧
      (μ.prod (stdGaussian E)).map (fun p =&gt; p.1+Real.sqrt η • p.2) =
        (volume : Measure E).withDensity
          (fun y =&gt; ENNReal.ofReal (Real.exp (-(-Real.log (A y)))/ZV)) ∧
      Integrable A (volume : Measure E) ∧
      (∫ y, A y ∂(volume : Measure E)) = ZV ∧
      (μ.prod (stdGaussian E)).map (fun p =&gt; p.1+Real.sqrt η • p.2) =
        (volume : Measure E).tilted (fun y =&gt; -(-Real.log (A y))) ∧
      (∀ y, 0 &lt; A y) ∧
      ContDiff ℝ 2 (fun y =&gt; -Real.log (A y)) ∧
      ∀ y, -Real.log (C*∫ x, Real.exp (-‖y-x‖^2/(2*η)) ∂μ) =
        -Real.log (A y)+Real.log ZV</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-examplecases-smoothedpicardhmc-smoothedgibbspotential.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-proof" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.SmoothedGibbsPotential.smoothed_gibbs_potential"><summary>Lean proof · smoothed_gibbs_potential</summary><p>Genuine Hessian-to-Gibbs normalization, shared actual Gaussian law, exact tilted integral, true probability total mass, derived integrability/partition, source tilt and positive logarithm.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem smoothed_gibbs_potential {V : E → ℝ} {α η : ℝ} (hα : 0 &lt; α) (hV : ContDiff ℝ 2 V)
    (hH : ∀ x v : E, α*‖v‖^2 ≤ fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ V) x v v) (hη : 0 &lt; η) :
    let ZV := ∫ x, Real.exp (-V x) ∂(volume : Measure E)
    let μ := (volume : Measure E).tilted (fun x =&gt; -V x)
    let C := ((Real.sqrt (2*Real.pi*η))⁻¹)^Module.finrank ℝ E
    let A := fun y : E =&gt; C*∫ x, Real.exp (-V x-‖y-x‖^2/(2*η)) ∂(volume : Measure E)
    0 &lt; ZV ∧ IsProbabilityMeasure μ ∧
      (μ.prod (stdGaussian E)).map (fun p =&gt; p.1+Real.sqrt η • p.2) =
        (volume : Measure E).withDensity
          (fun y =&gt; ENNReal.ofReal (Real.exp (-(-Real.log (A y)))/ZV)) ∧
      Integrable A (volume : Measure E) ∧
      (∫ y, A y ∂(volume : Measure E)) = ZV ∧
      (μ.prod (stdGaussian E)).map (fun p =&gt; p.1+Real.sqrt η • p.2) =
        (volume : Measure E).tilted (fun y =&gt; -(-Real.log (A y))) ∧
      (∀ y, 0 &lt; A y) ∧
      ContDiff ℝ 2 (fun y =&gt; -Real.log (A y)) ∧
      ∀ y, -Real.log (C*∫ x, Real.exp (-‖y-x‖^2/(2*η)) ∂μ) =
        -Real.log (A y)+Real.log ZV := by
  let ZV := ∫ x, Real.exp (-V x) ∂(volume : Measure E)
  let μ := (volume : Measure E).tilted (fun x =&gt; -V x)
  let C := ((Real.sqrt (2*Real.pi*η))⁻¹)^Module.finrank ℝ E
  let A := fun y : E =&gt; C*∫ x, Real.exp (-V x-‖y-x‖^2/(2*η)) ∂(volume : Measure E)
  have hi := AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.StrongConvexGibbsIntegrability.integrable_exp_neg_of_strongConvexOn
    hα (hV.differentiable (by norm_num))
    (AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.HessianStrongConvexity.strongConvexOn_univ_of_fderiv2_lower hV hH)
  have hZ : 0 &lt; ZV := integral_exp_pos hi
  have : IsProbabilityMeasure μ := isProbabilityMeasure_tilted hi
  have hc := AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.GaussianConvolutionRegularity.gaussian_convolution_potential_c2 μ hη
  have he (y : E) : C*∫ x, Real.exp (-‖y-x‖^2/(2*η)) ∂μ = A y/ZV := by
    rw [show μ = (volume : Measure E).tilted (fun x =&gt; -V x) from rfl,integral_tilted]
    dsimp only [A,ZV]
    rw [show (C*∫ x, Real.exp (-V x-‖y-x‖^2/(2*η)) ∂(volume : Measure E))/
        (∫ x, Real.exp (-V x) ∂(volume : Measure E)) =
      C*((∫ x, Real.exp (-V x-‖y-x‖^2/(2*η)) ∂(volume : Measure E))/
        (∫ x, Real.exp (-V x) ∂(volume : Measure E))) by ring]
    congr 1
    rw [← integral_div]
    apply integral_congr_ae
    filter_upwards with x
    rw [smul_eq_mul,show Real.exp (-V x-‖y-x‖^2/(2*η)) =
      Real.exp (-V x)*Real.exp (-‖y-x‖^2/(2*η)) by
        rw [show -V x-‖y-x‖^2/(2*η) = -V x+(-‖y-x‖^2/(2*η)) by ring,Real.exp_add]]
    ring
  have hA (y : E) : 0 &lt; A y :=
    (div_pos_iff_of_pos_right hZ).mp (he y ▸ hc.2.1 y)
  have hv (y : E) : -Real.log (C*∫ x, Real.exp (-‖y-x‖^2/(2*η)) ∂μ) =
      -Real.log (A y)+Real.log ZV := by
    rw [he,Real.log_div (hA y).ne&#x27; hZ.ne&#x27;]; ring
  have hlaw : (μ.prod (stdGaussian E)).map (fun p =&gt; p.1+Real.sqrt η • p.2) =
      (volume : Measure E).withDensity (fun y =&gt; ENNReal.ofReal (A y/ZV)) := by
    convert hc.1 using 1
    congr 1
    funext y
    exact congrArg ENNReal.ofReal (he y).symm
  have hDcont : Continuous (fun y =&gt; A y/ZV) := by
    have hefun : (fun y =&gt; A y/ZV) =
        (fun y =&gt; C*∫ x, Real.exp (-‖y-x‖^2/(2*η)) ∂μ) :=
      funext (fun y =&gt; (he y).symm)
    rw [hefun]
    exact continuous_const.mul hc.2.2.1.continuous
  have hnonneg : ∀ y, 0 ≤ A y/ZV := fun y =&gt; (div_pos (hA y) hZ).le
  have : IsProbabilityMeasure ((μ.prod (stdGaussian E)).map
      (fun p =&gt; p.1+Real.sqrt η • p.2)) := Measure.isProbabilityMeasure_map (by fun_prop)
  have hm : ∫⁻ y, ENNReal.ofReal (A y/ZV) ∂(volume : Measure E) = 1 := by
    have hm := (congrArg (fun m : Measure E =&gt; m Set.univ) hlaw).symm
    simpa only [withDensity_apply _ MeasurableSet.univ,setLIntegral_univ,
      measure_univ] using hm
  have hiD : Integrable (fun y =&gt; A y/ZV) (volume : Measure E) :=
    (lintegral_ofReal_ne_top_iff_integrable hDcont.aestronglyMeasurable
      (Filter.Eventually.of_forall hnonneg)).mp (by rw [hm]; exact ENNReal.one_ne_top)
  have hIntD : (∫ y, A y/ZV ∂(volume : Measure E)) = 1 := by
    have hp := ofReal_integral_eq_lintegral_ofReal hiD (Filter.Eventually.of_forall hnonneg)
    rw [hm] at hp
    have ht := congrArg ENNReal.toReal hp
    simpa only [ENNReal.toReal_ofReal (integral_nonneg hnonneg),ENNReal.toReal_one] using ht
  have hiA : Integrable A (volume : Measure E) := by
    convert hiD.mul_const ZV using 1
    funext y
    exact (div_mul_cancel₀ (A y) hZ.ne&#x27;).symm
  have hIntA : (∫ y, A y ∂(volume : Measure E)) = ZV := by
    rw [integral_div] at hIntD
    simpa only [one_mul] using (div_eq_iff hZ.ne&#x27;).mp hIntD
  have hTilt : (μ.prod (stdGaussian E)).map (fun p =&gt; p.1+Real.sqrt η • p.2) =
      (volume : Measure E).tilted (fun y =&gt; -(-Real.log (A y))) := by
    rw [Measure.tilted]
    simp_rw [neg_neg,Real.exp_log (hA _)]
    rw [hIntA]
    exact hlaw
  refine ⟨hZ,inferInstance,?_,hiA,hIntA,hTilt,hA,?_,hv⟩
  · change (μ.prod (stdGaussian E)).map (fun p =&gt; p.1+Real.sqrt η • p.2) =
      (volume : Measure E).withDensity (fun y =&gt; ENNReal.ofReal (Real.exp (-(-Real.log (A y)))/ZV))
    convert hlaw using 1
    congr 1
    funext y
    rw [neg_neg,Real.exp_log (hA y)]
  have hg : (fun y =&gt; -Real.log (A y)) =
      (fun y =&gt; -Real.log (C*∫ x, Real.exp (-‖y-x‖^2/(2*η)) ∂μ)-Real.log ZV) := by
    funext y; rw [hv]; ring
  rw [hg]
  exact hc.2.2.2.sub contDiff_const

end
end AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.SmoothedGibbsPotential</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-examplecases-smoothedpicardhmc-smoothedgibbspotential.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details></section><section data-source-comparison="AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.SmoothedGibbsPotential.smoothed_gibbs_potential"><h3>Source assumptions versus formal assumptions</h3><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Actual Lean</th><th>Difference kind</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Equation1.1 explicitly V in C2 with positive lower and upper Hessian bounds; Section3.1 eta&gt;0 and unnormalized exp(-V_eta)=exp(-V)*Gaussian.</td><td>E is a finite-dimensional real inner-product space equipped with its Borel measurable space, including dimension zero. V:E-&gt;R is globally C2. alpha&gt;0, eta&gt;0, and for every x,v, alpha norm(v)^2&lt;=D2V(x)[v,v]. The source upper Hessian bound and eta upper cap are not required for this normalization/regularity adapter.</td><td>generalization</td><td>The lower Hessian suffices for original Gibbs integrability; no upper Hessian or eta cap is consumed. Finite-dimensional Borel Hilbert formulation includes dimension zero.</td></tr><tr><td>Source smoothed potential is unnormalized; target probability law is normalized separately.</td><td>The original Gibbs mass Z_V is strictly positive and mu is a probability measure. The actual Gaussian add-noise law equals volume.withDensity(ofReal(exp(-V_eta)/Z_V)). The source unnormalized weight A_eta is integrable with integral exactly Z_V, so this same law is volume.tilted(-V_eta). A_eta is positive everywhere, V_eta is globally C2, and at every y the normalized negative-log density U_eta(y)=V_eta(y)+log Z_V.</td><td>source-implicit</td><td>The exact source convolution, preserved partition and normalized potential constant are derived from real measure semantics and source Gibbs hypotheses.</td></tr><tr><td>Equation4.1 and Lemma4.1 use posterior derivative identities and quantitative Hessian bounds.</td><td>Only source potential C2, normalization and actual law are proved.</td><td>unresolved</td><td>Actual source unnormalized smoothed potential V_eta, positive finite partition Z_V preserved by smoothing, genuine original and smoothed Gibbs probability laws, normalized U_eta=V_eta+log Z_V and source V_eta C2. No posterior score/covariance, curvature/higher derivative inequalities, exact-flow stationarity, numerical accuracy/stochastic errors/history/work, PBPS process or either complete main/composition. TV never transfers unbounded expected cost.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Actual source unnormalized smoothed potential V_eta, positive finite partition Z_V preserved by smoothing, genuine original and smoothed Gibbs probability laws, normalized U_eta=V_eta+log Z_V and source V_eta C2. No posterior score/covariance, curvature/higher derivative inequalities, exact-flow stationarity, numerical accuracy/stochastic errors/history/work, PBPS process or either complete main/composition. TV never transfers unbounded expected cost.</p><p><strong>Encoder–denoiser:</strong> accepted · equivalent-after-elaboration</p><p>A generalization is not a source correction. Proposed missing conditions require separate independent repair review. No proposed repair silently changes the original theorem.</p></section><details class="reader-ledger boundary-ledger"><summary>Scope and omitted-condition boundaries</summary><ul><li>Actual source unnormalized smoothed potential V_eta, positive finite partition Z_V preserved by smoothing, genuine original and smoothed Gibbs probability laws, normalized U_eta=V_eta+log Z_V and source V_eta C2. No posterior score/covariance, curvature/higher derivative inequalities, exact-flow stationarity, numerical accuracy/stochastic errors/history/work, PBPS process or either complete main/composition. TV never transfers unbounded expected cost.</li></ul></details><details class="proof-reader-provenance reader-ledger"><summary>Sources, dependencies, and reuse</summary><h2>Source and reuse</h2><h3>Samplinglib parents called</h3><ul><li><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-measure-gaussianconvolutionregularity.html#decl-autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-measure-gaussianconvolutionregularity-66a7ac0f88"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.GaussianConvolutionRegularity.gaussian_convolution_potential_c2</code></a></li><li><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-strongconvexgibbsintegrability.html#decl-autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-strongconvexgibbsintegrabili-51ed1587ae"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.StrongConvexGibbsIntegrability.integrable_exp_neg_of_strongConvexOn</code></a></li><li><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-hessianstrongconvexity.html#decl-autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-hessianstrongconvexity-stron-258a330876"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.HessianStrongConvexity.strongConvexOn_univ_of_fderiv2_lower</code></a></li></ul><h3>Mathlib API called (external library)</h3><ul><li>integral_exp_pos</li><li>isProbabilityMeasure_tilted</li><li>integral_tilted</li><li>Real.exp_add</li><li>Measure.isProbabilityMeasure_map</li><li>withDensity_apply</li><li>lintegral_ofReal_ne_top_iff_integrable</li><li>ofReal_integral_eq_lintegral_ofReal</li><li>integral_div</li><li>Real.exp_log</li><li>Measure.tilted</li><li>Real.log_div</li><li>ContDiff.sub</li></ul><h3>Mathematical sources</h3><ul><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S3.SS1">SPHMC source smoothing definition and Gibbs normalization</a> — Actual source unnormalized smoothed potential V_eta, positive finite partition Z_V preserved by smoothing, genuine original and smoothed Gibbs probability laws, normalized U_eta=V_eta+log Z_V and source V_eta C2. No posterior score/covariance, curvature/higher derivative inequalities, exact-flow stationarity, numerical accuracy/stochastic errors/history/work, PBPS process or either complete main/composition. TV never transfers unbounded expected cost.</li></ul><p>Samplinglib prose is not a quotation or a source-equivalence certificate. Definitions and aliases are explained as constructions, not counted as new mathematical proofs.</p></details></article><h3>Which proof obligations and prerequisites are covered?</h3><ul><li><span class="status status-green">Independently verified prerequisite</span> Actual unnormalized source potential, preserved partition, Gibbs target law and C2</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Equation4.1 score/covariance and Lemma4.1 Hessian estimates (remaining)</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Dynamics, errors/work, both main/composition and acceptance (remaining)</li></ul></section><section id="sphmc-actual-contraction-1" data-publication-item="sphmc-actual-contraction-1"><h2>For every actual input pair and every common two-vector noise value, the source exact-gradient numerical phase obeys the M_kappa norm contraction with simultaneous explicit universal constant c=1/65536 under h Lambda_J&lt;=c/kappa</h2><p>Fan Chen, Sinho Chewi, Jianfeng Lu and Matthew S. Zhang. Exact source numerical phase/metric, explicit constant elaboration, actual normalized-curvature producer boundary.</p><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.SS3">arXiv:2609.06906v1 · Proposition4.7 HTML590-620; exact M_kappa display preceding Theorem4.5</a> · faithful paraphrase</p><h3>Complete source statement (Samplinglib restatement)</h3><p>For every actual input pair and every common two-vector noise value, the source exact-gradient numerical phase obeys the M_kappa norm contraction with simultaneous explicit universal constant c=1/65536 under h Lambda_J&lt;=c/kappa. The actual averaged Hessian and remainder are supplied by the independently compiled genuine algorithm producer, never assumed as a contraction/phase certificate.</p><ul><li>E is a complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel sigma algebra, including dimension zero. f:E-&gt;R is genuinely globally C2, kappa&gt;=1, and (2kappa)^-1||v||²&lt;=D²f(z)[v,v]&lt;=||v||² for all z,v. This is the explicit normalized potential interface; actual V_eta construction is an independent producer.</li><li>J&gt;=2,h&gt;0. Actual nodes t_j=h/2(1-cos(j*pi/(J-1))), actual Lagrange cardinals ell_j, Lambda_J=sup_[0,h] sum|ell_j|, omega_ij=integral_0^{t_i}(t_i-s)ell_j(s)ds, b_j=integral_0^h ell_j(s)ds and c_j=integral_0^h(h-s)ell_j(s)ds. The sole step restriction is h Lambda_J&lt;=1/(65536 kappa); h&lt;=1 and h²Lambda&lt;=1 are derived.</li><li>a=exp(-h/2), sigma=sqrt(1-exp(-h)). P0=a p+sigma zeta0; Y0_j=x+t_j P0; Y1_i=Y0_i-sum omega_ij gradient f(Y0_j); Phi=(x+hP0-sum c_j gradient f(Y1_j), a(P0-sum b_j gradient f(Y1_j))+sigma zeta1). This is the actual exact-gradient phase, shared synchronously at every input/noise pair, not only almost surely.</li><li>Q_kappa(x,p)=(1/(2kappa)+1/2)||x||²+inner(x,p)+||p||² and norm_M=sqrt(Q). The proof uses genuine Euclidean L2 phase norms throughout; it never treats the default product max norm as Euclidean.</li></ul><h3>Exact source metric/actual contraction</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[h\Lambda_J\le\frac1{65536\kappa}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\|\Phi_z(\zeta)-\Phi_{z\prime}(\zeta)\|_{M_\kappa}\le\left(1-\frac{h}{65536\kappa}\right)\|z-z\prime\|_{M_\kappa}.\]</div><h2>Read the formalized proofs</h2><p>Each statement and proof below has its own closed Lean disclosure. Samplinglib parents, Mathlib calls and external mathematical sources are distinguished in each proof.</p><article class="proof-reader" data-authored-declaration="AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.ActualContraction.source_synchronous_contraction"><div class="eyebrow">Samplinglib mathematical lesson</div><h2>For every actual input pair and every common two-vector noise value, the source exact-gradient numerical phase obeys the M_kappa norm contraction with simultaneous explicit universal constant c=1/65536 under h Lambda_J&lt;=c/kappa</h2><p><code>AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.ActualContraction.source_synchronous_contraction</code> · theorem · <a href="../../../lessons/index.html">Teaching coverage</a></p><section class="reader-first-pass" aria-label="First reading"><div class="reader-first-pass-label">Read this first</div><h2>Mathematical statement</h2><p class="reader-claim">For every actual input pair and every common two-vector noise value, the source exact-gradient numerical phase obeys the M_kappa norm contraction with simultaneous explicit universal constant c=1/65536 under h Lambda_J&lt;=c/kappa. The actual averaged Hessian and remainder are supplied by the independently compiled genuine algorithm producer, never assumed as a contraction/phase certificate.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation reader-main-equation">\[h\Lambda_J\le\frac1{65536\kappa}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\|\Phi_z(\zeta)-\Phi_{z\prime}(\zeta)\|_{M_\kappa}\le\left(1-\frac{h}{65536\kappa}\right)\|z-z\prime\|_{M_\kappa}.\]</div><h3>Proof roadmap</h3><ol class="proof-roadmap"><li><span>1</span><strong>Reuse the actual numerical expansion</strong><p>The real coefficient producer gives Lambda&gt;=1. The sole source step restriction derives h&lt;=1 and h²Lambda&lt;=1. Call the actual FirstOrderDifference theorem on this same Phi, obtaining genuine H, curvature, norm&lt;=1 and Delta1=d+hA+Rd with ||R||&lt;=8Lambda h². Its actual measurable Gaussian Markov semantics were established in the parent.</p></li><li><span>2</span><strong>Call the existing exact source norm comparisons</strong><p>Open the already proved Q definition and two comparisons as public APIs, preserving their original proofs. Polarize Q into B with coefficient alpha+1/2 on the xx block, 1/2 on both mixed blocks and1 on pp. Coordinate Cauchy--Schwarz yields B(u,v)&lt;=3||u||2||v||2. No duplicate norm-comparison proof or new wrapper is introduced.</p></li><li><span>3</span><strong>Consume genuine kinetic dissipation</strong><p>The parent all-vector H curvature bounds feed the compiled full-space source drift theorem. Exact real inner-product algebra gives 2B(d,A)=-D_H(d)&lt;=-Q(d)/(8kappa). The operator and coordinate triangle bounds imply ||A||&lt;=3||d||, so Q(A)&lt;=162Q(d); this constant is a deliberate conservative weakening.</p></li><li><span>4</span><strong>Expand the actual endpoint energy</strong><p>For r=Rd and L=Lambda h²&lt;=1, derive ||d+hA||&lt;=4||d|| and ||r||&lt;=8L||d||. Thus the cross remainder is &lt;=1152LQ(d), the remainder energy is &lt;=1152LQ(d) using L²&lt;=L, and h²Q(A)&lt;=162LQ(d) using Lambda&gt;=1. The exact polarized identity and drift imply the full endpoint bound.</p></li><li><span>5</span><strong>Absorb with an explicit universal step constant</strong><p>The actual step condition and2466&lt;4096 give2466Lambda h²&lt;=h/(16kappa). Hence the genuine endpoint Q is &lt;=(1-h/(16kappa))Q(d). No strengthened convenient h restriction or suppressed dimension dependence is added.</p></li><li><span>6</span><strong>Take square roots without division by input energy</strong><p>Let t=h/(65536kappa), so0&lt;=t&lt;=1. The stronger squared loss4096t is bounded by the square of1-t. Nonnegativity of both Q values follows from the original lower comparison. Real square-root identities and monotonicity give the target. No division by Q(d), so equal inputs and dimension0 are included.</p></li></ol></section><details class="reader-ledger assumption-ledger"><summary>Full assumptions and notation</summary><h3>All objects and hypotheses</h3><ul><li>E is a complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel sigma algebra, including dimension zero. f:E-&gt;R is genuinely globally C2, kappa&gt;=1, and (2kappa)^-1||v||²&lt;=D²f(z)[v,v]&lt;=||v||² for all z,v. This is the explicit normalized potential interface; actual V_eta construction is an independent producer.</li><li>J&gt;=2,h&gt;0. Actual nodes t_j=h/2(1-cos(j*pi/(J-1))), actual Lagrange cardinals ell_j, Lambda_J=sup_[0,h] sum|ell_j|, omega_ij=integral_0^{t_i}(t_i-s)ell_j(s)ds, b_j=integral_0^h ell_j(s)ds and c_j=integral_0^h(h-s)ell_j(s)ds. The sole step restriction is h Lambda_J&lt;=1/(65536 kappa); h&lt;=1 and h²Lambda&lt;=1 are derived.</li><li>a=exp(-h/2), sigma=sqrt(1-exp(-h)). P0=a p+sigma zeta0; Y0_j=x+t_j P0; Y1_i=Y0_i-sum omega_ij gradient f(Y0_j); Phi=(x+hP0-sum c_j gradient f(Y1_j), a(P0-sum b_j gradient f(Y1_j))+sigma zeta1). This is the actual exact-gradient phase, shared synchronously at every input/noise pair, not only almost surely.</li><li>Q_kappa(x,p)=(1/(2kappa)+1/2)||x||²+inner(x,p)+||p||² and norm_M=sqrt(Q). The proof uses genuine Euclidean L2 phase norms throughout; it never treats the default product max norm as Euclidean.</li></ul><p class="muted"></p></details><h2>Mathematical proof</h2><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>1. Reuse the actual numerical expansion</h4><p>The real coefficient producer gives Lambda&gt;=1. The sole source step restriction derives h&lt;=1 and h²Lambda&lt;=1. Call the actual FirstOrderDifference theorem on this same Phi, obtaining genuine H, curvature, norm&lt;=1 and Delta1=d+hA+Rd with ||R||&lt;=8Lambda h². Its actual measurable Gaussian Markov semantics were established in the parent.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\Delta_1=d+hA+Rd,\quad A=(\Delta p,-H\Delta x-\Delta p),\quad\|R\|\le8\Lambda_Jh^2.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>source_first_order_difference; chebyshev_lobatto_coefficients; hfirst; hp; hr</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>2. Call the existing exact source norm comparisons</h4><p>Open the already proved Q definition and two comparisons as public APIs, preserving their original proofs. Polarize Q into B with coefficient alpha+1/2 on the xx block, 1/2 on both mixed blocks and1 on pp. Coordinate Cauchy--Schwarz yields B(u,v)&lt;=3||u||2||v||2. No duplicate norm-comparison proof or new wrapper is introduced.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\|d\|_2^2\le6Q(d),\quad Q(d)\le\tfrac32\|d\|_2^2,\quad B(u,v)\le3\|u\|_2\|v\|_2.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>phaseQuadraticForm; ordinary_energy_le_phaseQuadraticForm; phaseQuadraticForm_le_ordinary_energy; B_bound; B_self</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>3. Consume genuine kinetic dissipation</h4><p>The parent all-vector H curvature bounds feed the compiled full-space source drift theorem. Exact real inner-product algebra gives 2B(d,A)=-D_H(d)&lt;=-Q(d)/(8kappa). The operator and coordinate triangle bounds imply ||A||&lt;=3||d||, so Q(A)&lt;=162Q(d); this constant is a deliberate conservative weakening.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[2B(d,A)\le-\frac{Q(d)}{8\kappa},\quad\|A\|_2\le3\|d\|_2,\quad Q(A)\le162Q(d).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>source_kinetic_dissipation; hD; hidentity; hlin; hA; hQA</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>4. Expand the actual endpoint energy</h4><p>For r=Rd and L=Lambda h²&lt;=1, derive ||d+hA||&lt;=4||d|| and ||r||&lt;=8L||d||. Thus the cross remainder is &lt;=1152LQ(d), the remainder energy is &lt;=1152LQ(d) using L²&lt;=L, and h²Q(A)&lt;=162LQ(d) using Lambda&gt;=1. The exact polarized identity and drift imply the full endpoint bound.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[Q(d+hA+r)=Q(d)+2hB(d,A)+h^2Q(A)+2B(d+hA,r)+Q(r)\le\left(1-\frac h{8\kappa}+2466\Lambda_Jh^2\right)Q(d).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>quadratic_expansion; endpoint_quadratic_contraction; hcross; hqr; hAterm; hsum</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>5. Absorb with an explicit universal step constant</h4><p>The actual step condition and2466&lt;4096 give2466Lambda h²&lt;=h/(16kappa). Hence the genuine endpoint Q is &lt;=(1-h/(16kappa))Q(d). No strengthened convenient h restriction or suppressed dimension dependence is added.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[2466\Lambda_Jh^2\le\frac h{16\kappa},\quad Q(\Delta_1)\le\left(1-\frac h{16\kappa}\right)Q(d).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>habsorb; hcoef; hquad</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>6. Take square roots without division by input energy</h4><p>Let t=h/(65536kappa), so0&lt;=t&lt;=1. The stronger squared loss4096t is bounded by the square of1-t. Nonnegativity of both Q values follows from the original lower comparison. Real square-root identities and monotonicity give the target. No division by Q(d), so equal inputs and dimension0 are included.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[1-4096t\le(1-t)^2,\quad\sqrt{Q(\Delta_1)}\le(1-t)\sqrt{Q(d)}.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>hQd0; hQq0; hratio; hquadprime; Real.sq_sqrt; sq_le_sq₀</p></details></div><section class="lean-verification"><h2>Lean verification</h2><p>The exact proposition and proof begin folded so the mathematical argument remains the primary reading path.</p><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-statement" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.ActualContraction.source_synchronous_contraction"><summary>Lean statement · source_synchronous_contraction</summary><p>For every actual input pair and every common two-vector noise value, the source exact-gradient numerical phase obeys the M_kappa norm contraction with simultaneous explicit universal constant c=1/65536 under h Lambda_J&lt;=c/kappa. The actual averaged Hessian and remainder are supplied by the independently compiled genuine algorithm producer, never assumed as a contraction/phase certificate.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem source_synchronous_contraction {f : E → ℝ} {κ h : ℝ} {J : ℕ}
    (hκ : 1 ≤ κ) (hf : ContDiff ℝ 2 f)
    (hH : ∀ z v, (1 / (2 * κ)) * ‖v‖ ^ 2 ≤ (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ f) z v) v ∧
      (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ f) z v) v ≤ ‖v‖ ^ 2)
    (hJ : 2 ≤ J) (hh : 0 &lt; h) :
    let t := fun i : Fin J =&gt; h/2*(1-Real.cos ((i : ℝ)/(J-1 : ℝ)*Real.pi))
    let ell := fun j : Fin J =&gt; Lagrange.basis Finset.univ t j
    let Λ := sSup ((fun s : ℝ =&gt; ∑ j : Fin J, |(ell j).eval s|) &#x27;&#x27; Icc 0 h)
    let ω := fun i j : Fin J =&gt; ∫ s in 0..t i, (t i-s)*(ell j).eval s
    let b := fun j : Fin J =&gt; ∫ s in 0..h, (ell j).eval s
    let c := fun j : Fin J =&gt; ∫ s in 0..h, (h-s)*(ell j).eval s
    h * Λ ≤ 1/(65536*κ) →
    let a := Real.exp (-h / 2)
    let sigma := Real.sqrt (1 - Real.exp (-h))
    let P0 := fun (z ζ : E × E) =&gt; a • z.2 + sigma • ζ.1
    let Y0 := fun (z ζ : E × E) j =&gt; z.1 + t j • P0 z ζ
    let Y1 := fun (z ζ : E × E) i =&gt; Y0 z ζ i - ∑ j, ω i j • gradient f (Y0 z ζ j)
    let Φ := fun w : (E × E) × (E × E) =&gt;
      (w.1.1 + h • P0 w.1 w.2 - ∑ j, c j • gradient f (Y1 w.1 w.2 j),
        a • (P0 w.1 w.2 - ∑ j, b j • gradient f (Y1 w.1 w.2 j)) + sigma • w.2.2)
    ∀ z z&#x27; ζ : E × E,
      Real.sqrt (phaseQuadraticForm κ ((Φ (z,ζ)).1-(Φ (z&#x27;,ζ)).1,
        (Φ (z,ζ)).2-(Φ (z&#x27;,ζ)).2)) ≤
      (1-h/(65536*κ))*Real.sqrt (phaseQuadraticForm κ (z.1-z&#x27;.1,z.2-z&#x27;.2))</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-examplecases-smoothedpicardhmc-actualcontraction.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-proof" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.ActualContraction.source_synchronous_contraction"><summary>Lean proof · source_synchronous_contraction</summary><p>Exact source form and compiled parent composition, with all source domains/constant absorption and zero cases explicit. Existing Q/comparison proofs unchanged.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem source_synchronous_contraction {f : E → ℝ} {κ h : ℝ} {J : ℕ}
    (hκ : 1 ≤ κ) (hf : ContDiff ℝ 2 f)
    (hH : ∀ z v, (1 / (2 * κ)) * ‖v‖ ^ 2 ≤ (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ f) z v) v ∧
      (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ f) z v) v ≤ ‖v‖ ^ 2)
    (hJ : 2 ≤ J) (hh : 0 &lt; h) :
    let t := fun i : Fin J =&gt; h/2*(1-Real.cos ((i : ℝ)/(J-1 : ℝ)*Real.pi))
    let ell := fun j : Fin J =&gt; Lagrange.basis Finset.univ t j
    let Λ := sSup ((fun s : ℝ =&gt; ∑ j : Fin J, |(ell j).eval s|) &#x27;&#x27; Icc 0 h)
    let ω := fun i j : Fin J =&gt; ∫ s in 0..t i, (t i-s)*(ell j).eval s
    let b := fun j : Fin J =&gt; ∫ s in 0..h, (ell j).eval s
    let c := fun j : Fin J =&gt; ∫ s in 0..h, (h-s)*(ell j).eval s
    h * Λ ≤ 1/(65536*κ) →
    let a := Real.exp (-h / 2)
    let sigma := Real.sqrt (1 - Real.exp (-h))
    let P0 := fun (z ζ : E × E) =&gt; a • z.2 + sigma • ζ.1
    let Y0 := fun (z ζ : E × E) j =&gt; z.1 + t j • P0 z ζ
    let Y1 := fun (z ζ : E × E) i =&gt; Y0 z ζ i - ∑ j, ω i j • gradient f (Y0 z ζ j)
    let Φ := fun w : (E × E) × (E × E) =&gt;
      (w.1.1 + h • P0 w.1 w.2 - ∑ j, c j • gradient f (Y1 w.1 w.2 j),
        a • (P0 w.1 w.2 - ∑ j, b j • gradient f (Y1 w.1 w.2 j)) + sigma • w.2.2)
    ∀ z z&#x27; ζ : E × E,
      Real.sqrt (phaseQuadraticForm κ ((Φ (z,ζ)).1-(Φ (z&#x27;,ζ)).1,
        (Φ (z,ζ)).2-(Φ (z&#x27;,ζ)).2)) ≤
      (1-h/(65536*κ))*Real.sqrt (phaseQuadraticForm κ (z.1-z&#x27;.1,z.2-z&#x27;.2)) := by
  classical
  dsimp only
  let t := fun i : Fin J =&gt; h/2*(1-Real.cos ((i : ℝ)/(J-1 : ℝ)*Real.pi))
  let ell := fun j : Fin J =&gt; Lagrange.basis Finset.univ t j
  let Λ := sSup ((fun s : ℝ =&gt; ∑ j : Fin J, |(ell j).eval s|) &#x27;&#x27; Icc 0 h)
  let ω := fun i j : Fin J =&gt; ∫ s in 0..t i, (t i-s)*(ell j).eval s
  let b := fun j : Fin J =&gt; ∫ s in 0..h, (ell j).eval s
  let c := fun j : Fin J =&gt; ∫ s in 0..h, (h-s)*(ell j).eval s
  intro hstep
  change h*Λ ≤ 1/(65536*κ) at hstep
  have hcoeff := TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.ChebyshevLobattoQuadrature.chebyshev_lobatto_coefficients hJ hh
  rcases hcoeff with ⟨_,_,_,_,_,hΛ,_,_⟩
  change 1 ≤ Λ at hΛ
  have hk : 0 &lt; κ := lt_of_lt_of_le zero_lt_one hκ
  have hi : 1/(65536*κ) ≤ (1:ℝ) := (div_le_one (by positivity)).mpr (by linarith)
  have hh1 : h ≤ 1 := by nlinarith
  have hs : h^2*Λ ≤ 1 := by
    have hm := mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_left (hstep.trans hi) (le_of_lt hh)
    nlinarith
  let a := Real.exp (-h / 2)
  let sigma := Real.sqrt (1 - Real.exp (-h))
  let P0 := fun (z ζ : E × E) =&gt; a • z.2 + sigma • ζ.1
  let Y0 := fun (z ζ : E × E) j =&gt; z.1 + t j • P0 z ζ
  let Y1 := fun (z ζ : E × E) i =&gt; Y0 z ζ i - ∑ j, ω i j • gradient f (Y0 z ζ j)
  let Φ := fun w : (E × E) × (E × E) =&gt;
    (w.1.1 + h • P0 w.1 w.2 - ∑ j, c j • gradient f (Y1 w.1 w.2 j),
      a • (P0 w.1 w.2 - ∑ j, b j • gradient f (Y1 w.1 w.2 j)) + sigma • w.2.2)
  have hfirst := FirstOrderDifference.source_first_order_difference hκ hf hH hJ hh hs
  intro z z&#x27; ζ
  let H := h⁻¹ • ∑ j, b j •
    (∫ u in (0:ℝ)..1, InnerProductSpace.continuousLinearMapOfBilin
      (fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ f) (Y1 z&#x27; ζ j+u • (Y1 z ζ j-Y1 z&#x27; ζ j))))
  let d := WithLp.toLp 2 (z.1-z&#x27;.1,z.2-z&#x27;.2)
  let q := WithLp.toLp 2 ((Φ (z,ζ)).1-(Φ (z&#x27;,ζ)).1,(Φ (z,ζ)).2-(Φ (z&#x27;,ζ)).2)
  have hp := hfirst.2.2 z z&#x27; ζ
  change H.toLinearMap.IsSymmetric ∧ (∀ v, 1/(2*κ)*‖v‖^2 ≤ inner ℝ (H v) v ∧
    inner ℝ (H v) v ≤ ‖v‖^2) ∧ ‖H‖ ≤ 1 ∧
    ∃ R : WithLp 2 (E × E) →L[ℝ] WithLp 2 (E × E), ‖R‖ ≤ 8*Λ*h^2 ∧
      q = d+h • WithLp.toLp 2 (d.snd,-H d.fst-d.snd)+R d at hp
  rcases hp with ⟨hsym,hcurv,hHnorm,R,hR,heq⟩
  have hr : ‖R d‖ ≤ 8*Λ*h^2*‖d‖ :=
    (R.le_opNorm d).trans (mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_right hR (norm_nonneg d))
  have hquad := endpoint_quadratic_contraction hκ hh hΛ hstep H hsym hcurv hHnorm d (R d) hr
  dsimp only at hquad
  rw [← heq] at hquad
  let Qd := phaseQuadraticForm κ (WithLp.ofLp d)
  let Qq := phaseQuadraticForm κ (WithLp.ofLp q)
  let t := h/(65536*κ)
  change Qq ≤ (1-h/(16*κ))*Qd at hquad
  change Real.sqrt Qq ≤ (1-t)*Real.sqrt Qd
  have hQd0 : 0 ≤ Qd := by
    have hl := ordinary_energy_le_phaseQuadraticForm hκ (WithLp.ofLp d)
    change _ ≤ 6*Qd at hl
    nlinarith [sq_nonneg ‖d.fst‖,sq_nonneg ‖d.snd‖]
  have hQq0 : 0 ≤ Qq := by
    have hl := ordinary_energy_le_phaseQuadraticForm hκ (WithLp.ofLp q)
    change _ ≤ 6*Qq at hl
    nlinarith [sq_nonneg ‖q.fst‖,sq_nonneg ‖q.snd‖]
  have ht0 : 0 ≤ t := by dsimp [t]; positivity
  have ht1 : t ≤ 1 := by
    have hc : h/(65536*κ) ≤ 1 := (div_le_one (by positivity)).mpr (by linarith)
    exact hc
  have hratio : h/(16*κ) = 4096*t := by dsimp only [t];field_simp;ring
  have hquad&#x27; : Qq ≤ (1-t)^2*Qd := by
    rw [hratio] at hquad
    nlinarith [mul_nonneg (sq_nonneg t) hQd0, mul_nonneg ht0 hQd0]
  have hsq : (Real.sqrt Qq)^2 ≤ ((1-t)*Real.sqrt Qd)^2 := by
    simpa only [mul_pow,Real.sq_sqrt hQd0,Real.sq_sqrt hQq0] using hquad&#x27;
  exact (sq_le_sq₀ (Real.sqrt_nonneg _) (mul_nonneg (sub_nonneg.mpr ht1) (Real.sqrt_nonneg _))).mp hsq

end
end AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.ActualContraction</code></pre><p><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-examplecases-smoothedpicardhmc-actualcontraction.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details></section><section data-source-comparison="AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.ActualContraction.source_synchronous_contraction"><h3>Source assumptions versus formal assumptions</h3><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Actual Lean</th><th>Difference kind</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Finite Euclidean source domain with the exact M_kappa block form.</td><td>E is a complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel sigma algebra, including dimension zero. f:E-&gt;R is genuinely globally C2, kappa&gt;=1, and (2kappa)^-1||v||²&lt;=D²f(z)[v,v]&lt;=||v||² for all z,v. This is the explicit normalized potential interface; actual V_eta construction is an independent producer.</td><td>same</td><td>Real inner-product presentation preserves the exact source form. Background comparisons need neither finite dimension nor measurable structure; nonlinear algorithm uses complete finite E and BorelSpace.</td></tr><tr><td>Universal c and C in the source Proposition4.7 step/contraction.</td><td>For every actual input pair and every common two-vector noise value, the source exact-gradient numerical phase obeys the M_kappa norm contraction with simultaneous explicit universal constant c=1/65536 under h Lambda_J&lt;=c/kappa. The actual averaged Hessian and remainder are supplied by the independently compiled genuine algorithm producer, never assumed as a contraction/phase certificate.</td><td>source-implicit</td><td>Explicit conservative c=1/65536 is proved using the actual C8 algorithm remainder and source drift. Q/API definitions and comparison proofs are unchanged, with no extra completed-paper credit.</td></tr><tr><td>Actual V_eta from Lemma4.1.</