i><li><strong>constant_dependencies</strong>: The factor 5 is an ASTIS numerical consequence of the q=2 moment chain, not a constant printed in Lemma 4.16. — Independent source review classification: derived-explicit-constant; nonblocking.</li><li><strong>domains</strong>: The formalization permits eta=0 and coordinate-free finite-dimensional real inner-product spaces beyond the paper&#x27;s positive-eta R^d algorithmic setting. — Independent source review classification: valid-endpoint-and-coordinate-generalization; nonblocking.</li><li><strong>assumptions</strong>: No minimizer is supplied: positive C2 Hessian curvature gives strong convexity, compactness yields a minimizer, and differentiability gives zero gradient. — Independent source review classification: internal-minimizer-discharged; nonblocking.</li></ul><p>A generalization is not a source correction. Proposed missing conditions require separate independent repair review. No proposed repair silently changes the original theorem.</p></section><details class="reader-ledger boundary-ledger"><summary>Scope and omitted-condition boundaries</summary><ul><li>This closes the minimizer-existence gap in the q=2 component of Lemma 4.16. The source&#x27;s q&gt;2 concentration, the Picard kernel, final accuracy and query complexity are still not proved.</li></ul></details><details class="proof-reader-provenance reader-ledger"><summary>Sources, dependencies, and reuse</summary><h2>Source and reuse</h2><h3>Samplinglib parents called</h3><ul><li><a href="../../../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-hessianstrongconvexity.html#decl-autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-hessianstrongconvexity-stron-258a330876"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.HessianStrongConvexity.strongConvexOn_univ_of_fderiv2_lower</code></a></li><li><a href="../../../theorems/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-strongconvexminimizer-exists-isminon-and-gradient-eq-zero.html"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.StrongConvexMinimizer.exists_isMinOn_and_gradient_eq_zero</code></a></li><li><a href="../../../theorems/autosamplingtheory-examplecases-smoothedpicardhmc-initialgibbsphasetransport-initial-gibbs-phase-transport-q2.html"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.InitialGibbsPhaseTransport.initial_gibbs_phase_transport_q2</code></a></li></ul><h3>Mathlib API called (external library)</h3><ul><li>ContDiff.differentiable</li></ul><h3>Mathematical sources</h3><ul><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.06906v1">Smoothed Picard Hamiltonian Monte Carlo, Lemma 4.16, PDF p.22</a> — The source selects a minimizer internally; ASTIS now constructs it from the printed curvature assumptions.</li></ul><p>Samplinglib prose is not a quotation or a source-equivalence certificate. Definitions and aliases are explained as constructions, not counted as new mathematical proofs.</p></details></article><h3>Which proof obligations and prerequisites are covered?</h3><ul><li><span class="status status-orange">Formalized proof edge; source adapter/review separate</span> Construct the minimizer chosen in the source proof</li><li><span class="status status-orange">Formalized proof edge; source adapter/review separate</span> Discharge the critical-point premise in the q=2 initialization bound</li><li><span class="status status-red">TODO — not closed by these contributions</span> Prove the q&gt;2 concentration component (remaining)</li></ul></section><section id="sphmc-initial-gibbs-q2" data-publication-item="sphmc-initial-gibbs-q2"><h2>SPHMC actual smoothed-Gibbs q=2 initialization component</h2><p>Fan Chen, Sinho Chewi, Jianfeng Lu and Matthew S. Zhang. ASTIS expands the conditional q=2 moment and transport calculation.</p><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.06906v1">arXiv:2609.06906v1 · Lemma 4.16 proof, PDF p.22</a> · faithful paraphrase</p><h3>Complete source statement (Samplinglib restatement)</h3><p>Lemma 4.16 gives an all-q initialization bound for the actual smoothed Gibbs phase law. Its proof selects a minimizer, bounds its distance from the admissible reference point, applies Gibbs concentration and Gaussian smoothing, and couples the momenta identically. This ASTIS component proves the numerical q=2 squared-cost bound conditional on a supplied critical point; existence and all-q concentration remain open.</p><ul><li>Normalized C2 Hessian bounds alpha I &lt;= Hessian U &lt;= I with alpha&gt;0.</li><li>Supplied critical point p and source small-gradient condition at xref.</li><li>0 &lt;= eta &lt;= 1, kappa=1/alpha, finite-dimensional real Borel space.</li></ul><h3>Gibbs and smoothing</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\pi(dx)=Z^{-1}e^{-U(x)}dx,\quad\pi_\eta=\pi*\mathsf N(0,\eta I),\quad\Pi_\eta=\pi_\eta\otimes\gamma.\]</div><h3>Numerical q=2 bound</h3><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[W_{2,M_\kappa}^{2}(\delta_{x_{\rm ref}}\otimes\gamma,\Pi_\eta)\le5\kappa d.\]</div><h2>Read the formalized proofs</h2><p>Each statement and proof below has its own closed Lean disclosure. Samplinglib parents, Mathlib calls and external mathematical sources are distinguished in each proof.</p><article class="proof-reader" data-authored-declaration="AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.InitialGibbsPhaseTransport.initial_gibbs_phase_transport_q2"><div class="eyebrow">Samplinglib mathematical lesson</div><h2>Numerical initialization cost for the actual smoothed Gibbs phase law</h2><p><code>AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.InitialGibbsPhaseTransport.initial_gibbs_phase_transport_q2</code> · theorem · <a href="../../../lessons/index.html">Teaching coverage</a></p><section class="reader-first-pass" aria-label="First reading"><div class="reader-first-pass-label">Read this first</div><h2>Mathematical statement</h2><p class="reader-claim">For the normalized Gibbs law of U, smooth the position by an independent Gaussian of variance eta and adjoin independent standard-Gaussian momentum. The squared transport cost from the initial Dirac-position/Gaussian-momentum law to this actual phase reference is at most five times kappa times dimension.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation reader-main-equation">\[W_{2,M_\kappa}^{2}(\delta_{x_{\rm ref}}\otimes\gamma,(\pi*\mathsf N(0,\eta I))\otimes\gamma)\le 5\kappa d,\qquad\kappa=1/\alpha.\]</div><h3>Proof roadmap</h3><ol class="proof-roadmap"><li><span>1</span><strong>Control the reference-point displacement</strong><p>Strong convexity compares the gradient at xref with its zero value at p. Cauchy-Schwarz and the source&#x27;s small-gradient condition then bound the distance to p.</p></li><li><span>2</span><strong>Integrate the Gibbs position moment</strong><p>The already proved normalized Gibbs moment about p is finite and at most d/alpha. The squared triangle inequality makes the moment about xref integrable and bounds it by 4d/alpha.</p></li><li><span>3</span><strong>Add Gaussian smoothing</strong><p>Independent centered Gaussian noise contributes exactly eta times dimension to the position second moment. This is an identity, not an unverified limit exchange.</p></li><li><span>4</span><strong>Couple the momenta identically</strong><p>The initial and reference phase laws share standard-Gaussian momentum. The compiled coupling estimate bounds their squared M-kappa cost by the smoothed position moment; eta &lt;= 1 &lt;= kappa yields 5 kappa d.</p></li></ol></section><details class="reader-ledger assumption-ledger"><summary>Full assumptions and notation</summary><h3>All objects and hypotheses</h3><ul><li>E is a finite-dimensional complete real inner-product Borel space.</li><li>The genuine C2 Hessian of U lies between alpha I and I, with 0 &lt; alpha &lt;= 1 and kappa = 1/alpha.</li><li>A point p satisfies gradient U(p) = 0; xref satisfies squared gradient norm at most alpha times dimension.</li><li>The Gaussian smoothing variance eta lies in [0,1].</li></ul><p class="muted"></p></details><h2>Mathematical proof</h2><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>1. Control the reference-point displacement</h4><p>Strong convexity compares the gradient at xref with its zero value at p. Cauchy-Schwarz and the source&#x27;s small-gradient condition then bound the distance to p.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\alpha\|x_{\rm ref}-p\|^2\le\langle\nabla U(x_{\rm ref}),x_{\rm ref}-p\rangle\le\|\nabla U(x_{\rm ref})\|\,\|x_{\rm ref}-p\|,\quad\|x_{\rm ref}-p\|^2\le d/\alpha.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>HessianStrongConvexity and StrongConvexFirstOrder supply genuine strong monotonicity; positivity permits cancellation.</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>2. Integrate the Gibbs position moment</h4><p>The already proved normalized Gibbs moment about p is finite and at most d/alpha. The squared triangle inequality makes the moment about xref integrable and bounds it by 4d/alpha.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\mathbb E_\pi\|X-x_{\rm ref}\|^2\le 2\mathbb E_\pi\|X-p\|^2+2\|x_{\rm ref}-p\|^2\le4d/\alpha.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>GibbsPositionMoment.gibbs_position_moment provides the probability law, integrability and sharp center moment; integral_mono applies only after the shifted moment is shown integrable.</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>3. Add Gaussian smoothing</h4><p>Independent centered Gaussian noise contributes exactly eta times dimension to the position second moment. This is an identity, not an unverified limit exchange.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\mathbb E\|X+\sqrt\eta G-x_{\rm ref}\|^2=\mathbb E\|X-x_{\rm ref}\|^2+\eta d\le(4\kappa+\eta)d.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>PhaseReferenceMoment.gaussianSmoothing_position_second_moment proves product measurability, cross-term cancellation and integrability.</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h4>4. Couple the momenta identically</h4><p>The initial and reference phase laws share standard-Gaussian momentum. The compiled coupling estimate bounds their squared M-kappa cost by the smoothed position moment; eta &lt;= 1 &lt;= kappa yields 5 kappa d.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[W_{2,M_\kappa}^{2}(\mu_0,\Pi_\eta)\le(4\kappa+\eta)d\le5\kappa d.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>InitialPhaseTransport.phaseWassersteinSq_initial_le_position_moment supplies the actual coupling and its cost.</p></details></div><section class="lean-verification"><h2>Lean verification</h2><p>The exact proposition and proof begin folded so the mathematical argument remains the primary reading path.</p><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-statement" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.InitialGibbsPhaseTransport.initial_gibbs_phase_transport_q2"><summary>Lean statement · initial_gibbs_phase_transport_q2</summary><p>The Lean conclusion is a squared extended-nonnegative Kantorovich value, not yet a full q&gt;=2 Wasserstein bound. A supplied critical point is a proof-interface assumption that still needs an existence theorem for source-level closure.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem initial_gibbs_phase_transport_q2
